const e={content_version:"2026-08-09",disclaimer:"Vendor AI features change frequently. Always confirm the actual configuration with the system owner — this catalogue suggests what to ask about, it does not establish what is enabled."},t=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"microsoft-365-copilot","name":"Microsoft 365 / Copilot","vendor":"Microsoft","category":"Productivity Suite","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":5,"aliases":["m365","office 365","microsoft office","copilot","m365 copilot","microsoft copilot","teams","outlook","sharepoint"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Microsoft 365 Copilot Chat","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Web-grounded Copilot Chat is included at no extra cost with eligible Microsoft 365 business and enterprise subscriptions. It is therefore present in most tenants WITHOUT anyone having bought an AI product. This is the single most commonly missed 'already active' AI system in a mid-market estate.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Microsoft 365 Copilot (full add-on, in-app)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","summarization","content_generation","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The full per-user add-on (~USD 30/user/month) grounds Copilot in tenant data across Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook and Teams. Requires deliberate purchase and assignment. Bundled into the Microsoft 365 E7 'Frontier' suite from May 2026.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Teams meeting recap and intelligent summaries","capabilities":["summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"AI-generated meeting notes and speaker-attributed recaps require a Copilot or Teams Premium licence. Basic transcription alone is not an AI feature in the Act's sense.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Exchange Online Protection spam and phishing filtering","capabilities":["spam_filter"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Always on. Minimal risk; recorded for inventory completeness only.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Base productivity use carries no Annex III domain. The employment domain applies if Copilot is used inside Viva Insights/Glint for workforce analytics, or if a Copilot Studio agent is built to screen candidates or evaluate employee performance.","docs_url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/microsoft-365-copilot-licensing","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"dynamics-365-sales","name":"Dynamics 365 Sales","vendor":"Microsoft","category":"CRM","walkthrough_category":"crm_sales","prevalence":3,"aliases":["d365 sales","dynamics crm","crm","dynamics 365 crm"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Copilot in Dynamics 365 Sales","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","summarization","content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Record summarisation, meeting prep, email drafting and a natural-language chat pane. Basic Copilot features are bundled with the Dynamics 365 Sales licence; deeper generative features draw on Copilot Credits or the Microsoft 365 Copilot add-on. Microsoft is migrating this experience to the Sales agent in Microsoft 365 Copilot.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Predictive lead and opportunity scoring","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Sales Insights scoring models rank leads and contacts. Requires deliberate enablement and model training. Profiles named individuals at customer organisations.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Autonomous sales agents","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Sales Qualification Agent and similar autonomous agents are separately metered via Copilot Credits.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"No Annex III domain in normal B2B sales use. Escalate if scoring is used to decide access to a service or credit terms for individual consumers (essential_services).","docs_url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dynamics365/sales/copilot-overview","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"dynamics-365-customer-service","name":"Dynamics 365 Customer Service","vendor":"Microsoft","category":"Service Desk / ITSM","walkthrough_category":"service_desk","prevalence":3,"aliases":["d365 customer service","c-help","customer service workspace","omnichannel"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Copilot case and conversation summaries","capabilities":["summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Generates case summaries and conversation recaps for agents. Frequently the FIRST AI feature switched on in a Microsoft estate because it is low-friction and visibly useful. Basic Copilot features ship with the app licence; generative drafts and knowledge chat need the Copilot add-on or Copilot Credits.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Copilot draft response and knowledge search","capabilities":["content_generation","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Suggests replies to customers and answers agent questions from knowledge articles.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Customer-facing service agent (autonomous)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Autonomous service agent that resolves customer cases end to end. Interacts directly with natural persons — Art. 50(1) transparency applies as soon as it is enabled.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"No Annex III domain when serving business customers. If the same instance is used as an HR service desk handling employee cases, the employment domain applies and the summarisation/decision features become High Risk candidates. If used for emergency call triage or benefit eligibility, essential_services applies.","docs_url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dynamics365/release-plan/2026wave1/service/dynamics365-customer-service/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"dynamics-365-finance-operations","name":"Dynamics 365 Finance & Operations","vendor":"Microsoft","category":"ERP","walkthrough_category":"erp_finance","prevalence":3,"aliases":["d365 fno","fno","f&o","dynamics ax","dynamics 365 finance","supply chain management"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Copilot sidecar (generative help and guidance)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Conversational pane answering questions about app functionality and data. Must be explicitly enabled by an administrator; built on Copilot Studio orchestration.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Embedded Copilot capabilities and default AI UI elements","capabilities":["ui_component_generic"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"AI-branded UI elements appear in the interface in recent releases even where the underlying capability is not configured. Confirm with the system owner whether any of these actually perform a function — an unconfirmed AI-branded button is ui_component_generic, not a capability.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Finance insights (cash flow and payment prediction)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Customer payment prediction scores the likelihood a customer pays on time. Requires separate enablement and Dataverse capacity.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"No Annex III domain in normal finance/supply-chain use. Escalate to essential_services if payment prediction is used to decide whether an individual consumer gets credit terms.","docs_url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dynamics365/fin-ops-core/fin-ops/copilot/copilot-for-finance-operations","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"dynamics-365-project-operations","name":"Dynamics 365 Project Operations","vendor":"Microsoft","category":"ERP / Project Management","walkthrough_category":"erp_finance","prevalence":2,"aliases":["d365 po","project operations","po"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Copilot for project (task plans, risk, status reports)","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Generates project task plans from a natural-language description, suggests risks and mitigations, and drafts status reports. Enabled via Settings > Parameters > Feature Control > Enable Copilot in Project Operations, so it is off until someone turns it on.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Copilot search across project data","capabilities":["natural_language_query"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Natural-language search depends on Dataverse/CDS capacity, which is a common cost-driven reason organisations remove or never enable it.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Resource assignment features that allocate named people to project tasks based on skills or past performance touch Annex III point 4 (task allocation, evaluation of workers). Ask whether Copilot influences who gets staffed on what.","docs_url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dynamics365/project-operations/project-management/copilot-features","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"power-bi","name":"Power BI / Microsoft Fabric","vendor":"Microsoft","category":"Business Intelligence / Analytics","walkthrough_category":"analytics","prevalence":5,"aliases":["powerbi","power bi","fabric","microsoft fabric","dw model"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Copilot in Power BI","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization","content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"IMPORTANT: Copilot is enabled BY DEFAULT for tenants holding a paid Fabric capacity of F2 or higher (or Power BI Premium P1+). Many organisations therefore already have it live without a deliberate decision. A Power BI Pro or PPU licence alone is not sufficient. Outside the EU data boundary it is disabled by default.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Q&A natural-language visual","capabilities":["natural_language_query"],"default_state":"active","notes":"The classic Q&A visual is a long-standing Power BI feature available without Fabric capacity. Not generative, but it is natural-language querying.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI visuals (key influencers, decomposition tree, anomaly detection)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Key influencers performs automated driver analysis. Where the analysed rows are people (employees, customers), this is profiling.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"This is a conditional case that turns entirely on WHAT IS IN THE MODEL. A Power BI model over sales data carries no Annex III domain. The same Copilot over an HR data model — headcount, attrition, performance ratings — puts natural-language querying and key-influencer profiling into the employment domain. Always ask what subject areas the semantic model covers.","docs_url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/create-reports/copilot-introduction","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"power-platform","name":"Power Platform / Copilot Studio","vendor":"Microsoft","category":"Low-Code Platform / Automation","walkthrough_category":"dev_tools","prevalence":4,"aliases":["power apps","power automate","copilot studio","power virtual agents","pva","powerapps"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Copilot Studio custom agents","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","automated_decision_making","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Low-code studio for building agents that reason, call tools and act. CRITICAL FOR DISCOVERY: what the agent DOES is defined by the customer, not the vendor — so the organisation is likely acting as PROVIDER, not merely deployer, for anything built here. Agents can be created by citizen developers without central IT visibility; ask for the tenant agent inventory.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI Builder (form processing, prediction, classification)","capabilities":["document_analysis","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Prebuilt and custom models for document extraction, sentiment and category prediction. Consumes AI Builder credits.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Copilot in Power Automate / Power Apps authoring","capabilities":["content_generation","basic_automation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Natural-language flow and app authoring is on by default in most tenants. Affects the maker, not data subjects — low concern by itself.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"The highest-variance entry in this catalogue. A custom agent built to screen CVs, allocate shifts or evaluate employees puts the organisation in the employment domain AS A PROVIDER, which carries substantially heavier obligations than deployer status. Any agent making decisions about individuals' access to services falls under essential_services. Treat every Copilot Studio agent as a separate system requiring its own assessment.","docs_url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/fundamentals-what-is-copilot-studio","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"servicenow","name":"ServiceNow","vendor":"ServiceNow","category":"ITSM / Service Management","walkthrough_category":"service_desk","prevalence":4,"aliases":["service now","snow","now platform"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Now Assist","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","summarization","content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"LICENSING CHANGED IN 2026 — verify which model the customer is on. Under the legacy structure Now Assist required a Pro Plus or Enterprise Plus SKU and was commonly not licensed. From 1 July 2026 ServiceNow retired Standard/Professional/Enterprise in favour of Foundation, Advanced and Prime, and Now Assist is bundled into ALL THREE. Customers migrating to the new tiers acquire the entitlement whether or not they asked for it, but capabilities still require explicit activation per application.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Predictive Intelligence","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Machine-learning categorisation, assignment and similarity matching for incidents and cases. Long-standing platform capability, typically requires deliberate model training.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI Agents / Moveworks-based agentic experience","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Autonomous agents intended in the Prime tier to replace level-1 service desk roles. Interacts directly with employees — Art. 50(1) transparency applies.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Virtual Agent (rule-based)","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"The pre-generative conversational interface is topic/rule driven rather than generative. Distinguish it from Now Assist when the system owner says 'we have a chatbot'.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"ITSM use carries no Annex III domain. HIGH RISK CONDITIONAL: ServiceNow HR Service Delivery handles employee cases — onboarding, leave, grievances, performance queries — which places Now Assist summarisation and any agentic decision-making squarely in the employment domain. Ask specifically whether the HRSD module is in scope, because the same instance can be Minimal Risk for IT and High Risk for HR.","docs_url":"https://www.servicenow.com/products/now-assist.html","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"salesforce","name":"Salesforce (Sales Cloud / Service Cloud)","vendor":"Salesforce","category":"CRM","walkthrough_category":"crm_sales","prevalence":4,"aliases":["sfdc","sales cloud","service cloud","einstein","agentforce","salesforce crm"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Sales Cloud Einstein (scoring and insights)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Lead scoring, opportunity scoring and account insights are included in Sales Cloud (Enterprise edition and above) rather than sold separately, so the entitlement often exists unnoticed. Requires setup and model training to actually produce scores.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Einstein Conversation Insights","capabilities":["summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Transcribes and analyses sales calls, surfacing keywords, competitor mentions and rep talk-time.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Einstein generative AI (email and reply drafting, summaries)","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Generative features rely on the Einstein Trust Layer and are gated by edition and Data Cloud provisioning.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Agentforce autonomous agents","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Priced per conversation or via Agentforce editions, and requires Data Cloud as a prerequisite — a substantial separate purchase. Customer-facing agents interact directly with natural persons.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Standard CRM use carries no Annex III domain. Escalate where Agentforce or Einstein decides individual consumers' eligibility, pricing or access to a service (essential_services). Note that Einstein Conversation Insights analysing a named rep's own calls is worker monitoring — employment domain — if managers use it to evaluate performance.","docs_url":"https://www.salesforce.com/agentforce/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"sap-s4hana","name":"SAP S/4HANA","vendor":"SAP","category":"ERP","walkthrough_category":"erp_finance","prevalence":4,"aliases":["s4","s/4","s4hana","sap erp","sap ecc"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Joule (AI copilot)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"SAP's cross-portfolio copilot, natively integrated and grounded in SAP business data. Entitlement depends on the edition and on SAP AI Units; requires activation and configuration rather than arriving switched on.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Joule Agents (finance and supply chain)","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Agentic capabilities such as dispute resolution and cash application. Consumption-based via AI Units.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Embedded predictive analytics","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Predictive accounting, demand forecasting and similar embedded models. These operate on transactions and materials rather than persons in most configurations.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Core ERP finance and logistics carry no Annex III domain. Escalate if credit-management scoring is applied to individual consumers (essential_services), or if SAP is the system of record driving safety functions in utilities or transport (critical_infrastructure).","docs_url":"https://www.sap.com/products/artificial-intelligence/ai-assistant.html","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"sap-successfactors","name":"SAP SuccessFactors","vendor":"SAP","category":"HR Management / HCM","walkthrough_category":"employment","prevalence":3,"aliases":["successfactors","sf hcm","sap hcm","employee central"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Joule for HR","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Conversational assistant across Employee Central quick actions, answering employee and manager questions about personal and job information. Requires explicit enablement of both Joule and each individual AI feature, with permission-based controls for sensitive HR contexts.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"AI-assisted job description and goal writing","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Generative drafting of job requisitions, competencies and performance goals. Job advertisement targeting is explicitly named in Annex III point 4.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"AI-assisted successor and talent recommendations","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Analyses skills, competencies and work experience to recommend successors for critical roles. This directly influences promotion decisions.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Performance & Goals and Career Development agents","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"On the 2026 roadmap alongside People Intelligence, HR Service and Payroll agents. Confirm availability before recording as present.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"HIGH RISK CONDITIONAL. Every AI feature here operates in the employment domain (Annex III point 4). Successor recommendation and performance agents affect promotion and evaluation of workers and are High Risk the moment they are switched on, regardless of a human-in-the-loop claim. Ask which specific AI features have been enabled in Admin Center rather than accepting 'we have Joule' as an answer.","docs_url":"https://www.sap.com/products/hcm/ai-joule-product-tour.html","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"workday","name":"Workday","vendor":"Workday","category":"HR Management / HCM","walkthrough_category":"employment","prevalence":3,"aliases":["workday hcm","workday illuminate","workday sana"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Workday AI platform (Illuminate / Sana)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Workday's native AI layer, now marketed under the Sana brand following the acquisition, having previously been positioned as Illuminate. Confirm which naming the customer's tenant uses — both refer to the same underlying AI layer.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Skills inference and candidate-to-job matching","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Infers employee skills from profile and work history and matches candidates to requisitions. Directly in scope of recruitment and selection under Annex III point 4.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Recruiter Agent and Contingent Sourcing Agent","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Agents that identify talent and screen applicants to improve applicant quality. Rolling out through 2026; some are demo-stage. Verify production availability before recording.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Talent Mobility / Succession Agent and career path suggestions","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Recommends internal moves, learning and career paths for named employees.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"AI-assisted job description generation","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Drafts requisition text.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"HIGH RISK CONDITIONAL, and one of the strongest cases in this catalogue. Candidate matching and the Recruiter Agent perform recruitment and selection; the Talent Mobility and Succession agents influence promotion. Both are named activities in Annex III point 4. Where Workday also runs payroll and time tracking, worker monitoring may add a second employment trigger.","docs_url":"https://www.workday.com/en-us/artificial-intelligence.html","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"bamboohr","name":"BambooHR","vendor":"BambooHR","category":"HR Management","walkthrough_category":"employment","prevalence":2,"aliases":["bamboo hr","bamboo"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Ask BambooHR (AI assistant)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Chat interface answering employee HR questions from HRIS records, org chart, benefits, policies and handbooks. On the Core plan it handles routine questions such as PTO balances; on Pro it also reads company policy documents. Available on desktop and mobile.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Bamboo AI (connected intelligence platform and agents)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Launched July 2026 as an embedded system of AI agents spanning HR reporting and payroll workflows. NEWER THAN MANY EXISTING ASSESSMENTS — an inventory captured before mid-2026 will not reflect it, so re-confirm with the system owner.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"eNPS AI topic summaries","capabilities":["summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Parses free-text responses in employee engagement surveys to extract themes and topics. Analysis of employee sentiment at aggregate level.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Payroll anomaly analysis","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Compares payroll period over period to surface differences. Detection of numeric anomalies rather than assessment of persons.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"HIGH RISK CONDITIONAL and the canonical example for this catalogue. BambooHR is Minimal Risk while no AI is enabled, but every feature above sits in the employment domain, so switching on Ask BambooHR or Bamboo AI moves the system upward immediately. Watch particularly for eNPS topic summarisation drifting toward inference about individual employees' attitudes.","docs_url":"https://www.bamboohr.com/platform/hr-data-and-reporting/ask-bamboohr","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"personio","name":"Personio","vendor":"Personio","category":"HR Management","walkthrough_category":"employment","prevalence":3,"aliases":["personio hr"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Personio Assistant","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Answers questions in plain language about people, time, compensation and recruiting data, produces visualisations and drafts summary emails. Also handles routine employee self-service questions on policies and time off. Data is hosted in the EU and is not used to train models — relevant to the DPIA, not to classification.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"AI-assisted recruiting (candidate matching)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"unknown","notes":"Personio acquired AI recruiting startup aurio in 2026 and is integrating candidate-matching capability. The extent of what is generally available to customers is not clearly documented — confirm directly with Personio or the system owner rather than assuming presence.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"AI features in Performance and Surveys","capabilities":["summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"AI enhancements announced across Core HR, Payroll, Performance, Surveys and analytics. Feature-by-feature availability varies by plan.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"HIGH RISK CONDITIONAL. Personio Assistant querying compensation and turnover data by employee is workforce analytics in the employment domain. Anything from the aurio recruiting acquisition is recruitment and selection — Annex III point 4 — and should be treated as High Risk on activation.","docs_url":"https://www.personio.com/product/assistant/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"hibob","name":"HiBob (Bob)","vendor":"HiBob","category":"HR Management","walkthrough_category":"employment","prevalence":2,"aliases":["hibob","hi bob","bob","bob hr","hibong"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Bob Companion","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"The central AI interface in Bob, using workforce data to deliver answers, insights and suggested next steps in the flow of work. HiBob states it respects existing permission layers and requires a human to approve actions affecting company data.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Bob AI insights and recommendations","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Surfaces workforce insights and recommendations to HR and managers.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"AI Agents and Bob Studio","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"On the Q3 2026 roadmap alongside MCP support, for building custom AI-powered workflows. Note that custom workflows built in Bob Studio may make the customer a PROVIDER rather than a deployer.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"HIGH RISK CONDITIONAL. All Bob AI operates on workforce data in the employment domain. HiBob's human-in-the-loop design and permission inheritance are useful mitigations but do NOT remove the Annex III classification — Art. 14 human oversight is a requirement for High Risk systems, not an exemption from being one.","docs_url":"https://www.hibob.com/platform/core/ai/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"oracle-fusion-hcm","name":"Oracle Fusion Cloud HCM","vendor":"Oracle","category":"HR Management / HCM","walkthrough_category":"employment","prevalence":2,"aliases":["oracle hcm","fusion hcm","oracle fusion","oracle cloud hcm"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Oracle AI Agent Studio for Fusion Applications","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","automated_decision_making","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Platform for building, connecting and running agentic applications, included with Fusion Applications rather than sold separately. Agents built here can access workflows, policies and approval hierarchies and execute decisions. Customer-authored agents likely make the organisation a PROVIDER.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Fusion Agentic Applications for HR","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Oracle reports over 1,000 agents delivered through Fusion Applications with 22 agentic applications live in production across finance, supply chain, HR and sales operations. Confirm which are activated in the customer's pod.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Embedded generative AI (job descriptions, summaries)","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Generative drafting embedded across HCM flows including requisitions and performance feedback.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"HIGH RISK CONDITIONAL. Oracle's agents can make and execute decisions within business processes, which is exactly the automated_decision_making escalation trigger. In an HCM context that lands in the employment domain. Ask specifically which agents are enabled and whether any act without human approval.","docs_url":"https://www.oracle.com/applications/fusion-ai/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"visma","name":"Visma (HRM & Payroll)","vendor":"Visma","category":"HR Management / Payroll","walkthrough_category":"employment","prevalence":3,"aliases":["visma","visma.net","visma hrm","visma lon","datalon","visma enterprise"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Document interpretation and automated bookkeeping suggestions","capabilities":["document_analysis","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"OCR with automatic field extraction, account suggestion and bookkeeping assistance. Operates on invoices and documents rather than assessments of persons.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Payroll cycle AI assistant","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Automates payroll cycle steps and compliance checks. Rolled out selectively per market and per release.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Employee-facing chatbots","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Chatbots supporting employee requests for logistical matters. Generative AI pilots are rolled out selectively within parts of the suite.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"Visma deliberately releases AI incrementally per product and market rather than as a platform-wide switch, so a customer's exposure depends heavily on which Visma modules and which country version they run. Payroll administration alone is generally not Annex III point 4 — the trigger is AI affecting recruitment, evaluation, promotion, termination or task allocation. Establish which specific Visma products are in the estate before concluding.","docs_url":"https://www.visma.com/company/technology/ai","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"unit4","name":"Unit4 ERPx","vendor":"Unit4","category":"ERP","walkthrough_category":"erp_finance","prevalence":2,"aliases":["unit4","erpx","unit4 erp","agresso"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Ava (Advanced Virtual Agent)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Successor to the Wanda chatbot, surfaced inside ERPx and Microsoft Teams. Interprets natural-language instructions, gives personalised recommendations and orchestrates actions such as approving purchase orders and invoices, reviewing timesheets and managing workflow approvals.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI for Your World trial programme","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"From July 2026 Unit4 offers ERPx customers commitment-free access to its AI tools including Ava, with sign-up open until 31 December 2026 and use until 31 August 2027. WATCH THIS: a no-cost trial is exactly how AI gets switched on without a procurement decision or an AI Act assessment.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Core ERP finance use carries no Annex III domain. Unit4 is strong in people-centric and public-sector organisations, so check whether the HCM module is in use — Ava reviewing timesheets shades toward worker monitoring, and any AI influencing assessment or task allocation puts it in the employment domain.","docs_url":"https://www.unit4.com/products/platform-technology/ai-innovation","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"ifs","name":"IFS Cloud","vendor":"IFS","category":"ERP / EAM / FSM","walkthrough_category":"erp_finance","prevalence":2,"aliases":["ifs","ifs applications","ifs.ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"IFS.ai Copilot","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Context-aware assistant embedded in IFS Cloud, offering page-specific prompts and conversational interaction with data across CRM, Supply Chain, Finance, HCM, Projects, Manufacturing, Service and Asset Management. Available from IFS Cloud 25R1 and on mobile.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"IFS.ai embedded recommendations and scheduling optimisation","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Context-aware recommendations supporting dispatchers, service managers and field technicians, including optimised scheduling of field work.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Two conditional paths matter here. First, IFS field service scheduling ALLOCATES WORK TO NAMED TECHNICIANS and can surface their performance — that is task allocation and worker monitoring under Annex III point 4, so the employment domain applies. Second, IFS is heavily used in utilities, energy and asset-intensive industries; where an AI component acts as a safety component in managing electricity, gas, water or heating supply, critical_infrastructure applies.","docs_url":"https://www.ifs.com/ai/architecture","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"infor","name":"Infor CloudSuite","vendor":"Infor","category":"ERP","walkthrough_category":"erp_finance","prevalence":2,"aliases":["infor","cloudsuite","infor ln","syteline","infor m3"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Infor GenAI Assistant","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Conversational assistant embedded in Infor CloudSuites that writes, summarises and analyses within business processes. Infor describes it as in LIMITED AVAILABILITY, so most customers will not have it — do not assume presence.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Infor Industry AI Agents","capabilities":["basic_automation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The April 2026 release expanded the agent library beyond 100 agents across ERP, analytics, integration, process mining and operations, with an Agentic Orchestrator. Typically a separate commercial conversation.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Manufacturing and distribution use carries no Annex III domain. Check whether Infor HCM modules are in scope, and whether any agent performs workforce scheduling that allocates tasks to named workers (employment).","docs_url":"https://www.infor.com/platform/enterprise-ai","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"atlassian-jira","name":"Jira / Jira Service Management","vendor":"Atlassian","category":"Project / Issue Tracking / ITSM","walkthrough_category":"dev_tools","prevalence":4,"aliases":["jira","jsm","jira service desk","jira software"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Atlassian Intelligence","capabilities":["summarization","content_generation","natural_language_query","basic_automation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"IMPORTANT DEFAULT: since 2026 Atlassian Intelligence is included automatically on Standard, Premium and Enterprise cloud plans — drafting, summarisation, natural-language JQL and AI-generated automation rules. It is NOT an add-on line item. Only the Free tier excludes it. Admins can disable it, so confirm whether they have.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Rovo (Search, Chat and Agents)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Also bundled into every paid cloud subscription, metered by monthly credits per seat (25 on Standard, 70 on Premium, 150 on Enterprise). Rovo Agents can be customer-authored, which raises the provider question.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Virtual service agent (JSM)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Deflects and resolves service requests conversationally. Interacts directly with employees or customers — Art. 50(1) transparency applies.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"This is the most commonly UNDER-reported active AI in a mid-market estate: because Atlassian bundled it rather than selling it, system owners routinely answer 'we don't have AI in Jira' when it is switched on by default. Always verify against the admin settings rather than the owner's recollection. Employment domain applies if JSM is used for HR service requests, or if Rovo Agents are used to evaluate team or individual performance.","docs_url":"https://www.atlassian.com/software/rovo","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"atlassian-confluence","name":"Confluence","vendor":"Atlassian","category":"Knowledge Management / Wiki","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":4,"aliases":["confluence","atlassian confluence","wiki"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Atlassian Intelligence in Confluence","capabilities":["summarization","content_generation","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Page summarisation, AI writing assistance and definition lookup, included on all paid cloud plans rather than sold as an add-on. Same bundling caveat as Jira.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Rovo Search and Chat over Confluence content","capabilities":["natural_language_query","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Enterprise search and conversational retrieval across connected content, credit-metered by plan tier.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Knowledge management carries no Annex III domain by itself. Consider whether the Confluence corpus contains HR policy or employee records that Rovo can surface to the wrong audience — that is a GDPR and access-control concern rather than an AI Act classification.","docs_url":"https://www.atlassian.com/software/rovo","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"slack","name":"Slack","vendor":"Salesforce","category":"Collaboration / Messaging","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":4,"aliases":["slack ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Thread and channel summarisation, huddle notes","capabilities":["summarization"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Basic AI features including thread summaries and huddle notes are included on all paid Slack plans from Pro upward. Slack AI is bundled into plans and is no longer sold as a separate add-on. The Free plan excludes AI.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI-powered enterprise search and channel recaps","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Advanced AI search, the 'Today' briefing and workflow AI actions come with Business+ and Enterprise+ plans.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Slackbot personal assistant","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Conversational assistant within the workspace.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Ordinary team messaging carries no Annex III domain. The employment concern is worker monitoring: if AI summarisation or analytics over employee conversations is used by managers to evaluate individual behaviour or performance, Annex III point 4 applies. Ask what analytics management actually consumes.","docs_url":"https://slack.com/features/ai","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"zoom","name":"Zoom","vendor":"Zoom","category":"Video Conferencing / UCaaS","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":4,"aliases":["zoom meetings","zoom ai companion","ai companion"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Zoom AI Companion","capabilities":["summarization","content_generation","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"active","notes":"IMPORTANT DEFAULT: AI Companion is included at NO ADDITIONAL COST with all paid Zoom accounts from Pro upward — meeting summaries, next-step detection and chat compose. Because there is no separate SKU, organisations frequently have it without a purchase decision. Excluded only on the free Basic plan. Account owners can disable it.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI Companion 3.0 agentic workflows and cross-app search","capabilities":["natural_language_query","basic_automation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Adds agentic workflows, a web interface and search across connected sources such as Google Drive and OneDrive for paid users.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Meetings carry no Annex III domain by default. Two watch items. First, if AI meeting summaries of employee one-to-ones or appraisals feed performance management, the employment domain applies. Second, if any Zoom analytics is configured to infer participant engagement, attentiveness or sentiment, that is emotion recognition in a workplace setting — PROHIBITED under Art. 5(1)(f), not merely High Risk. Ask explicitly about engagement or sentiment scoring.","docs_url":"https://news.zoom.com/zoom-ai-companion/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"google-workspace","name":"Google Workspace / Gemini","vendor":"Google","category":"Productivity Suite","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":3,"aliases":["gsuite","g suite","google apps","gemini","google gemini","gmail","google docs"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Gemini in Gmail, Docs, Slides, Sheets, Meet and Chat","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"active","notes":"IMPORTANT DEFAULT: since January 2025 Google discontinued the separate Gemini Business and Gemini Enterprise add-ons and bundled Gemini into all paid Workspace plans, raising base prices instead. Any organisation on Business Standard or above has Gemini available without a separate AI purchase.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Gemini app and NotebookLM for Workspace","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Standalone conversational assistant included with paid Workspace plans.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Gmail spam and phishing filtering","capabilities":["spam_filter"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Always on. Minimal risk; recorded for inventory completeness.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"No Annex III domain in general productivity use. Employment domain applies if Gemini is used to draft or screen recruitment materials, or if Workspace admin analytics is used to evaluate individual employee activity.","docs_url":"https://workspace.google.com/blog/product-announcements/empowering-businesses-with-AI","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"hubspot","name":"HubSpot","vendor":"HubSpot","category":"CRM / Marketing Automation","walkthrough_category":"crm_sales","prevalence":4,"aliases":["hubspot crm","breeze","hubspot breeze","agent hub"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Breeze AI / Agent Hub","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"active","notes":"HubSpot's AI layer, now surfaced as Agent Hub, is bundled with credit allowances rather than sold as a separate product: 500 credits on Starter, 3,000 on Professional, 5,000 on Enterprise, based on the customer's highest subscription tier. Credits are therefore present by default on any paid portal.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Customer Agent","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Autonomous support agent, priced outcome-based since April 2026 at roughly 50 credits per resolved conversation. Interacts directly with natural persons — Art. 50(1) transparency applies.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Prospecting Agent and lead scoring","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Researches and prioritises prospects and scores leads. Profiles named individuals at target organisations.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Data Agent","capabilities":["natural_language_query","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Enriches and maintains CRM records.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Sales and marketing use carries no Annex III domain. If HubSpot is used to run a careers site or process job applicants, the recruiting use puts it into the employment domain — an unusual but real configuration in smaller organisations.","docs_url":"https://www.hubspot.com/products/artificial-intelligence","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"zendesk","name":"Zendesk","vendor":"Zendesk","category":"Customer Service / Helpdesk","walkthrough_category":"service_desk","prevalence":3,"aliases":["zendesk support","zendesk suite"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Zendesk AI agents (automated resolutions)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Requires the Advanced AI add-on and is billed per automated resolution. Without the add-on the customer has a rule-based flow builder, which is basic_automation rather than an AI agent — a distinction worth making explicitly during discovery.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Copilot (agent assist)","capabilities":["summarization","content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Agent-facing assistance priced around USD 50 per agent per month on top of seats.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI triage and intent detection","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Intelligent triage classifies incoming tickets by intent, language and sentiment. Part of the Advanced AI add-on.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Customer service carries no Annex III domain. Escalate to essential_services if AI decides eligibility or claims outcomes for individuals, and to employment if Zendesk runs an internal HR helpdesk. Note that ticket sentiment analysis applied to EMPLOYEE conversations rather than customer ones would raise the Art. 5(1)(f) emotion-inference question.","docs_url":"https://www.zendesk.com/service/ai/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"freshworks","name":"Freshworks (Freshdesk / Freshservice)","vendor":"Freshworks","category":"Customer Service / ITSM","walkthrough_category":"service_desk","prevalence":2,"aliases":["freshdesk","freshservice","freshsales","freddy","freddy ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Freddy AI Copilot","capabilities":["summarization","content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Agent-assist add-on at roughly USD 29 per agent per month, restricted to Pro and Enterprise plans. Suggests replies, summarises ticket threads, adjusts tone and drafts knowledge articles. Can be assigned to a subset of agents, so partial deployment is common — expect active_partial rather than a clean yes/no.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Freddy AI Agent (autonomous bot)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Session-based pricing separate from Copilot. Interacts directly with natural persons.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Freddy Insights","capabilities":["summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Analytics and proactive recommendations, included free alongside the Copilot add-on.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Service desk use carries no Annex III domain. Freshservice is frequently used for employee-facing IT and HR onboarding workflows — if Freddy handles HR cases, the employment domain applies.","docs_url":"https://www.freshworks.com/freddy-ai/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"intercom","name":"Intercom (Fin)","vendor":"Intercom","category":"Customer Service / Messaging","walkthrough_category":"service_desk","prevalence":2,"aliases":["intercom","fin","fin ai","fin.ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Fin AI Agent","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Outcome-priced at roughly USD 0.99 per resolution with a minimum monthly commitment. Fin resolves customer conversations autonomously, including handoffs and disqualifications. Deliberately purchased, so it is rarely dormant — if a customer has Intercom's AI, they are using it.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Fin AI Copilot (agent assist)","capabilities":["summarization","content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Assists human support agents with drafted answers and conversation summaries.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Fin talks directly to customers, so Art. 50(1) transparency is the primary obligation — users must be told they are speaking to AI. Escalate to essential_services if Fin decides eligibility, refunds or access to a service for individuals rather than routing to a human.","docs_url":"https://fin.ai/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"notion","name":"Notion","vendor":"Notion","category":"Knowledge Management / Productivity","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":3,"aliases":["notion ai","notion workspace"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Notion AI (writing, Q&A, agents)","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization","chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"active","notes":"The separate USD 10 AI add-on was retired in May 2025 and AI is now bundled into the Business and Enterprise plans. Free and Plus plans get only a limited trial. So on Business or above, AI is present by default; below that it effectively is not.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Notion Agent and Custom Agents","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Task automation and recurring team workflows. Customer-authored agents raise the provider question.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Enterprise Search and AI Meeting Notes","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Cross-app search over connected tools and automatic meeting transcription and notes.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"No Annex III domain in general knowledge work. Small organisations sometimes run hiring pipelines in Notion databases — if a Notion agent screens or ranks applicants, the employment domain applies and the organisation is likely the PROVIDER of that agent.","docs_url":"https://www.notion.com/product/ai","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"asana","name":"Asana","vendor":"Asana","category":"Work Management","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":2,"aliases":["asana ai","asana ai studio"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI Studio (no-code AI workflow builder)","capabilities":["basic_automation","content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Included on ALL paid plans with a base credit allotment — 50,000 credits monthly on Starter, 75,000 on Advanced, 200,000 on Enterprise. Only the Personal plan excludes it. Higher tiers (AI Studio Plus and Pro) are paid upgrades. Because it is bundled, it is commonly present without a decision.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI Teammates","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","basic_automation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Prebuilt agents that triage, prioritise and act on work items alongside human team members.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Smart summaries and status updates","capabilities":["summarization","content_generation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Project status generation and thread summarisation.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Work management is not Annex III by default. The conditional to probe is task allocation: Annex III point 4 covers AI that allocates tasks based on individual behaviour or personal traits, and monitors or evaluates worker performance. If AI Studio workflows assign work to named people based on their past throughput, or if managers use AI workload analytics to assess individuals, the employment domain applies.","docs_url":"https://asana.com/product/ai","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"monday","name":"monday.com","vendor":"monday.com","category":"Work Management","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":3,"aliases":["monday","monday com","monday work management","monday crm"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"monday AI (Sidekick, AI blocks, agents)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","summarization","basic_automation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"From 6 May 2026 AI credits are included in the purchase flow for new customers, with minimum monthly allocations by plan (1,000 Basic, 2,000 Standard, 3,000 Pro). Because credits are bought alongside seats, AI capability is effectively provisioned by default on new subscriptions. Credits do not roll over.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI-assisted automations and digital workers","capabilities":["basic_automation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Agent-style automations acting on boards. Customer-configured, which raises the provider question for anything consequential.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Same task-allocation conditional as Asana under Annex III point 4. Additionally, monday.com is often used as a lightweight recruiting pipeline in smaller organisations — if AI ranks or screens candidates on a hiring board, the employment domain applies.","docs_url":"https://support.monday.com/hc/en-us/articles/35277848309394-The-pricing-model-for-monday-AI-portfolio","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"miro","name":"Miro","vendor":"Miro","category":"Collaboration / Whiteboarding","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":3,"aliases":["miro board","miro ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Miro AI (generation, clustering, summarisation)","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Credit-metered and present from the Free plan upward (10 AI credits monthly free, more on Starter, Business and Enterprise). Generates diagrams, clusters sticky notes and summarises boards.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Sidekicks and Flows","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Specialised AI collaborators and visual AI workflows, available on the Business plan and above.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Whiteboarding carries no Annex III domain. Low-priority system for AI Act purposes — record it, note the transparency obligation for AI-generated content under Art. 50(2), and move on.","docs_url":"https://miro.com/ai/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"docusign","name":"DocuSign IAM","vendor":"DocuSign","category":"Contract Lifecycle Management / eSignature","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":4,"aliases":["docusign","docu sign","docusign navigator","docusign clm"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Navigator with Iris AI (agreement intelligence)","capabilities":["document_analysis","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Extracts structured data from unstructured agreements, enables semantic contract search with fuzzy matching, and correlates terms across documents. Part of the IAM platform rather than classic eSignature — a customer with only DocuSign eSignature does NOT have this.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI contract agents (review, drafting, negotiation)","capabilities":["document_analysis","content_generation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Agents that surface related agreements, recommend specific language updates for compliance and assist negotiation. Recommending contract terms edges toward decisions affecting obligations — watch the automated_decision_making escalation.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Contract analysis carries no Annex III domain in commercial B2B use. Escalate if AI autonomously accepts or rejects contract terms without human review, since automated_decision_making escalates to High Risk where decisions materially affect rights or obligations. If employment contracts are the corpus and AI influences terms offered to individual workers, the employment domain applies.","docs_url":"https://www.docusign.com/products/platform/navigator","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"adobe-acrobat","name":"Adobe Acrobat (AI Assistant)","vendor":"Adobe","category":"Document Management","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":4,"aliases":["acrobat","adobe acrobat","acrobat pro","acrobat ai assistant","acrobat studio"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Acrobat AI Assistant","capabilities":["document_analysis","summarization","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"A PAID ADD-ON, explicitly not included in Acrobat Pro or Standard. Provides generative summary, generative Q&A and generative outline over PDFs. Free users get a small number of requests, which means a limited capability may exist even without the add-on. Acrobat Studio (2026) includes AI Assistant by default — check which SKU the customer holds.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Document reading carries no Annex III domain. The relevant question is data governance rather than classification: Adobe routes AI traffic to third-party model providers, with optional in-tenant processing on enterprise plans, so confidential document content may leave the tenant.","docs_url":"https://www.adobe.com/acrobat/generative-ai-pdf.html","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"adobe-creative-cloud","name":"Adobe Creative Cloud (Firefly)","vendor":"Adobe","category":"Creative / Design","walkthrough_category":"marketing","prevalence":3,"aliases":["creative cloud","photoshop","illustrator","firefly","adobe firefly","adobe express"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Firefly generative image, video and audio","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Generative Fill, Generative Expand and Text to Image are embedded across Photoshop, Illustrator and Express and consume generative credits included with Creative Cloud subscriptions. Effectively on by default for any Creative Cloud seat.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"No Annex III domain. The live obligation is Art. 50(2) and 50(4): AI-generated or manipulated image, audio and video content must be disclosed in a machine-readable form, and deepfakes must be labelled human-perceptibly. Adobe's Content Credentials help satisfy this but must actually be enabled and preserved through the publishing pipeline. This is a marketing-team compliance task, not an IT one.","docs_url":"https://www.adobe.com/products/firefly.html","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"canva","name":"Canva","vendor":"Canva","category":"Creative / Design","walkthrough_category":"marketing","prevalence":4,"aliases":["canva pro","canva teams","magic studio","canva ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Magic Studio (Magic Write, Magic Media, Magic Edit)","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"AI text, image and short-video generation plus Magic Edit, Magic Eraser, Magic Grab and Magic Expand are built into the editor and available to free and paid users alike, with higher limits on paid plans. Administrators can restrict Magic and AI features through admin controls, so confirm whether they have.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Magic Design (template and design generation)","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Generates on-brand designs from a text prompt or uploaded assets.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"No Annex III domain. Same Art. 50(2)/50(4) disclosure obligation as Adobe Firefly for any externally published generated or manipulated content. Canva's very low friction and free-tier availability mean it is often in use across an organisation without appearing in any procurement record — ask marketing directly rather than relying on the IT application inventory.","docs_url":"https://www.canva.com/canva-ai/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"teamtailor","name":"Teamtailor","vendor":"Teamtailor","category":"ATS / Recruitment","walkthrough_category":"employment","prevalence":3,"aliases":["team tailor","teamtailor ats"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Co-pilot resume summarisation","capabilities":["summarization","document_analysis"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Automatically summarises incoming resumes as a five-point bullet list on the candidate profile. Powered by OpenAI models.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Co-pilot candidate screening and matching","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Evaluates candidates against defined hiring criteria and suggests matches from existing talent pools. This is selection of natural persons for employment — the clearest Annex III point 4 trigger in the catalogue.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Co-pilot job advert generation and interview questions","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Drafts job descriptions from a title and suggests three interview questions per skill or trait. Targeted job advertising is explicitly named in Annex III point 4.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Meeting insights (interview transcription and summaries)","capabilities":["summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Real-time written summaries of candidate interviews, mapping answers to interview kit questions.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Bias mitigation in AI summaries","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Flags skills matches while anonymising demographic data during initial screening. A mitigation control, NOT a reason to downgrade classification — Art. 10 requires bias examination of High Risk systems, so this helps satisfy an obligation rather than avoid one.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"HIGH RISK CONDITIONAL — and in practice usually unconditional. Teamtailor is popular across the Nordics and any Co-pilot feature touching candidate evaluation places it in Annex III point 4. Note that the customer is a DEPLOYER here, which still carries Art. 26 obligations including human oversight, input data relevance and informing affected candidates.","docs_url":"https://www.teamtailor.com/en/ai-recruiting-software/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"greenhouse","name":"Greenhouse","vendor":"Greenhouse Software","category":"ATS / Recruitment","walkthrough_category":"employment","prevalence":2,"aliases":["greenhouse ats","greenhouse recruiting"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Greenhouse Notetaker","capabilities":["summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Records and transcribes interviews, generating AI notes mapped to scorecard questions. Launched mid-2026. Recording candidates raises GDPR consent obligations alongside AI Act ones.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Candidate Insights Agent","capabilities":["summarization","recommendation_profiling","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Answers questions about a candidate from scorecards, notes and activity, and surfaces hiring-manager briefings. Synthesises an assessment of a natural person for a hiring decision.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Job Kickoff Agent and AI Report Insights","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Structures job setup from kickoff notes, and turns recruiting dashboards into shareable summaries with suggested next steps. Rolling out through Q3 2026.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Greenhouse MCP (external AI tool access)","capabilities":["natural_language_query"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Connects approved external AI tools such as Claude, Gemini or Copilot to Greenhouse data. IMPORTANT: this creates a route for candidate data to reach a general-purpose AI system outside the ATS — assess it as its own data flow.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"HIGH RISK CONDITIONAL. Greenhouse positions its AI as strengthening structured hiring rather than shortcutting it, and keeps humans in the decision seat — a genuine design choice, but Annex III point 4 covers AI INTENDED TO BE USED for recruitment and selection, not only AI that decides alone. The Candidate Insights Agent informing a hiring decision is in scope.","docs_url":"https://www.greenhouse.com/ai-recruiting","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"lever","name":"Lever (LeverTRM)","vendor":"Employ Inc.","category":"ATS / Recruitment CRM","walkthrough_category":"employment","prevalence":2,"aliases":["lever","levertrm","lever ats"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Talent Fit (AI-powered candidate matching)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Ranks candidates against job requirements and returns a shortlist with scoring and explanations, including gaps to clarify. RANKING CANDIDATES IS THE ARCHETYPAL ANNEX III POINT 4 USE CASE.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"AI-powered screening","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Automatically prioritises top candidates using AI matching, insights and signals.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"AI Interview Companion and transcripts","capabilities":["summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Structured pre-call insights plus unlimited AI transcripts and summaries of interviews.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"AI fraud detection signals","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Flags suspicious applications by detecting potential fraud patterns. Flagging an applicant as suspicious is a consequential automated judgement about a natural person and warrants particular scrutiny of error rates and appeal routes.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"HIGH RISK CONDITIONAL. Lever's transparent scoring is helpful for the Art. 13 explainability obligation but does not change the classification. The fraud-detection signal deserves separate attention: a false positive can silently eliminate a candidate, which is exactly the harm Annex III point 4 exists to address.","docs_url":"https://www.lever.co/solutions/ai-powered-screening","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"recruitee","name":"Recruitee (Tellent)","vendor":"Tellent","category":"ATS / Recruitment","walkthrough_category":"employment","prevalence":2,"aliases":["recruitee","tellent","tellent recruitee"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI job description creation and translation","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Drafts and translates job descriptions using NLP.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"AI application evaluation against criteria","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Evaluates applications against predefined criteria and rediscovers existing candidates in the ATS. Assessment of natural persons for selection.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"AI Evaluation Insights (feedback summarisation)","capabilities":["summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Consolidates hiring-team comments into summaries highlighting recurring themes to support decision-making.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"AI-personalised candidate and rejection emails","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Generates personalised candidate correspondence including rejection messages.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"HIGH RISK CONDITIONAL. Tellent explicitly positions its AI as decision-SUPPORT with hiring decisions remaining with recruiters. That is the right design and supports Art. 14 human oversight, but Annex III point 4 captures systems intended to be used FOR recruitment and selection, so support-only framing does not exempt the evaluation feature.","docs_url":"https://recruitee.com/ai-recruitment-automation","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"linkedin-recruiter","name":"LinkedIn Recruiter","vendor":"Microsoft (LinkedIn)","category":"Sourcing / Recruitment","walkthrough_category":"employment","prevalence":4,"aliases":["linkedin recruiter","linkedin talent solutions","linkedin hiring assistant","recruiter lite"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI-Assisted Search","capabilities":["natural_language_query","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active","notes":"IMPORTANT DEFAULT: AI-Assisted Search has REPLACED boolean search as the default in Recruiter. Recruiters describe a role in natural language and the system recommends candidates based on inferred skills, experience and fit. Because it is the default rather than an option, a customer using Recruiter at all is using AI candidate recommendation.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Hiring Assistant (AI agent)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making","content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"LinkedIn's first AI agent, generally available in English since late 2025. Automates intake, sourcing, PRE-SCREENING and message drafting. Pre-screening applicants is directly within Annex III point 4.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"HIGH RISK CONDITIONAL, with an unusual wrinkle worth raising with the client: because AI-Assisted Search is now the DEFAULT, an organisation cannot claim to use LinkedIn Recruiter without AI. Note also that the deployer has limited visibility into how LinkedIn's matching model works, which makes the Art. 26 obligations around input data relevance and human oversight harder to discharge — a genuine procurement risk to document.","docs_url":"https://business.linkedin.com/hire/hiring-assistant","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"qualtrics","name":"Qualtrics XM","vendor":"Qualtrics","category":"Experience Management / Survey","walkthrough_category":"employment","prevalence":2,"aliases":["qualtrics","qualtrics xm","text iq","qualtrics employee experience"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Text iQ with emotion and emotional-intensity enrichment","capabilities":["emotion_recognition","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"READ THIS CAREFULLY. Qualtrics runs cross-lingual models that assign each free-text response a value on four dimensions: actionability, effort, EMOTION and EMOTIONAL INTENSITY. The emotion dimension determines what the respondent is feeling and the intensity dimension how strongly. This is emotion inference from text, not merely sentiment polarity.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Employee Experience (EX) engagement, lifecycle and 360 feedback analytics","capabilities":["emotion_recognition","recommendation_profiling","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"The EX product applies the same Text iQ enrichment to EMPLOYEE feedback — engagement surveys, lifecycle surveys, manager 360s and exit interviews.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Experience Agents","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Autonomous agents that detect friction in real time and act to resolve it across surveys, tickets and digital experiences.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Automated Text Analytics (generative topic modelling)","capabilities":["summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Generative topic modelling and classification across calls, chats, surveys and social, enriched with sentiment and emotion data.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"THE SHARPEST FINDING IN THIS CATALOGUE — and it is a PROHIBITION question, not a High Risk one. Art. 5(1)(f) bans emotion recognition systems in the workplace and in education institutions, except for medical or safety reasons. Qualtrics Text iQ infers emotion and emotional intensity, and Qualtrics EX applies exactly that to employee feedback. If a client runs Qualtrics employee engagement surveys with emotion enrichment enabled, escalate immediately: this is a candidate Art. 5 prohibited practice with an August 2025 compliance date already passed, not something to schedule for a future High Risk workstream. Customer-facing (CX) use of the same feature is not prohibited — the workplace context is what triggers Art. 5(1)(f). Establish precisely which surveys are in scope.","docs_url":"https://www.qualtrics.com/platform/experience-agents/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"surveymonkey","name":"SurveyMonkey","vendor":"SurveyMonkey (Momentive)","category":"Survey / Feedback","walkthrough_category":"employment","prevalence":3,"aliases":["survey monkey","momentive"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Build with AI (survey generation)","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Available FREE to all SurveyMonkey users — generates a survey from a plain-language description in seconds, with chat-based editing. Present on every account including free ones.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI Analysis Suite (sentiment analysis, thematic analysis, response quality)","capabilities":["summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Sorts open-ended responses by positive, negative or neutral tone, surfaces themes, and flags low-quality or suspicious responses. Available on Advantage-tier plans and above. NOTE: sentiment polarity is a narrower thing than emotion inference — do not automatically map this to emotion_recognition the way Qualtrics Text iQ warrants.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI survey theme generator","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Generates branded survey themes from logos and colours. No effect on persons.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Customer surveys carry no Annex III domain. If SurveyMonkey is used for EMPLOYEE engagement surveys, the employment domain applies to the analysis features. Probe whether sentiment analysis has been extended to anything resembling emotional state inference — if it has, the Art. 5(1)(f) workplace prohibition becomes live, as with Qualtrics.","docs_url":"https://www.surveymonkey.com/product/features/ai/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"zoominfo","name":"ZoomInfo","vendor":"ZoomInfo","category":"Sales Intelligence / Data","walkthrough_category":"crm_sales","prevalence":2,"aliases":["zoom info","zoominfo copilot","gtm.ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"ZoomInfo Copilot","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","content_generation","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Surfaces and prioritises decision-makers in real time, personalises outreach and automates CRM updates. Built on a data graph of roughly 100 million companies and 500 million contacts.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"GTM.AI context layer and intent signals","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Headless context layer exposing enrichment, intent signals, technographics and contact verification to external agents including Copilot Studio, Agentforce, HubSpot Breeze and general-purpose assistants.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"No Annex III domain — this profiles individuals in their professional capacity for B2B sales. The dominant legal exposure here is GDPR (lawful basis for processing contact data obtained without the data subject's knowledge, and Art. 14 notification duties) rather than the AI Act. Flag it to the client as such so the AI Act assessment does not become the only place this risk is recorded.","docs_url":"https://www.zoominfo.com/products/copilot","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"gong","name":"Gong","vendor":"Gong","category":"Revenue Intelligence / Conversation Analytics","walkthrough_category":"crm_sales","prevalence":2,"aliases":["gong io","gong.io","gong revenue ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Conversation intelligence (call recording, transcription, analysis)","capabilities":["summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Core to the product — every call is processed through speech recognition and NLP, extracting objections, competitor mentions and talk-time. If an organisation has Gong at all, this is on; there is no meaningful 'Gong without AI' configuration.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI Coach and rep scorecards","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Identifies top-performing behaviours, builds SCORECARDS FOR INDIVIDUAL SALES REPS and lets managers track individual performance and coaching needs. This analyses and rates the behaviour of named employees.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Deal risk and forecast modelling","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Models deal risk from call and email-cadence signals. Operates on deals, though the underlying signals are individual rep activity.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"HIGH RISK CONDITIONAL, and frequently missed because Gong is filed under 'sales tools' rather than 'HR tools'. Annex III point 4 covers AI used to MONITOR AND EVALUATE THE PERFORMANCE AND BEHAVIOUR of workers. Gong's AI Coach and scorecards do precisely that for named sales representatives, so where managers use Gong output in performance reviews, coaching decisions or quota and territory allocation, the employment domain applies and the system is High Risk. Two further points: recording customer calls triggers consent obligations in several EU member states, and any feature inferring rep or customer emotional state from voice would raise the Art. 5(1)(f) workplace prohibition — confirm whether voice-based emotion analytics is in use.","docs_url":"https://www.gong.io/platform/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"outreach","name":"Outreach","vendor":"Outreach","category":"Sales Engagement","walkthrough_category":"crm_sales","prevalence":2,"aliases":["outreach io","outreach.io","kaia"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Kaia (conversation intelligence and live coaching)","capabilities":["summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Outreach's conversation intelligence product, distinct from its named agents. Transcribes meetings, coaches sellers live during the call, and identifies SENTIMENT and the context in which topics are discussed. Afterwards it writes the summary and follow-up email and updates deal fields automatically.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Revenue Agent, Research Agent and Deal Agent","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","content_generation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Outreach's agentic layer. The Research Agent analyses individual stakeholders from internal and public sources; the Deal Agent acts on pipeline. Confirm which agents are contracted — the agentic tier is a separate commercial conversation from core sequences.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI email generation and pipeline forecasting","capabilities":["content_generation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Generates sequence content and forecasts pipeline outcomes.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Same conditional as Gong but weaker: real-time coaching of named sellers produces individual performance signal, so if managers use Kaia output to evaluate representatives, the employment domain applies under Annex III point 4. Confirm how coaching data is used before concluding either way. Kaia's sentiment detection is aimed at customer conversations rather than employee emotional state, so it does not by itself raise Art. 5(1)(f) — but verify it is not being applied to infer the seller's own state.","docs_url":"https://www.outreach.ai/ai-agents","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"onestream","name":"OneStream","vendor":"OneStream Software","category":"Financial Consolidation / CPM","walkthrough_category":"erp_finance","prevalence":2,"aliases":["one stream","onestream xf","sensible ml"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"SensibleAI Forecast","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"AutoML forecasting engine blending internal financial data with external drivers such as macroeconomic indicators and weather. Designed for explainability, exposing forecast drivers and model behaviour. Forecasts financial line items, NOT persons — the recommendation_profiling mapping is imperfect here but is the closest available key.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"SensibleAI Agents and SensibleAI Studio","capabilities":["basic_automation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Agents automating finance tasks, plus a studio for building custom agents. Customer-built agents raise the provider question.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Generative scenario commentary","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Produces narrative commentary explaining forecast context.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Financial consolidation and planning carry no Annex III domain — this operates on financial aggregates, not on natural persons. One of the genuinely low-risk entries in this catalogue, though workforce planning models that forecast headcount reductions for named teams would warrant a second look.","docs_url":"https://www.onestream.com/solutions/sensible-ai-forecast/","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"silverbucket","name":"Silverbucket (EG Silverbucket)","vendor":"EG (formerly Silverbucket Oy)","category":"Resource Management","walkthrough_category":"erp_finance","prevalence":1,"aliases":["silver bucket","silverbucket","eg silverbucket"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI Resourcing Assistant","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"CHANGED DURING 2026 — do not rely on older assessments. EG Silverbucket now markets 'AI-Powered Resource Intelligence' with an AI Resourcing Assistant that surfaces insights, automates routine tasks and answers questions through a chat interface for resource managers and leadership. Inventories captured in early 2026 recorded Silverbucket as having no AI features at all; that is no longer accurate.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI demand forecasting from historical patterns","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Learns from historical patterns to forecast future resourcing demand and anticipate hiring needs. Announced for H2 2026 — confirm general availability before recording as present.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Worth a careful look despite being a small vendor. Resource management software ALLOCATES NAMED PEOPLE TO PROJECTS, and Annex III point 4 covers AI used to allocate tasks based on individual behaviour or personal traits and to evaluate worker performance. If the AI Resourcing Assistant recommends who staffs what based on individual utilisation or capability, the employment domain applies. The forecasting feature explicitly anticipates HIRING NEEDS, which connects it to recruitment planning.","docs_url":"https://egsoftware.com/global/hseq-and-asset-management/eg-silverbucket","researched":"2026-08-07"},{"id":"chatgpt","name":"ChatGPT (Free / Go / Plus / Pro, Business and Enterprise)","vendor":"OpenAI","category":"General-purpose AI Assistant / Shadow AI","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":5,"aliases":["chatgpt","chat gpt","openai","gpt","chatgpt enterprise","chatgpt team","chatgpt business","openai chatgpt"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"ChatGPT consumer tiers (Free, Go, Plus, Pro — personal accounts)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","summarization","document_analysis"],"default_state":"active","notes":"The single most common piece of unmanaged AI in a European estate: employees signing in with a personal account and pasting work content in. On personal accounts the 'Improve the model for everyone' data control is ON by default, so conversations and uploaded files can be used to train OpenAI models unless the individual turns it off in Settings > Data Controls. There is no organisational DPA, no admin visibility, no retention control and no audit trail. This is the entry's whole point: the same brand name behaves completely differently depending on which account the person is signed into, and the consultant cannot tell from the UI.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"ChatGPT Business (formerly Team) and ChatGPT Enterprise","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","summarization","document_analysis","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Separately purchased per-seat workspace tiers. OpenAI states business data is not used to train its models by default — the same commitment it publishes for the API ('data sent to the OpenAI API is not used to train or improve OpenAI models unless you explicitly opt in', developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/your-data). Enterprise adds SSO/SCIM, domain verification, retention controls, audit logging and a Compliance API. An organisation that has NOT bought this has none of it, which is why the correct default state here is not_licensed even though the product is everywhere. Ask the system owner whether SSO domain capture is enforced — without it, staff keep using personal accounts alongside the paid workspace.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"ChatGPT is a general-purpose AI model/system; OpenAI carries the GPAI provider obligations under Chapter V. The deployer's exposure is entirely about use. It becomes an Annex III high-risk deployment the moment it is put inside a covered decision process — pasting CVs in to rank or shortlist candidates is Annex III point 4(a); using it to draft or justify decisions on eligibility for benefits, credit or insurance is point 5(a)/(b); using it to draft an employee's performance assessment is point 4(b). Building it into a customer-facing bot triggers Art. 50(1) disclosure that the person is interacting with an AI system, and Art. 50(2)/(4) labelling for synthetic content and deep fakes. Note the boundary: the consumer-tier training default is primarily a GDPR and trade-secret problem, not an AI Act one — do not conflate the two in the report. Art. 4 AI-literacy duties apply to any organisation whose staff use it in the course of work, licensed or not.","docs_url":"https://openai.com/enterprise-privacy/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"gemini-app-consumer","name":"Gemini app (Google AI Plus / Pro / Ultra, personal accounts)","vendor":"Google","category":"General-purpose AI Assistant / Shadow AI","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":4,"aliases":["gemini app","google ai pro","google ai ultra","google one ai","bard","gemini advanced"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Gemini Apps on a personal Google Account (Keep Activity / Gemini Apps Activity)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","summarization","document_analysis"],"default_state":"active","notes":"This entry deliberately covers the CONSUMER product only — Gemini in Google Workspace is a different contract and is handled under the google-workspace entry. Google states that only personal Google Accounts can subscribe to Google AI Plus/Pro/Ultra; Workspace tenants buy a Gemini add-on instead. On a personal account the 'Keep Activity' setting is on by default, a subset of chats is read by human reviewers including Google's trained service providers, reviewed chats are retained for up to three years disconnected from the account, and standard activity auto-deletes after 18 months. Turning Keep Activity off still leaves 72 hours of retention. On Android the assistant is a system-level default, so the shadow-AI exposure here arrives on the device rather than through procurement.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Not inherently Annex III; Google is the GPAI provider. The reason to keep this as a separate catalogue entry from google-workspace is that the two share a brand but not a legal posture: the consumer app has human review and three-year retention of reviewed chats and no enterprise DPA, and no Workspace admin control reaches it. Escalation to high risk follows the same rule as any general assistant — putting it into recruitment shortlisting (Annex III 4(a)), employee evaluation (4(b)) or eligibility decisions for essential services (point 5). The practical finding to write up is usually a GDPR one (Art. 5, Art. 28 processor, and international-transfer questions) plus an Art. 4 AI-literacy gap, not an AI Act classification. If the organisation already licenses Gemini in Workspace, the correct remediation is to route staff to that tenant, not to ban the brand.","docs_url":"https://support.google.com/gemini/answer/13594961","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"claude-ai","name":"Claude (Free, Pro, Max, Team and Enterprise)","vendor":"Anthropic","category":"General-purpose AI Assistant / Shadow AI","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":3,"aliases":["claude","claude ai","anthropic","claude for work","claude team","claude enterprise"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Claude consumer tiers (Free, Pro, Max — personal accounts)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","summarization","document_analysis"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Personal-account Claude, the same shadow-AI pattern as consumer ChatGPT. Anthropic's pricing page states model training applies to consumer plans with an opt-out available to the user, so the organisation has no control over it. Free includes web search, memory, file creation and code execution; Pro adds Claude Code and unlimited Projects, which means an employee on a personal Pro subscription can be running an agent against local files and repositories with no corporate oversight. There is no admin console over these accounts.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Claude Team and Claude Enterprise","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","summarization","document_analysis","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Separately purchased seats. Anthropic states Team and Enterprise content is not used for model training by default. Team gives central billing, SSO, admin controls over remote and local connectors, and enterprise search across the organisation's connected systems; Enterprise adds SCIM, audit logs, a Compliance API for observability, extended data-retention options and a HIPAA-ready offering. The connector admin control is the one to check first — 'enterprise search across your organisation' means Claude can be pointed at Google Drive, SharePoint or a ticketing system, which changes the data-protection assessment substantially.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Same structure as ChatGPT: Anthropic is the GPAI provider, the customer is a deployer, and risk tier is decided by what the assistant is wired into. No Annex III domain is inherent. It escalates when Claude (or a Claude Code agent) is placed in the loop of an Annex III decision — CV screening or candidate ranking (point 4(a)), performance evaluation text (point 4(b)), or eligibility assessments for essential services (point 5). Art. 50(1) applies if a Claude-backed assistant talks to customers or citizens. A specific thing to ask: whether anyone is running Claude Code or agentic connectors against production systems, because an agent that acts (writes to a CRM, sends mail, changes a record) is a materially different risk conversation from a chat window, and the licence tier does not tell you which is happening.","docs_url":"https://claude.com/pricing","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"grammarly-superhuman","name":"Grammarly (Superhuman Suite: Grammarly, Go, Mail, Docs)","vendor":"Superhuman (formerly Grammarly, Inc.)","category":"Writing Assistance / Productivity Suite","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":3,"aliases":["grammarly","grammarly business","superhuman","superhuman go","grammarly enterprise","coda"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Grammarly writing assistant (browser extension and desktop app)","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization","document_analysis"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"The classic uninventoried AI. Individuals install the free browser extension themselves, and once installed it reads and rewrites text in essentially every input field in the browser — including the free-text boxes of an HR system, a CRM, a case management tool or a patient-facing portal. No procurement event occurs, so it never appears on a software register. Marked active_partial because in most estates it is genuinely running on some machines and not others. First question for the system owner: is the extension blocked by browser policy, and if not, on how many endpoints is it present.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Superhuman Suite — Superhuman Go agents, Mail, Docs (Coda)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","summarization","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Grammarly, Inc. renamed itself Superhuman in October 2025 after acquiring Coda and Superhuman Labs; the Grammarly product keeps its name inside the suite. Superhuman Go is a cross-application AI assistant with a no-code agent builder and a marketplace of pre-built agents, and Docs (Coda) connects to 800+ tools. Sold as a separate subscription. The enterprise tier provides SAML SSO, SCIM, custom roles, audit logging, legal hold and BYOK, and Superhuman states customer content is never used to train AI models — but also states data is stored in US data centres, which is the item to raise with a European client rather than the AI Act classification.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Writing assistance is not Annex III on its own — rewriting a sentence makes no decision about a person. Two boundaries matter. First, an agent from Superhuman Go that is granted write access to an HR or applicant system and is used to draft assessments or screen candidates crosses into Annex III point 4(a)/(b); the agent builder makes that a configuration change, not a purchase, so the risk classification can move without procurement noticing. Second, Art. 50(2) obligations on marking synthetic content apply to generated text published as public-interest information. Nothing here scores or ranks employees, and I found no employee-monitoring or productivity-scoring feature documented on the enterprise page — do not assume one exists.","docs_url":"https://www.superhuman.com/enterprise","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"box-ai","name":"Box AI","vendor":"Box","category":"Content Cloud / Document AI","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":2,"aliases":["box","box ai","box ai studio","box hubs","box agent","box extract"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Box AI (AI Home, Preview, Notes, Hubs, Box Agent)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","summarization","natural_language_query","content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Box AI is not on by default. An administrator must accept the Box AI legal terms (product addendum or a negotiated agreement) and then click Enable Box AI in Admin Console > Box AI > Settings. Access can be granted to all managed users, to named users and groups (up to 100 each), or to everyone except named exclusions — so partial rollouts are common and 'we don't use AI' from a business owner is frequently wrong. Box shipped a redesigned Box Agent in April 2026. Enterprise plans include 1,000 AI Units; Business and Business Plus must buy units. A June 2026 AI Units Admin Report gives admins consumption visibility per user, which is the fastest way to find out who is actually using it.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Box AI Extract and Box AI Studio","capabilities":["document_analysis","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Extract pulls structured fields out of documents and writes them back as Box metadata; AI Studio is configured separately from the general Box AI toggle and lets an organisation build custom agents over its own content. Both consume AI Units and the API paths require purchased units on Business and Business Plus. Extract is the one to look at closely in a compliance review: once contract, invoice or HR-document fields are being extracted into metadata automatically, downstream workflows start making decisions on values no human verified.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Content management is domain-neutral, so typical_domains is empty deliberately. The escalation depends on the repository: Box holding recruitment files and Box AI Extract parsing CVs into metadata that feeds a shortlisting workflow is Annex III point 4(a); Box holding loan or insurance files with Extract feeding an eligibility decision is point 5(b). Neither is a Box product property — it is a customer configuration, which is exactly why the AI Units Admin Report matters as evidence. No biometric or emotion capability is documented, so Art. 5 is not engaged. Where Box AI is used to draft communications sent to consumers, Art. 50 disclosure applies to the channel, not to Box.","docs_url":"https://docs.box.com/en/box-ai/admins/configuring-box-ai","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"dropbox-dash","name":"Dropbox Dash","vendor":"Dropbox","category":"Enterprise Search / Knowledge Assistant","walkthrough_category":"productivity","prevalence":2,"aliases":["dash","dropbox dash","dropbox ai","dash for business"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Dash universal search and Answers","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization","document_analysis","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Dash is a separately sold product, not a switch inside Dropbox core — a Dropbox file-storage customer does not get it automatically, which is why the default state is not_licensed rather than available_not_enabled. It indexes content across connected third-party apps (Google Drive, Outlook, Slack, Salesforce and others) and answers natural-language questions over the combined index, plus summarisation and drafting from a start page. Dropbox states Dash respects source-system permissions at document level in near real time. The compliance question is not the AI: it is that deploying Dash creates a second index of content whose original access controls live somewhere else, so verify the permission mirroring rather than taking it on trust.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Enterprise search over a company's own documents is not an Annex III use in itself and makes no decision about a natural person. It escalates only through what is indexed and who queries it: if the index reaches HR case files, occupational-health records or disciplinary material and Dash is then used to assemble a picture of an individual employee, the deployment starts to look like monitoring under Annex III point 4(b) — that is a deployment choice, not a product property. No emotion recognition, biometric processing or scoring is documented in Dash, so Art. 5 is not in play. Treat the main finding as GDPR data-minimisation and connector scoping.","docs_url":"https://dash.dropbox.com/features/universal-search","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"lime-crm","name":"Lime CRM","vendor":"Lime Technologies","category":"CRM (Nordic mid-market)","walkthrough_category":"crm_sales","prevalence":3,"aliases":["lime","lime crm","lime technologies","lime go","lundalogik"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Lime CRM AI Agents — customer service (First Line Support Agent, Reopen Ticket Agent, Solution Agent)","capabilities":["document_analysis","summarization","basic_automation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"unknown","notes":"First Line Support Agent triages every inbound ticket — categorising, prioritising, translating and flagging duplicates. Reopen Ticket Agent reads incoming replies and decides autonomously whether to reopen the ticket, raise a new one, or do nothing. Solution Agent drafts a resolution summary for the handler at closure. The Reopen decision runs without a human in the loop, which is why automated_decision_making is tagged. Recorded as unknown deliberately: Lime's AI page describes each agent but does not state whether they are included in the base licence, sold as an AI package, or switched on per installation. Do not assume — get it in writing from the Lime partner, because the answer changes the whole assessment.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Lime CRM AI Agents — sales and marketing (Deal Predictor, Next Best Action, Win/Loss, Lead Qualifier, NPS Survey, Mailing Insights)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","summarization","basic_automation"],"default_state":"unknown","notes":"Deal Predictor scores deals objectively and suggests next steps; Next Best Action recommends follow-ups from meeting notes and calls; Win/Loss categorises outcomes from historical notes; Lead Qualifier scores website enquiries, filters spam, detects duplicates and routes qualified leads; NPS Survey Agent analyses written survey responses for themes and sentiment; Mailing Insights benchmarks campaign results. Lead Qualifier and Deal Predictor profile identifiable natural persons and their organisations, so GDPR Art. 22 and transparency duties apply even though the AI Act tier does not move. The NPS sentiment analysis reads free-text survey answers — text, not biometric data — so it does not meet the Art. 3(39) definition of an emotion recognition system. Licensing and enablement are not published; same caveat as above.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Lime AI Assist (beta)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"An on-demand assistant inside Lime CRM giving instant answers, summaries, translations and next-step suggestions when a user asks. Lime labels it beta on its own AI page, so treat capability claims and stability with corresponding caution and expect the feature set to change. Lime Technologies also launched a separate Workflows AI platform and AI features in Lime Go and Lime Connect — those are different products and are not covered by this entry.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"No inherent Annex III domain — Lime is horizontal B2B CRM, so typical_domains is empty. It escalates in the same place as any CRM: if a Lime-based service desk or sales process is deciding a natural person's access to an essential private service, lead and deal scoring becomes Annex III point 5(b). Lime has a large public-sector and utilities base in Sweden and Norway, and a Lime deployment used to triage citizen or ratepayer cases in a municipality can drift towards Annex III point 5(a) (public assistance benefits and services) if the AI's triage decision affects the substance of the outcome rather than just the queue. The Reopen Ticket Agent is worth naming explicitly in a report because it takes an autonomous action on a person's case without review — that is not high risk by itself, but it is exactly the kind of silent automation a Art. 26 deployer needs to be able to describe. Be candid in the deliverable that Lime's public documentation does not state licensing or default state for any of these agents.","docs_url":"https://www.lime-technologies.com/en/products/lime-crm/ai/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"pipedrive","name":"Pipedrive","vendor":"Pipedrive","category":"CRM (SMB / sales pipeline)","walkthrough_category":"crm_sales","prevalence":3,"aliases":["pipedrive","pipe drive","pipedrive ai","ai sales assistant"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI Sales Assistant","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","summarization"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"A panel that continuously analyses deals, contacts and email to surface win-probability predictions, flag stalled or neglected deals, recommend next best actions and give performance tips. Because it profiles the buyer-side contacts as well as the deal, GDPR transparency and Art. 22 questions apply to the scoring. Marked active_partial: on plans that include it the assistant simply appears for users with no per-user opt-in, but Pipedrive's own AI product page does not state which tiers include it (resellers report Premium and Ultimate) — confirm entitlement in the account rather than from the marketing page.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI email writer and AI email summarisation (beta)","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Turns short prompts into personalised outreach emails from deal context, and condenses long threads into digests. Pipedrive lists the summariser as beta. Note that summarising a thread means the AI reads the full correspondence with an identifiable individual, including anything the counterparty wrote — a processing question worth recording separately from the generation question. No plan gating is stated on the vendor AI page.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI report generator and AI-powered marketplace search","capabilities":["natural_language_query","content_generation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Pipedrive explicitly states both are available on ALL plans — the report generator builds a chart or metric from a plain-English description of what you want, with 15+ ready prompts, and marketplace search finds integrations in natural language. This is one of the few genuinely on-by-default-for-everyone AI features in this catalogue, which is why active is used here and nowhere else in the CRM group. Low risk in itself; the relevance is that a Pipedrive customer on the cheapest plan who says 'we have no AI' is wrong.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Pipedrive is horizontal SMB sales software with no inherent Annex III use, hence empty typical_domains. The realistic Nordic escalation is intermediaries: Pipedrive is very common among insurance brokers, mortgage brokers, energy resellers and lease finance agents, and once win-probability scoring or lead prioritisation influences which applicants get pursued or what terms they are offered, you are at Annex III point 5(b) (creditworthiness / credit scoring) or 5(c) (life and health insurance risk assessment and pricing). The AI Act tier turns on whether the score affects the person's access to or pricing of the service, not on the vendor's marketing label. Nothing in Pipedrive infers emotional state or processes biometrics, so Art. 5 is not engaged. Where Pipedrive AI drafts messages that a salesperson sends, Art. 50(1) is not triggered — a human is the sender.","docs_url":"https://www.pipedrive.com/en/products/ai-crm","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"superoffice-crm","name":"SuperOffice CRM","vendor":"SuperOffice","category":"CRM (Nordic mid-market)","walkthrough_category":"crm_sales","prevalence":3,"aliases":["superoffice","super office","superoffice crm","superoffice copilot","supernotes"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"SuperOffice Copilot","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"A ChatGPT-style panel inside the CRM that answers questions over the tenant's own contacts, deals and pipelines and can draw on public sources. SuperOffice states AI is built into every plan, which is unusually generous for a Nordic mid-market CRM and is why this is active_partial rather than not_licensed — but it only applies to CRM Online tenants on a current release, and on-premises SuperOffice installations (still common in Norwegian and German mid-market) do not get it. Confirm the deployment model before recording a state.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Writing assist and AI image generation","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Drafts emails, proposals and campaign copy, adjusts tone and length, and generates images from prompts for marketing material. SuperOffice places these in the entry-level plans. Low intrinsic risk — a human still sends the message — but relevant to Art. 50(2) if generated images are published as public-interest content, and to marketing-consent hygiene if it is used to mass-produce outreach.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"SuperOffice AI Agents (Growth, Plus and Super plans)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","basic_automation","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"SuperOffice describes autonomous agents on the higher plan tiers that work across the customer base, surface insights proactively and automate multi-step workflows. The vendor page does not break down which named agents exist per tier, so I have not invented a list — the honest record is that agentic capability is gated to Growth/Plus/Super and a customer on a lower plan does not have it. Ask the system owner which tier the tenant is on and what the agents are permitted to write back to.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"SuperNotes AI meeting assistant","capabilities":["summarization","document_analysis","content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"A separate add-on at EUR 29 per user per month that joins calls, records and transcribes them, does pre-meeting research, and writes structured notes and follow-ups back into SuperOffice with a link from the appointment to the full recording. SuperOffice says it is built with GDPR and EU data handling in mind but the product page does not address participant consent or meeting-recording notification at all — that gap is the finding. In Norway and Sweden this is a recording-consent and works-council question before it is an AI question.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"A CRM is not inherently Annex III, so typical_domains is empty. Two escalation routes. First, if SuperOffice is the front end for a lender, insurer, energy retailer or telco and its AI is used to score or rank a natural person for access to the service, that is Annex III point 5(b) (creditworthiness) or 5(c) (life and health insurance risk assessment and pricing) — common in the Nordic broker market and easy to miss because it looks like ordinary lead scoring. Second, SuperNotes creates an employment exposure that is not obvious: transcripts of a salesperson's own calls, stored against their user, become an evaluation dataset the moment a manager uses them to appraise performance, which is Annex III point 4(b). Note the distinction from Art. 5(1)(f) — SuperNotes transcribes and summarises, it does not infer emotional state, so the prohibition is not engaged. Art. 50(1) does not apply to drafting assistance a human reviews and sends, but does apply if an autonomous SuperOffice agent converses with a customer directly.","docs_url":"https://www.superoffice.com/crm/ai-and-crm/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"zoho-crm","name":"Zoho CRM (Zia)","vendor":"Zoho","category":"CRM (SMB / mid-market suite)","walkthrough_category":"crm_sales","prevalence":2,"aliases":["zoho","zoho crm","zia","ask zia","zoho one"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Zia prediction, scoring and anomaly detection","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Zia scores leads and deals to identify prospects most likely to convert, predicts churn on subscription records, predicts field values, and raises anomaly alerts on unusual patterns to named stakeholders. This directly profiles identifiable people. Zoho gates most Zia prediction capability to the higher CRM editions and it is not present for a Standard or Professional customer, so not_licensed is the correct default — but Zoho's Zia help overview does not state edition limits, prediction quotas or whether Zia self-activates on purchase, so verify entitlement in the account rather than quoting a tier.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Zia email and call intelligence (sentiment, transcription)","capabilities":["emotion_recognition","summarization","document_analysis"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Analyses inbound email and call content for sentiment and intent, and transcribes calls. Tagged emotion_recognition because it infers an emotional state, but read the escalation note before treating that as a prohibition — the analysed party here is the customer, not the employee. The item to check with the system owner is whether the same call transcripts and sentiment scores are surfaced in a manager-facing view of an individual salesperson, because Zoho also markets performance-gap analysis, and that combination is what moves this into employment territory.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Zia Vision and generative build assistance","capabilities":["document_analysis","content_generation","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Zia Vision validates uploaded images, performs character recognition and detects duplicates; the generative side creates modules, reports and workflows from natural-language descriptions, and Zia produces data enrichment from web sources and product recommendations with optimal contact timing. Vision is image validation and OCR, not facial recognition — do not classify it as biometric on the vendor's description alone, but do ask what image types are actually being processed, because ID-document or photo verification would change the answer.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Zoho CRM is horizontal, so typical_domains is empty. Escalation paths: lead and churn scoring used to gate access to an essential private service is Annex III point 5(b)/(c); Zia data enrichment scraping web sources into contact records is a GDPR lawful-basis problem rather than an AI Act one, but it belongs in the same report. On the sentiment feature, be precise: Art. 5(1)(f) prohibits emotion recognition systems in the workplace and in education, and Art. 3(39) defines those as inferring emotions from BIOMETRIC data. Zia's sentiment runs on customers' written and spoken words in a sales context, so it is neither workplace-directed nor, for the email path, biometric-derived — it is not prohibited. It would become an Art. 5 question if Zoho's call analysis were repurposed to infer an employee's emotional state from voice characteristics, so confirm the direction of analysis before writing either conclusion.","docs_url":"https://www.zoho.com/crm/zia/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"genesys-cloud-cx","name":"Genesys Cloud CX","vendor":"Genesys","category":"CCaaS / Contact Centre Platform","walkthrough_category":"service_desk","prevalence":3,"aliases":["genesys","genesys cloud","genesys cloud cx","purecloud","agent copilot","genesys ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Genesys Agent Copilot","capabilities":["summarization","content_generation","recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Real-time assistance to the human agent during an interaction: knowledge article and canned-response suggestions triggered by detected customer intent, AI-generated answers compiled from knowledge search, live voice transcription, automated interaction summaries and wrap-up code prediction, translated across 70+ languages. AI Experience tokens are included with every Genesys Cloud CX package and new customers get default token access, but Copilot itself has to be configured — intents, knowledge base and assist rules — before it does anything, and it consumes tokens per use. Low direct risk, but it is the component that produces the transcripts everything else scores.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Genesys AI Scoring / automated agent evaluations (Virtual Supervisor, Agent Auto-Complete forms)","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"This is the high-risk system hiding inside a customer-service licence. AI Scoring evaluates named agents against evaluation forms on up to 100% of interactions, assessing nine service fundamentals — compliance, EMPATHY, efficiency, greeting verification, identity authentication, active listening, personalisation, conversation closure and hold management — plus programme-level scoring for sales effectiveness, troubleshooting accuracy, inferred CSAT and churn risk. The Agent Auto-Complete form type SUBMITS the evaluation automatically once every question has an AI-generated answer, i.e. with no human evaluator in the loop. Genesys itself recommends manually scoring at least 25 interactions to establish an agreement score of 85%+ before scaling, and reviewing low-confidence questions monthly. Requires quality management / WEM entitlement and consumes AI Experience tokens; capped at 50 AI scoring requests per agent per day.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Agent empathy analysis","capabilities":["emotion_recognition","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Scores each AGENT from -100 to +100 on their ability to relate to the customer's emotions, by classifying the agent's own language as empathetic (apologising, expressing understanding, appreciating patience) or unhelpful (impatience, rudeness, inappropriate language, failing to listen, lacking knowledge). Genesys markets it explicitly for tailoring training and coaching, integrating into quality assurance against predefined empathy standards, and recognising and rewarding high scorers. Marked active_partial because Genesys's June 2026 programme-level controls announcement states that sentiment and empathy analysis REMAIN ENABLED for existing and new programmes unless an administrator disables them — a genuine default-on for any tenant holding the required CX 3 / WEM / Speech and Text Analytics entitlement. Check Admin > Quality > Speech and Text Analytics, and the per-programme setting.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Customer sentiment analysis","capabilities":["emotion_recognition"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Classifies phrases in the interaction transcript as positive, negative or neutral to characterise the CUSTOMER's experience. Same June 2026 programme-level control and same default-on behaviour as empathy analysis; organisation-level settings take precedence and programme settings can only restrict, never expand. Requires Genesys Cloud CX 3 Digital, the WEM add-on and the Speech and Text Analytics upgrade. This one analyses the customer, not the worker, and is deliberately tagged with no employment domain — keeping the two straight is the whole point of splitting these into separate features.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Predictive Routing","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Part of Genesys AI alongside Copilot, Predictive Engagement and bots. It matches interactions to individual agents on predicted outcome, which in practice allocates work to named workers on the basis of their measured past behaviour and performance. Commonly bought as a pure efficiency feature and almost never assessed, but see the escalation note — task allocation is named explicitly in Annex III point 4(b). Requires configuration and consumes AI Experience tokens.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"Annex III point 4(b) covers AI intended to be used to make decisions affecting the terms of work-related relationships, to allocate tasks based on individual behaviour or personal traits, or to MONITOR AND EVALUATE the performance and behaviour of persons in such relationships. AI Scoring, agent empathy analysis and Predictive Routing all land inside it — automated agent evaluation is high-risk regardless of the fact that it arrived bundled with a contact-centre licence, and the Agent Auto-Complete form type removes the human evaluator entirely, which makes the Art. 26(2) human-oversight assignment a hard requirement rather than good practice. On Art. 5(1)(f): the prohibition on emotion recognition in the workplace bites only on 'emotion recognition systems' as defined in Art. 3(39), i.e. inferring emotions from BIOMETRIC data. Genesys publishes an explicit position on this — it states its customer sentiment analysis and Agent Empathy Analysis operate on the language used in transcripts 'without using biometric data' and therefore do not fall under the restriction. That argument has real footing for transcript-word analysis. It does NOT extend to any feature that reads acoustic properties of the agent's voice (tone, pitch, prosody), which is biometric-derived and which the Commission's guidelines on prohibited practices treat as in scope. So the boundary a consultant must establish, in writing, is whether the model reads words or reads the waveform. Finally, Art. 50(1) applies to Genesys bots that converse with customers directly, and Art. 26(7) requires informing worker representatives before putting agent-evaluation AI into service at a workplace.","docs_url":"https://help.genesys.cloud/faqs/how-does-the-eu-emotion-ai-ban-affect-customer-sentiment-analysis-and-agent-empathy-analysis/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"nice-cxone-mpower","name":"NiCE CXone Mpower","vendor":"NiCE","category":"CCaaS / Workforce Engagement","walkthrough_category":"service_desk","prevalence":3,"aliases":["nice","nice cxone","cxone","incontact","enlighten","cxone mpower","nice enlighten ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"CXone Mpower Quality Management — Auto Score","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"NiCE markets 'Auto Score for 100% unbiased evaluation coverage': interactions are scored automatically at scale by LLMs powered by NiCE AI models, across calls, chat, email, social and CRM tickets, producing AI-generated summaries and recommendations that pinpoint an individual agent's strengths, skill gaps and next-best coaching actions. Companion Copilot for Supervisors surfaces the same material to the manager. This is a workforce evaluation system sold inside a customer-experience platform. It sits in the WEM part of the suite and is licensed separately from core CXone seats, so a customer running voice-only ACD will not have it — confirm before recording state.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Enlighten AI behavioural models (Customer Satisfaction and Sales Effectiveness datasets)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","emotion_recognition"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"NiCE's documentation describes conversational datasets that score named agents on behaviours: the Customer Satisfaction dataset tracks ten positive behaviours plus one negative, and the Sales Effectiveness dataset ten sales metrics plus an overall score, viewable per interaction or across all interactions. Release notes confirm 'agent Enlighten behaviors' require an Enlighten CSAT licence and that agent sentiment, customer sentiment and Enlighten sentiment appear together in a single metrics view. Tagged emotion_recognition because agent sentiment is an inferred affective state attributed to a worker — see the escalation note for why the acoustic-versus-text question decides whether that is Annex III or Art. 5.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"CXone Mpower Copilot for Agents","capabilities":["summarization","content_generation","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Real-time assistance to the human agent — knowledge retrieval, suggested responses, after-call summarisation. Sold as a licensed component of the Mpower suite rather than included in a base seat. Benign in itself, but it produces the transcripts and summaries that Auto Score and the Enlighten behavioural models then evaluate the agent against, so it should be documented as part of the same data chain in the deployer's Art. 26 records.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"CXone Mpower Agents (Autopilot / AI Studio)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","automated_decision_making","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Launched June 2025: CXone Mpower identifies automation opportunities across front, middle and back office and generates AI agents from outcome-based no-code prompts in Mpower AI Studio, with access to CXone APIs, Knowledge, Experience Memory, channels and Enlighten models. These agents handle customer interactions end to end and take fulfilment actions, so they make decisions with effects on the customer without a human in the loop — Art. 50(1) disclosure applies, and the no-code generation route means new AI systems can enter production without a procurement or change-control event.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"Auto Score and the Enlighten agent-behaviour models are Annex III point 4(b) systems — monitoring and evaluating the performance and behaviour of persons in a work-related relationship, and generating coaching and skill-gap findings that feed development and, in most contact centres, bonus and retention decisions. Buying CXone as a 'customer experience platform' does not change that; the deployer obligations in Art. 26 attach to the QM module. Practical requirements: assign competent human oversight over AI-generated evaluations (Art. 26(2)), keep the automatically generated logs (Art. 26(6)), and inform workers and their representatives before putting it into service (Art. 26(7)). On Art. 5(1)(f): NiCE surfaces 'agent sentiment' as a metric alongside customer sentiment. If that agent sentiment is inferred from acoustic voice features it is emotion recognition from biometric data about a worker in the workplace and is PROHIBITED, not merely high-risk — there is no legitimate-interest route around it and the only carve-outs are medical and safety reasons. If it is derived purely from the words in the transcript it falls outside Art. 3(39) and stays in the Annex III bucket. NiCE's public pages do not state which, so this must be answered by the vendor in writing before a Nordic client's contact centre is signed off. Note also the naming drift: Enlighten remains the model family but the platform is now CXone Mpower, and older assessments referencing 'NICE inContact' or plain 'CXone' describe the same estate.","docs_url":"https://www.nice.com/products/quality-management","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"puzzel","name":"Puzzel Contact Centre","vendor":"Puzzel","category":"CCaaS (Nordic)","walkthrough_category":"service_desk","prevalence":3,"aliases":["puzzel","puzzel contact centre","puzzel co-pilot","intelecom","puzzel ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Puzzel Co-Pilot","capabilities":["summarization","content_generation","recommendation_profiling","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Listens to live calls, gives the agent a pre-call summary, surfaces customer history, recommends next best actions in real time against detected caller intent, and answers ad-hoc questions mid-call from the knowledge base. Puzzel states plainly that Co-Pilot is an add-on to the Contact Centre solution, so a standard Puzzel tenant does not have it. Puzzel positions European hosting and GDPR alignment as a differentiator, which is often the reason Nordic public-sector buyers choose it over US CCaaS — worth capturing in the report as a mitigating fact.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Live Summary","capabilities":["summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Real-time call summarisation that removes manual after-call note-taking, launched alongside Co-Pilot. It transcribes and condenses the conversation between a named agent and an identified customer. Low intrinsic risk, but the transcript store it creates is the substrate for quality scoring later, and in a Nordic works-council context the existence of full call transcripts attributable to individual agents is itself a consultation topic.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Virtual Agent Suite (chat, voice and email AI agents)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"AI agents that handle customer enquiries end to end across chat, voice and email rather than deflecting to a human. Because they resolve rather than triage, they take actions affecting the customer autonomously. Art. 50(1) disclosure applies on every channel where the agent speaks to a natural person, including the voice channel where it is easiest to omit.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Conversational Intelligence","capabilities":["emotion_recognition","document_analysis","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Automatically tags, transcribes and analyses interactions across voice and digital channels to identify trends and improve call handling, using NLP-based sentiment analysis. Puzzel also markets automated customer sentiment inside Agent Assist so agents can respond more empathetically. The analysed subject on the pages I could verify is the CUSTOMER, which is why no employment domain is tagged here — but Puzzel's public documentation does not state whether the same models also produce an agent-side sentiment score, and that is a direct question for the vendor.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Puzzel Quality Management","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Puzzel describes it as AI that automatically scores interactions and highlights coaching opportunities. That means an AI system producing scores attributable to individual agents and feeding coaching decisions — the same workforce-evaluation pattern as Genesys and NiCE, in a smaller Nordic package that clients tend not to think of as an 'AI system' at all. Puzzel does not publish the scoring dimensions or whether human sign-off is required on an AI-generated score, so both need to be established during the assessment.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"Quality Management's automatic scoring of individual agents is Annex III point 4(b) — monitoring and evaluating the performance and behaviour of persons in a work-related relationship — with the usual deployer duties: human oversight over the score (Art. 26(2)), log retention (Art. 26(6)), and informing workers' representatives before the system goes live (Art. 26(7)), which in Norway and Sweden overlaps with existing co-determination duties and is usually the fastest way to get the client's attention. Sentiment analysis on the customer side is NOT within Art. 5(1)(f): the prohibition covers emotion recognition in the workplace and education, and its Art. 3(39) definition requires inference from biometric data. The prohibition would engage only if Puzzel's models were turned on the agent's own voice characteristics — establish in writing whether any agent-side sentiment or tone metric exists before clearing this. Everything else here is Art. 50 transparency for the virtual agents, and standard GDPR call-recording and transcript-retention analysis. Puzzel's European hosting reduces transfer risk but has no bearing on the AI Act classification.","docs_url":"https://www.puzzel.com/ai-solutions","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"five9","name":"Five9 Intelligent CX Platform","vendor":"Five9","category":"CCaaS / Contact Centre Platform","walkthrough_category":"service_desk","prevalence":2,"aliases":["five9","five 9","five9 genius","five9 agent assist","five9 ai agents"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Five9 Agentic Quality Management","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Analyses up to 100% of interactions and produces competency-based intelligence used for routing and coaching decisions. It explicitly benchmarks each individual agent against top performers, identifies performance gaps, generates 'Knowledge AI Gaps' recommendations based on what top performers do, and alerts supervisors in real time for targeted intervention while a call is still running. Ranking a named worker against peers and then routing work on the result is the sharpest employment exposure in this group. Five9 positions it alongside Workforce Engagement Management; the page does not spell out the licensing split, so establish which SKUs the client actually holds.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Five9 AI Insights","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Generative AI analytics giving CX leaders customisable metrics against their own goals — win-loss reasons, upsell success rates, product feedback and, in Five9's own list of examples, 'agent comprehension'. The headline framing is customer satisfaction, which is why no employment domain is tagged by default. But custom metrics are defined by the customer, and the moment one of them is written against agent behaviour the feature becomes a workforce-evaluation system without any change to the licence. Ask to see the actual list of custom metrics configured in the tenant; do not accept the product description.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Five9 AI Agents","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Autonomous voice and digital agents that handle customer interactions end to end. Art. 50(1) requires the customer to be told they are dealing with an AI system unless it is obvious to a reasonably well-informed person — and with current voice quality it is not obvious. Separately licensed.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Five9 Agent Assist","capabilities":["summarization","content_generation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Real-time guidance and after-call summarisation for the human agent, with dynamic guidance tailored to the agent's assessed competency level. Note that 'tailored to competency level' means the assist tool is already consuming a per-agent capability assessment — that assessment, not the assist itself, is the part that needs classifying.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"Agentic Quality Management is an Annex III point 4(b) system on two separate grounds: it evaluates the performance and behaviour of named workers, AND it feeds routing decisions, which is task allocation based on individual behaviour and personal traits — 4(b) names both. Deployer duties under Art. 26 apply, including human oversight of the comparative ranking, and Art. 26(7) worker-representative information before go-live. I found no documented emotion or affect inference about AGENTS in Five9's published material, so I have not tagged emotion_recognition anywhere in this entry and Art. 5(1)(f) is not raised — if a client reports an agent-sentiment dashboard, treat that as new information and re-open the question rather than assuming it is the same as the competency scoring. Five9's real-time supervisor alerting during a live call is worth a separate line in any works-council discussion: continuous real-time monitoring of a named worker is a proportionality question under GDPR Art. 6 and national labour law even where the AI Act tier is settled. Prevalence 2: dominant in the US, a minority choice in European estates.","docs_url":"https://www.five9.com/products/capabilities/contact-center-agentic-management-monitoring-tools","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"talkdesk","name":"Talkdesk","vendor":"Talkdesk","category":"CCaaS / Contact Centre Platform","walkthrough_category":"service_desk","prevalence":2,"aliases":["talkdesk","talkdesk copilot","talkdesk autopilot","talkdesk cx cloud"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Talkdesk Quality Management (AI evaluation and scoring)","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling","emotion_recognition","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Talkdesk describes AI-driven automation that evaluates 100% of interactions with 'fair, consistent scoring at scale' and surfaces targeted coaching opportunities, combining keyword-based scoring with generative AI that understands TONE, sentiment and intent. Scorecards, time-stamped supervisor annotations and coaching assignment are built in. It is part of the Workforce Engagement Management suite, licensed separately from core Talkdesk seats. The word 'tone' is doing a lot of work here and Talkdesk does not say whether it means word choice or vocal delivery — that ambiguity is the single most important thing to resolve before signing this off in an EU deployment.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Talkdesk Copilot (agentic)","capabilities":["summarization","content_generation","recommendation_profiling","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Upgraded to agentic reasoning: it interprets conversation context, searches multiple knowledge sources and generates answers for the human agent within seconds, plus after-call summarisation. Assistive rather than evaluative, so no employment tag on this feature — but the interaction data and summaries it produces are what Quality Management then scores the agent on, and the two are usually bought together.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Talkdesk Autopilot (AI virtual agents)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Voice and digital AI agents that resolve customer requests end to end, with multi-agent orchestration for complex cases, automatic language detection across five languages in a single call, 27 supported languages and three voice types including Neural HD. Because it is a synthetic voice conducting a real conversation, Art. 50(1) disclosure obligations are live from the first second of the call, and the natural-sounding Neural HD voices make omission more consequential, not less.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"Quality Management's automated evaluation of individual agents is Annex III point 4(b) and carries the full deployer set — human oversight of AI scores, log keeping, and worker-representative notification under Art. 26(7). The critical unresolved question is Art. 5(1)(f). Talkdesk states its generative AI 'understands tone', and a model that infers an agent's emotional state from acoustic voice features (pitch, prosody, speech rate) during their working day is an emotion recognition system in the area of work under Art. 3(39) and is PROHIBITED outright, with only medical and safety carve-outs; a model that infers tone from the words in the transcript is not caught by the definition and stays in Annex III. Talkdesk's public documentation does not distinguish the two, so do not record either conclusion — record the question and get the answer from Talkdesk in writing. Customer-side sentiment, which Talkdesk also performs, is outside Art. 5 entirely regardless of how it is derived, because the prohibition is scoped to workplace and education. Prevalence is set at 2 because Talkdesk is a strong product with a comparatively thin European mid-market footprint next to Genesys and the Nordic incumbents.","docs_url":"https://www.talkdesk.com/cloud-contact-center/wem/quality-management/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"basware","name":"Basware AP Automation","vendor":"Basware","category":"Accounts Payable Automation / e-Invoicing","walkthrough_category":"erp_finance","prevalence":3,"aliases":["basware","invoiceai","smartpdf","smartcoding","basware ap"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"InvoiceAI (PO and non-PO invoice automation)","capabilities":["document_analysis","basic_automation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Basware's agentic AP automation layer, with separate variants for PO-backed and non-PO invoices. It processes the invoice end to end where confidence allows, routing only exceptions to a human. Sold as part of the AP Automation suite rather than a standalone purchase, but has to be configured per entity and per invoice flow before it does anything. The deployer question is where the confidence threshold sits and what proportion of invoices are actually posted with no human review.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"SmartPDF","capabilities":["document_analysis"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Machine reading of inbound PDF invoices at the ingestion point, which Basware quotes at 92%+ automation. Part of the AP automation capability set. Pure document extraction — no person is profiled, scored or decided about — so this belongs in the catalogue as an assessed-and-cleared item rather than a risk.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"SmartCoding","capabilities":["document_analysis","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Machine-learning coding of non-PO invoices — predicting GL account, cost centre and dimensions from historical posting behaviour — which Basware quotes at up to 89% touchless. It learns from how the organisation's own AP staff coded past invoices, so the model encodes individual coders' habits; that is a data-lineage point worth noting but not a workforce-evaluation one, because nothing is scored back to the individual.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AP Protect and Basware Guardian for AP","capabilities":["document_analysis","automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Basware lists these under AP Assurance as separate solutions alongside AP Audit & Recovery, covering fraud and error prevention and compliance monitoring across the AP process. Basware's published material frames the analysis at transaction and supplier level. I have NOT tagged an employment domain, because I could not verify any vendor statement that these modules flag or profile the organisation's own employees or approvers — see the escalation note for why that distinction is the one to test.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"AP automation is not Annex III: detecting a duplicate invoice or a manipulated supplier bank detail is a decision about a document and a counterparty organisation, not about a natural person's rights. Two boundaries decide whether that stays true in a given deployment. First, if AP Protect or Guardian is configured to surface named internal approvers or AP clerks as anomalous — 'this approver releases an unusual pattern of invoices' — then the system is evaluating the behaviour of a worker and falls under Annex III point 4(b), with all the Art. 26 duties, and in practice also triggers works-council consultation in Nordic jurisdictions. Basware's public pages describe transactional assurance, so do not assert this: put it as a direct configuration question. Second, where a supplier is a sole trader or a natural person, an automated fraud flag that blocks their payment engages GDPR Art. 22 on solely automated decisions with legal or similarly significant effects, independently of the AI Act. Basware's e-invoicing compliance features are regulatory plumbing (national mandates, Peppol) and carry no AI Act relevance at all — say so explicitly, so the client can stop worrying about them.","docs_url":"https://www.basware.com/en/ai-hub","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"fortnox","name":"Fortnox","vendor":"Fortnox","category":"SME Accounting and Payroll (Sweden)","walkthrough_category":"erp_finance","prevalence":3,"aliases":["fortnox","fortnox bokföring","fortnox bokforing","fortnox lön","fakturatolkning"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Fakturatolkning (automatic supplier-invoice interpretation with posting proposal)","capabilities":["document_analysis","basic_automation"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Fortnox states on its accounting-software page that however an invoice arrives, the user does not fill in supplier, amount or date manually — Fortnox does it — and that the user always receives a proposal for how the invoice should be posted. This is machine reading plus a learned coding suggestion, shipped as part of the subscription rather than a paid add-on, which is why it is active_partial rather than not_licensed. It is genuinely present in tens of thousands of Swedish SMEs whose owners would not describe themselves as using AI at all. No decision about a natural person is made.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Automatic bookkeeping of bank and tax-account transactions","capabilities":["basic_automation","document_analysis"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Fortnox describes 'självgående bokföring' — most transactions on the connected bank account and Skattekonto are posted automatically without user intervention. Included in the subscription, but it only operates once the bank feed is connected and the matching rules are in place, so real-world coverage varies a lot between clients. The risk conversation here is accounting accuracy and the Bokföringslagen audit trail, not the AI Act.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Nothing in Fortnox's bookkeeping automation is Annex III — posting a ledger entry is a decision about a transaction, not about a person, and there is no profiling, emotion inference or biometric processing anywhere in it. Include Fortnox in a catalogue anyway, because its Swedish prevalence means it appears in almost every Nordic SME estate and clients need a documented 'assessed, not high-risk' answer rather than a gap. Two things could change that and both need checking rather than assuming: (1) Fortnox Lön — if any AI is applied to payroll or absence data in a way that evaluates an employee, that is Annex III point 4(b); (2) Fortnox Finansiering, the invoice-financing and payments arm — if an automated model assesses a customer's or a sole trader's creditworthiness there, that is Annex III point 5(b), the credit-scoring entry, and it is squarely high-risk. I found no vendor documentation stating that AI is used for either, so neither is recorded as a feature above; they are listed here as questions for the system owner, not findings.","docs_url":"https://www.fortnox.se/produkt/bokforingsprogram","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"medius","name":"Medius","vendor":"Medius","category":"Accounts Payable Automation / Spend Management","walkthrough_category":"erp_finance","prevalence":3,"aliases":["medius","mediusflow","medius ap","medius copilot","medius detect","medius expense"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI Receipt Verification (Medius Expense)","capabilities":["document_analysis","automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Medius states this flags potentially AI-GENERATED receipts in expense reports and alerts the approver to investigate before processing. This is the one feature in the whole finance group that points at an employee rather than a supplier: the subject of the assessment is a member of staff, the output is effectively a suspicion of fabricated evidence, and the consequence — a challenged or rejected claim, or a disciplinary referral — lands on that individual. It is also a model working on a hard problem with a real false-positive rate. Part of the Medius Expense capability set, not the core AP module, so a Medius AP-only customer will not have it.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Fraud & Risk Detection (Medius Detect)","capabilities":["document_analysis","automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Anomaly detection across the invoice lifecycle covering duplicate invoices, vendor fraud, incorrect amounts, unapproved suppliers, invoice date manipulation and changes to supplier payment details, with inline alerts to AP managers and a risk dashboard Medius calls Fire Station. Medius states explicitly that it requires Medius Accounts Payable Automation, i.e. it is an add-on module on top of the AP suite. Medius's own description is transaction- and supplier-focused, not employee-focused — I checked specifically and found no documented employee-behaviour profiling here, which is why the employment tag sits on receipt verification and not on this feature.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"SmartFlow (non-PO coding, tax and approver prediction)","capabilities":["document_analysis","recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Medius states SmartFlow auto-fills coding, tax and APPROVER values for non-PO invoices with 95% precision after just two invoices, enabling straight-through processing. Worth noting that the predicted value includes which named person should approve the invoice — a small piece of automated task allocation to an identified worker. On its own this is trivial and nowhere near Annex III, but it is the kind of thing that should appear in the inventory rather than being invisible.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Medius Capture","capabilities":["document_analysis"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Data extraction and validation from invoices, trained on hundreds of millions of invoices since 2016, with Medius claiming a fully touchless capture rate. Medius also states it captures human-in-the-loop correction events as a decade-long training dataset — that is a useful thing to know when a client asks what happens to their data: their AP team's corrections improve a shared model. Worth confirming the contractual position on that in the client's own agreement.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Medius Copilot","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"An assistant that guides invoice approvers through the approval process with pre-defined prompts and free-text answers to support their approval decision. It advises the human approver rather than approving on its own, so the accountability chain is intact — but if approvers routinely accept its recommendation, the practical human oversight is thinner than the org chart suggests, which is exactly the Art. 26(2) 'automation bias' point where a related system is high-risk.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Supplier Conversations","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"An autonomous agent that answers supplier email enquiries about invoice and payment status around the clock without AP team involvement. The correspondents are natural persons working at supplier organisations, and they are being replied to by an AI system in a channel where they have no reason to expect one — this is a live Art. 50(1) transparency obligation that is very easy to miss because it is framed internally as 'reducing AP workload' rather than as a chatbot.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"typical_domains is left empty on purpose: Medius is bought as AP automation and the great majority of its AI acts on invoices and suppliers, so tagging the whole product as employment would over-warn every customer. The specific escalation is AI Receipt Verification in Medius Expense. Annex III point 4(b) covers AI used to monitor and evaluate the behaviour of persons in a work-related relationship, and a model that examines an employee's submitted receipts and flags them as probably fabricated is evaluating that employee's conduct with a directly adverse potential consequence. If it is used only as an advisory prompt to an approver who independently reviews the underlying claim, the Art. 6(3) filter may apply — a system performing a narrow procedural task or preparatory input that does not materially influence the outcome is not high-risk — but that exemption has to be documented and the deployer has to be able to show the approver genuinely exercises judgement. In practice, if a flag reliably leads to rejection, the filter fails and the system is high-risk. Nothing here engages Art. 5: there is no emotion inference and no biometric processing anywhere in the Medius stack. For suppliers who are natural persons, an automated fraud flag that blocks payment is also a GDPR Art. 22 question. Finally, the Supplier Conversations agent needs an Art. 50(1) disclosure in its email footer, which is a five-minute fix and a good early win in an engagement.","docs_url":"https://www.medius.com/ai-innovation/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"oracle-netsuite","name":"Oracle NetSuite","vendor":"Oracle","category":"Cloud ERP (mid-market)","walkthrough_category":"erp_finance","prevalence":3,"aliases":["netsuite","oracle netsuite","net suite","suitepeople","text enhance"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"NetSuite Text Enhance","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Generative text embedded directly in NetSuite record fields — item descriptions, customer-facing emails and letters, and free-text fields across the suite — drafted from surrounding record context, powered by Oracle Cloud Infrastructure Generative AI. Oracle positions it as included with NetSuite rather than a separate SKU, but a feature must be switched on and terms accepted before it appears, hence available_not_enabled. IMPORTANT CAVEAT ON SOURCING: netsuite.com and oracle.com block automated retrieval, so I could not confirm the current field coverage or enablement path against Oracle's own live pages in this pass — treat the capability as real but verify the specifics in the tenant's Enable Features page and the release notes for the account's version before quoting them to a client.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"NetSuite Bill Capture","capabilities":["document_analysis","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Machine-reading of inbound vendor bills — PDF and scanned documents dropped into a NetSuite inbox — extracting vendor, amounts, dates and lines and pre-populating a vendor bill record for review. Standard NetSuite functionality on recent versions rather than a paid add-on, but enabled per account and configured per subsidiary. Same sourcing caveat as above. No decision about a natural person is involved; the compliance interest is accuracy and the audit trail on what a human actually reviewed before posting.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Deliberately empty typical_domains: NetSuite is bought as finance and operations software and the finance AI makes no decision about a person. The escalation nobody expects is SuitePeople, NetSuite's own HR and payroll module. If Text Enhance is exposed on SuitePeople records — drafting job requisitions, or generating narrative text in performance review fields — then the same 'included' AI feature is operating inside recruitment and employee-evaluation processes, which is Annex III point 4(a) and 4(b), with no separate purchase to flag it. Ask specifically whether SuitePeople is licensed and whether Text Enhance appears on those records, rather than asking whether the client 'uses AI in HR'. Second escalation: NetSuite deployed by a lender, leasing company or insurer where AI-assisted output informs a creditworthiness or pricing decision reaches Annex III point 5(b)/(c). Because Oracle blocks automated documentation retrieval, this entry is deliberately thinner than NetSuite's actual AI surface — do not read the two-feature list as exhaustive.","docs_url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/index.html","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"qlik","name":"Qlik Cloud Analytics","vendor":"Qlik","category":"Business Intelligence / Data Integration","walkthrough_category":"analytics","prevalence":4,"aliases":["qlik","qlik sense","qlikview","qlik cloud","qlik answers","insight advisor","qlik automl","qlik predict"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Qlik Answers","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Qlik's agentic AI assistant, launched February 2026, answering questions from governed data and unstructured content through a combination of assistants and agents. Qlik states that a tenant runs either Qlik Answers or Insight Advisor, not both — choosing Answers makes it the primary way users interact with analytics, which is a migration decision, not a feature toggle. Requires tenant-level activation and content sources to be configured before it returns anything.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Insight Advisor","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"The natural-language assistant built into Qlik Sense since 2019 — search-based analysis, automatic chart generation and associative insight suggestions. Because it has shipped inside Qlik Sense for years, it is present and usable in most existing Nordic Qlik estates without anyone having made an AI purchasing decision, which is exactly the kind of thing missing from a first-pass AI inventory. It is superseded per-tenant if the customer moves to Qlik Answers.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Qlik Predict (formerly Qlik AutoML)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The escalation trigger in any Qlik estate. A no-code AutoML service aimed explicitly at analytics teams with no machine-learning background: supply historical data, configure an experiment, and Qlik evaluates algorithms and produces a deployed model with explainability output. The Predict Agent rolled out through Q2 2026. Because it needs no data scientist, business units build person-scoring models in it without any model-governance process — HR building an attrition model on employee data is the classic case. Separately licensed, so its presence is a deliberate purchase and easy to confirm.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Qlik as a visualisation and NL-query platform is not high risk; the domain depends entirely on the loaded data. Insight Advisor and Qlik Answers raise Art. 50 transparency questions only — users must be able to tell that an answer is machine-generated, and Answers grounding on unstructured content makes provenance of the answer a governance issue. The concrete escalation is **Qlik Predict**: name the model, not the platform. A Predict model scoring employees for attrition, performance or promotion is Annex III point 4(a)-(b); scoring loan applicants for default risk is point 5(b); life or health insurance risk scoring is point 5(c); scoring students for progression or admission is point 3; scoring benefit claimants for eligibility or fraud is point 5(a). Critically, a customer who builds the model in Qlik Predict and puts it into service is the **provider** of that high-risk system under Art. 16 and Art. 25, not merely a deployer — full quality management, technical documentation, conformity assessment and registration, which is a far heavier obligation than most Qlik owners expect. Qlik markets HR and People Analytics as a solution area, so a consultant should ask directly whether any Predict experiment has been run on HR data. A Predict model forecasting demand, stock or machine failure stays outside Annex III entirely.","docs_url":"https://www.qlik.com/us/products/qlik-predict","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"tableau","name":"Tableau","vendor":"Salesforce","category":"Business Intelligence / Data Visualisation","walkthrough_category":"analytics","prevalence":4,"aliases":["tableau","tableau cloud","tableau server","tableau pulse","tableau next","tableau agent","einstein copilot for tableau","einstein discovery"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Tableau Pulse","capabilities":["summarization","natural_language_query","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Inverts the dashboard model: instead of users going to a dashboard, Pulse monitors defined metrics, detects anomalies and pushes AI-written insight digests to people by email and Slack. Point-in-time metrics reached GA on Tableau Cloud in 2026. A site admin enables Tableau AI and the metric layer before anything is generated. The compliance point is distribution — Pulse narratives reach executives who never open the underlying view and cannot see what was excluded, so the written insight becomes the decision input.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Tableau Agent (formerly Einstein Copilot for Tableau)","capabilities":["natural_language_query","content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"In-product assistant that answers questions in natural language, drafts calculations and builds visualisations from a description. Renamed from Einstein Copilot for Tableau, so older customer documentation and AI registers will use the old name. Gated behind Tableau+ or the equivalent AI entitlement rather than included with a standard Creator licence — a plain Tableau Cloud tenant will not have it.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Tableau Next","capabilities":["natural_language_query","basic_automation","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The Salesforce-platform-native agentic analytics product, built on Agentforce and the Data Cloud semantic layer, positioned by Salesforce as an addition to the estate rather than a replacement for Tableau Cloud or Server. Semantic models can be managed from VS Code, Cursor or Agentforce Vibes, and MCP support lets a customer's own LLM query governed Tableau data. Separate licensing and, in practice, a separate project.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Einstein Discovery in Tableau","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"This is the feature that decides whether a Tableau deployment is high risk. Einstein Discovery trains predictive models and surfaces row-level scores and prescriptive recommendations inside a Tableau dashboard — so a viz that looks like a report is in fact scoring each row and telling a manager what to do about it. When the rows are employees, applicants, students or borrowers, the dashboard is the user interface of a scoring system. Requires a CRM Analytics / Einstein Discovery entitlement and someone to have built and deployed a model; it is absent from a visualisation-only deployment.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Tableau is domain-neutral: the risk tier is set entirely by what data the workbook connects to and whether a model is scoring people. Natural-language querying via Tableau Agent and Pulse narratives raise Art. 50 transparency questions — users must understand that the insight text is machine-generated — but do not by themselves make anything high risk. The escalation trigger to name is **Einstein Discovery**: the moment an Einstein Discovery model scores individual people and that score is visible to a decision-maker, classify by who the people are. Employees or job applicants scored for attrition, promotion or performance puts it in Annex III point 4(a)-(b). Borrowers or applicants scored for default risk puts it in point 5(b); life or health insurance risk and pricing in point 5(c); students scored for admission, progression or outcomes in point 3. Public-sector benefit eligibility is point 5(a). Two boundaries that keep a Tableau estate out of scope: an aggregate dashboard with no row-level person score is not high risk however sensitive the source data, and a Pulse anomaly alert on headcount or attrition rate is monitoring, not individual evaluation. Where Einstein Discovery is in play, also check who trained the model — a customer that built it themselves is a provider under Art. 25(1) and inherits the full Chapter III obligations, not just the deployer duties in Art. 26.","docs_url":"https://www.tableau.com/products/new-features","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"databricks","name":"Databricks Data Intelligence Platform","vendor":"Databricks, Inc.","category":"Lakehouse / Data and AI Platform","walkthrough_category":"analytics","prevalence":3,"aliases":["databricks","ai/bi genie","genie","genie one","mosaic ai","unity catalog","lakehouse"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI/BI Genie (Genie One, Genie Agents, Genie Code)","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization","content_generation"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Genie is built natively into the platform rather than sold as a separate SKU, and Databricks states Genie One and Genie Agents remain free of charge through 31 January 2027, with Genie Code on pay-as-you-go from 8 July 2026. Genie One is the business-user surface for asking data questions in natural language; Genie Agents are the configured environments where a data team pins trusted datasets, sample queries, metrics, business rules and verified answers; Genie Code is the developer assistant. All responses are governed through Unity Catalog. Genie One reached Google Sheets and Excel in August 2026, which puts natural-language querying of the lakehouse into the tools finance and HR actually use. Account admins control budgets and cost, not access — so the practical control is which Genie Agents exist and what they point at.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Mosaic AI","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making","content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"The model development, fine-tuning, serving, evaluation and monitoring stack, absorbed from the MosaicML acquisition. This is where a customer builds and puts into service their own models rather than consuming a vendor's. That distinction is the whole compliance story for Databricks: nothing Databricks ships scores a person, but Mosaic AI is the toolchain in which the customer builds the thing that does. Present on the platform but requires a team to actually train and deploy something.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Databricks is a platform with no inherent Annex III domain — the tier depends on which models are trained and which data a Genie Agent exposes. Two boundaries worth being exact about. On Genie: natural-language querying is an Art. 50 transparency matter, users must know answers are machine-generated, and the real control is the scope of each Genie Agent. A Genie Agent pointed at an HR schema effectively hands plain-language interrogation of employee data to anyone with workspace access — and now to anyone with Excel. Unity Catalog row and column controls are the mitigation; verify they are actually applied rather than assumed. On Mosaic AI: this is the strongest provider-versus-deployer case in the analytics category. A customer who trains a scoring model in Mosaic AI and puts it into service under their own name becomes the **provider** of a high-risk AI system under Art. 16, carrying the full Chapter III obligations — risk management system, data governance, technical documentation, logging, human oversight design, conformity assessment and EU database registration — which is an order of magnitude beyond the Art. 26 duties a customer of a packaged product takes on. Classify by the scored population: employees or candidates, Annex III point 4; credit applicants, point 5(b); life or health insurance risk and pricing, point 5(c); students, point 3; benefit claimants including fraud scoring, point 5(a); emergency call triage or dispatch, point 5(d). A demand-forecasting or predictive-maintenance model built in exactly the same tooling is out of scope, so the question to ask is never 'do you use Mosaic AI' but 'what is a row in your training data'.","docs_url":"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"google-looker","name":"Looker","vendor":"Google Cloud","category":"Business Intelligence / Semantic Layer","walkthrough_category":"analytics","prevalence":3,"aliases":["looker","google looker","looker studio","gemini in looker","conversational analytics","lookml"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Conversational Analytics in Looker","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Chat-with-your-data powered by Gemini and grounded in the LookML semantic layer, so answers inherit the governed metric definitions rather than being free-form text-to-SQL. An administrator must switch on the 'Gemini in Looker' setting and grant user permissions; as of 26 January 2026 it no longer requires an allowlist for Looker data sources. Included in Looker pricing tiers rather than sold separately. For EU customers, Looker (Google Cloud core) offers localised processing so queries are handled inside the EU — get that on the record early, because it removes a transfer argument later.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Explore data agents and dashboard agents","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Reusable agents configured with custom instructions, a business glossary and persona framing, connected to up to five Explores, shareable across the organisation or publishable to other applications. Dashboard agents (preview) query a dashboard and its underlying model and inherit the dashboard's permission model. The governance point is that the custom instructions are an uncontrolled prompt layer — whoever writes them shapes every answer the organisation receives, and there is usually no review process for them.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Advanced Analytics (Python execution in Conversational Analytics)","capabilities":["natural_language_query","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Lets the agent run generated Python against query results for statistical work beyond SQL, enabled as a separate preview feature. This is where an ad-hoc conversational question can quietly become a model — a forecast or a segmentation produced in a chat window, with no artefact anyone governs. Token usage and engagement are visible through System Activity dashboards once an admin turns the corresponding preview on.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Looker is a semantic layer over whatever the customer put in BigQuery, so it has no inherent Annex III domain. Conversational Analytics raises Art. 50 transparency only: users must understand answers are machine-generated, and the LookML grounding is a real mitigation worth crediting because it constrains the model to governed definitions. Escalation happens through the data, not the feature. The specific thing to look for is an Explore built on the output of a **BigQuery ML model** — Looker itself does not train models, but it is a common front end for BQML scores, and a dashboard that ranks employees by predicted attrition or applicants by predicted performance makes the combined BQML-plus-Looker deployment a high-risk system under Annex III point 4, with the customer as provider because they trained it. Point 5(b) applies the same way to BQML credit models surfaced in Looker, point 3 to student outcome models, point 5(a) to benefit-eligibility models in a public-sector tenant. Advanced Analytics deserves a separate question: Python generated in a chat can produce a person-level score that never passes through any model-review process. Aggregate reporting, however sensitive the source, remains outside Annex III.","docs_url":"https://docs.cloud.google.com/looker/docs/conversational-analytics-overview","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"sas-viya","name":"SAS Viya","vendor":"SAS Institute","category":"Advanced Analytics / Statistical Modelling","walkthrough_category":"analytics","prevalence":3,"aliases":["sas","sas viya","viya","sas viya copilot","model studio","sas visual analytics","sas visual investigator"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"SAS Viya Copilot","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"active","notes":"A family of assistants embedded across the analytics lifecycle, and unusually for an enterprise platform SAS states plainly that it is included at no extra cost as part of SAS Viya 4, SAS Viya for Learners, SAS Visual Analytics and SAS Visual Statistics, with no additional setup or purchase required. It generates and explains SAS and Python code and reusable macros in Viya and VS Code, guides model pipeline construction in SAS Model Studio with suggested nodes and explanations, answers SAS Model Manager questions in natural language, drives conversational dashboard creation in Visual Analytics, and supports discovery in SAS Data Governance and SAS Visual Investigator. SAS announced further industry-specific Copilot expansion in April 2026, including financial crime prevention in banking and supply chain planning in manufacturing. Because it is bundled, a Viya customer has generative AI in the estate whether or not they ever bought AI.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"SAS Model Studio pipelines (with Copilot guidance)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The visual model-building environment where scorecards and classification models are actually constructed, now with Copilot proposing pipeline nodes and explaining choices. Unlike the BI tools in this category, scoring people is not an edge case for SAS — it is the core use in Nordic banking, insurance and public administration, and has been for decades. Requires the relevant Viya modelling licence. Ask directly what is in production, because the answer is usually a credit scorecard or a fraud model, not a sales forecast.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"SAS Visual Investigator (with Copilot search and analysis)","capabilities":["document_analysis","recommendation_profiling","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Investigation and case management used for financial crime, AML and fraud, with Copilot accelerating search and analysis across case material. It builds networks and risk assessments around named individuals and generates alerts that trigger human investigation. The regulatory classification changes completely depending on whether the deployer is a bank meeting its AML obligations or a law-enforcement authority.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Left empty deliberately, but SAS is the one product in this category where the answer is usually 'yes' rather than 'it depends' — its customers buy it precisely to score people. Name the artefact, not the platform. A **Model Studio** credit scorecard evaluating creditworthiness or setting a credit score is Annex III point 5(b), with the customer as **provider** under Art. 16 because they built and deployed the model — full Chapter III obligations, conformity assessment and EU database registration, not merely the Art. 26 deployer duties. Life or health insurance risk assessment and pricing is point 5(c). Models scoring employees or candidates for recruitment, promotion, task allocation or termination are point 4(a)-(b). Public-sector eligibility or benefit-fraud models are point 5(a) — the Dutch and Nordic welfare-fraud scoring cases are the reference points here and they were built in exactly this class of tooling. **SAS Visual Investigator** splits two ways: deployed by a bank for AML it is regulated financial crime work and not Annex III point 6, because point 6 applies to law-enforcement authorities and those acting on their behalf; deployed by a police or customs authority it is point 6, and if it profiles named individuals to assess the risk that they will commit an offence it is not high risk but **prohibited outright** under Art. 5(1)(d), unless it purely supports human assessment already grounded in objective, verifiable facts directly linked to a criminal activity. State that boundary explicitly to any public-sector SAS customer. Viya Copilot itself is an Art. 50 transparency matter only — but flag that because it is bundled at no cost, it will be running in estates whose AI register says the organisation uses no generative AI.","docs_url":"https://www.sas.com/en_us/software/viya/copilot.html","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"snowflake","name":"Snowflake Cortex AI","vendor":"Snowflake Inc.","category":"Cloud Data Platform / AI Layer","walkthrough_category":"analytics","prevalence":3,"aliases":["snowflake","cortex ai","cortex analyst","snowflake intelligence","cortex agents","snowflake cortex"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Cortex Analyst","capabilities":["natural_language_query"],"default_state":"active","notes":"One of the very few features in this catalogue that is genuinely on unless someone turns it off. Snowflake's documentation states Cortex Analyst is enabled by default in accounts that support it, and an ACCOUNTADMIN must set ENABLE_CORTEX_ANALYST to FALSE to disable it; use requires the SNOWFLAKE.CORTEX_USER or CORTEX_ANALYST_USER database role. It is a managed text-to-SQL service exposed over REST, generating and executing SQL against the warehouse from a natural-language question, grounded in a Semantic View that defines logical tables, dimensions, facts, metrics and relationships. It runs on Snowflake-hosted Llama and Mistral models with optional Azure-hosted OpenAI. Billed per message processed, only on successful responses, with warehouse compute charged separately. A consultant will find this live in accounts where nobody made an AI decision.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Cortex Agents","capabilities":["natural_language_query","document_analysis","basic_automation","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Managed agentic runtime, GA since 4 November 2025, that plans a request, calls tools, runs code and returns an answer — invoking Cortex Analyst as one of its tools. 2026 added an MCP server connecting to Salesforce Agentforce, UiPath and Anthropic platforms, plus native Microsoft Teams and Copilot integration in preview. The MCP surface is the thing to flag: it lets external agents query governed Snowflake data, which moves the access-control boundary outside Snowflake's own role model.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Snowflake Intelligence","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization","document_analysis"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"The business-user-facing analytical agent over both warehouse tables and large document collections, answering questions across thousands of documents at once. Requires setup of the agent and its data scope. Where the document corpus includes HR files, case notes or customer correspondence, the agent will happily surface personal data that was previously protected by obscurity rather than by permissions.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Snowflake is infrastructure — no inherent Annex III domain, and text-to-SQL does not evaluate anyone. Two things make it worth its own conversation. First, the **default-on status of Cortex Analyst** is the single most useful finding a consultant can bring to a Snowflake owner: unless ACCOUNTADMIN has set ENABLE_CORTEX_ANALYST to FALSE, natural-language querying is live, and the exposure is determined by which Semantic Views exist and which roles hold CORTEX_USER. If a Semantic View exposes an HR schema, salary data or applicant records, ordinary staff can interrogate it in plain language with no dashboard to audit. Second, escalation runs through models rather than through Cortex: a scoring model trained in Snowflake ML on employee, applicant, borrower or claimant data and served from Snowflake makes the customer the **provider** of a high-risk system under Art. 16 — Annex III point 4 for employment, point 5(b) for creditworthiness, point 5(a) for public benefits — with conformity assessment and registration duties, not just Art. 26 deployer duties. Art. 50 obligations on Cortex Analyst are thin because it returns figures rather than published content, but a user must be able to tell that the SQL and the narrative were machine-generated. Aggregate analytics stays out of scope entirely.","docs_url":"https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/snowflake-cortex/cortex-analyst","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"alteryx","name":"Alteryx","vendor":"Alteryx, Inc.","category":"Data Preparation / Analytics Automation","walkthrough_category":"analytics","prevalence":2,"aliases":["alteryx","alteryx designer","auto insights","aidin","alteryx copilot","magic documents","alteryx one"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Alteryx Copilot","capabilities":["natural_language_query","content_generation","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Assistant in Designer that takes a question and produces workflow steps, recommends tools and places them automatically. Alteryx's own AI FAQ states it is the user's choice whether to download and use it, so it is user-opt-in rather than admin-controlled — meaning individual analysts can enable it without IT knowing. It runs on Google Gemini, customised with Alteryx data, which is a third-country processing question the customer needs an answer to.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"GenAI Tools in Designer","capabilities":["content_generation","document_analysis","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"A palette of generative tools an analyst adds to a workflow, sending prompts to an LLM through the user's own API credentials. This is the highest shadow-AI risk in the product: the workflow may push customer records into an LLM endpoint the organisation never contracted with, because the key belongs to the analyst. Ask whether Designer workflows are scanned for these tools before they are promoted to Server.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Magic Documents and Playbooks (Auto Insights)","capabilities":["summarization","content_generation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Magic Documents drafts a stakeholder-ready written summary of what Auto Insights found; Playbooks package recommended analytical actions. Both are administrator-controlled — the admin can turn them on or off — and both connect to OpenAI models via Microsoft Azure rather than to a user-supplied endpoint, which is a materially better data-flow position than the Designer GenAI tools. Note that Magic Documents produces narrative business claims that circulate independently of the underlying data.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Predictive analytics and Assisted Modeling","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Alteryx ships forecasting and classification model building for analysts without a data science background. As with Qlik Predict, the fact that no specialist is required is what makes it a governance problem — a finance or HR analyst can build and schedule a person-level classification model inside a workflow with no model review. Requires the relevant platform tier.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Fin (Designer Cloud assistant) and Ask Alteryx","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Fin is the in-product chatbot in Designer Cloud and, per the AI FAQ, is not presented as an opt-in choice the way Copilot and the GenAI Tools are; Ask Alteryx and Alteryx Agents use Google Gemini via a global endpoint. The FAQ does not state an admin off-switch for Fin, so treat it as present unless the customer can show otherwise. The 'global endpoint' wording is worth pushing on for any customer with EU data-residency commitments.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Alteryx has no inherent Annex III domain, but it is disproportionately used for HR analytics, finance close and customer analytics, so ask what the workflows operate on before concluding anything. The escalation trigger is a **classification model built in Alteryx predictive tooling and scheduled in a Server workflow**: scoring employees for attrition or performance is Annex III point 4, scoring applicants for credit is point 5(b), scoring claimants is point 5(a) — and because the customer built the model, they are the **provider** under Art. 16, not a deployer. The distinctively Alteryx risk is invisibility: a scoring model inside a scheduled workflow has no dashboard, no login page and no product name, so it will not appear in an AI inventory built by asking which AI products the organisation licenses. Ask for a list of scheduled Server workflows that write to an HR or credit table. On transparency, Magic Documents output circulating as a written business summary should be identifiable as machine-generated. One branding note for the register: Alteryx's current trust documentation names Copilot, GenAI Tools, Magic Documents, Playbooks, Fin and Ask Alteryx, and no longer uses the 'AiDIN' umbrella that appeared throughout the 2024 material — a customer's AI register will still say AiDIN.","docs_url":"https://www.alteryx.com/trust/alteryx-artificial-intelligence-faqs","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"ibm-watsonx-bi","name":"IBM watsonx BI","vendor":"IBM","category":"Business Intelligence / Enterprise Reporting","walkthrough_category":"analytics","prevalence":2,"aliases":["watsonx bi","watsonx","cognos","cognos analytics","ibm cognos","cognos analytics with watson"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"watsonx BI conversational insights","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"IBM positions watsonx BI as a business insight agent rather than a dashboard tool: ask questions conversationally and get answers grounded in the governed semantic models and trusted metrics already defined in IBM Cognos Analytics, with connections out to the lakehouse, automation workflows and existing reporting tools. Because it reuses Cognos metric definitions, the exposure is inherited from a semantic model that in most Cognos estates was built years ago by people who have left. Treat maturity with care — watsonx BI's original GA target slipped and IBM has been folding capability into it since, so confirm what the customer actually has entitlement to rather than what the product page describes.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Cognos Analytics AI Assistant","capabilities":["natural_language_query","chatbot_assistant","summarization"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"The assistant panel embedded in IBM Cognos Analytics with Watson, taking text input to suggest visualisations, explain data and simplify exploration. It has shipped inside Cognos Analytics for several releases, so it is present in existing deployments without any AI purchase — the same pattern as Qlik's Insight Advisor. IBM has been gating newer embedded AI in Cognos and Planning Analytics behind current versions, so a customer on an older release may not have it. Confirm the release level before recording it as active.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"No inherent Annex III domain — Cognos and watsonx BI report on whatever the semantic model exposes, and neither trains models that score people. Natural-language querying is an Art. 50 transparency matter: users need to be able to tell that an answer and its narrative were machine-generated, and the risk specific to this pair is misplaced confidence, because answers grounded in a governed Cognos metric layer feel authoritative even when the underlying model is stale. The escalation question is what the semantic model contains. Cognos has an unusually deep footprint in Nordic public administration, healthcare and higher education, so a realistic finding is a semantic model exposing patient, pupil or benefit-claimant data to conversational querying by staff who previously needed a report author. That is a GDPR minimisation and access-control problem rather than an automatic Annex III classification — Annex III attaches when a system evaluates individuals, and reporting does not. It becomes high risk only if a model scoring people is surfaced through it: a student-progression or admissions model is point 3, an employee model is point 4, a benefit-eligibility or fraud model is point 5(a). Note also that IBM's product pages block automated retrieval, so this entry was compiled from IBM product and documentation pages surfaced through IBM's own domain rather than a live page render.","docs_url":"https://www.ibm.com/products/watsonx-bi","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"thoughtspot","name":"ThoughtSpot","vendor":"ThoughtSpot, Inc.","category":"Search and AI-Driven Analytics","walkthrough_category":"analytics","prevalence":2,"aliases":["thoughtspot","spotter","spotterviz","spottermodel","thoughtspot sage"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Spotter","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization","recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"ThoughtSpot's agentic analyst. Beyond answering questions it does multi-step reasoning — decomposing a question, testing assumptions, checking results and returning recommended actions — across cloud warehouses, business applications and unstructured sources, grounded in a governed semantic layer with token-level traceability. The part that changes its regulatory character is write-back: Spotter creates Jira tickets, updates Salesforce opportunities, posts to Slack and triggers enterprise workflows. It does not merely inform a decision, it executes one. Enforces role-based, row-level and column-level security with zero LLM data retention.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Spotter agent suite (SpotterViz, SpotterModel, SpotterCode)","capabilities":["natural_language_query","content_generation","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Announced December 2025 as a coordinated set: SpotterViz builds dashboards from natural language, SpotterModel builds semantic models with no code, SpotterCode generates code for embedded analytics applications. SpotterModel is the one to scrutinise — an AI-authored semantic model defines what the organisation's metrics mean, and if it is wrong or exposes the wrong columns, every downstream Spotter answer inherits the error with no visible signal.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Spotter for Industries","capabilities":["natural_language_query","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Launched March 2026, extending Spotter with domain vocabulary and context for verticals including insurance, travel and hospitality, and manufacturing. The insurance variant is the one with regulatory weight: an agent tuned to insurance language, given claims and policy data and asked to recommend actions, is operating adjacent to risk assessment and pricing of individual policyholders.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Domain depends entirely on the loaded data, but ThoughtSpot escalates more easily than a conventional BI tool for two specific reasons that should be named rather than generalised. First, **Spotter acts**: creating a ticket, updating a CRM record or triggering a workflow is an automated action, so where the affected record is a person and the action affects their rights or obligations, the deployer cannot argue the system merely informs a human. Second, **Spotter for Industries (Insurance)** applied to individual policyholder risk or pricing falls under Annex III point 5(c), which covers risk assessment and pricing for life and health insurance. Beyond those: Spotter over HR data producing per-employee recommendations is point 4; over lending data producing per-applicant recommendations is point 5(b); in a public-sector tenant over benefit data, point 5(a). Spotter answering aggregate commercial questions — revenue, churn rate by cohort, supply chain — stays outside Annex III, and Art. 50 transparency in that case is satisfied by making clear the answer is machine-generated. Ask what write-back integrations are enabled; that single answer usually decides the tier.","docs_url":"https://www.thoughtspot.com/product/spotter","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"mailchimp","name":"Intuit Mailchimp","vendor":"Intuit","category":"Email Marketing / Marketing Automation","walkthrough_category":"marketing","prevalence":4,"aliases":["mailchimp","intuit mailchimp","intuit assist","mailchimp ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Intuit Assist for Mailchimp","capabilities":["content_generation","chatbot_assistant","summarization"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Intuit's cross-product AI assistant surfaced inside the Mailchimp campaign builder. Drafts email copy, subject lines, SMS and social posts, and proposes complete automations (Welcome New Contacts, Recover Lost Contacts, Abandoned Cart) that a marketer turns on with one click. Mailchimp states it is available to certain users on Premium, Standard and Legacy plans, in select countries, English only, at no additional cost — so on an eligible plan it appears without anyone buying or enabling it, but coverage is patchy. Ask the system owner which plan and which country the account is billed in before assuming it is live.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Analytics AI","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Conversational analytics agent launched 28 May 2026 and stated by Mailchimp to be available to all paying customers globally. It reads the account's campaign history together with connected commerce data (Shopify, WooCommerce, Wix), explains what changed and why, and recommends next actions. The recommendations are audience-level rather than individual-level, but the underlying joins are over identified contacts. Confirm what commerce data has been connected — that, not Mailchimp, determines how sensitive the profiling is.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Send Time Optimization and predictive segmentation","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Predicts the day and hour each contact is most likely to engage and shifts the send accordingly, and builds segments from predicted engagement behaviour. This is per-person behavioural profiling under GDPR Art. 4(4) and needs to appear in the privacy notice and ROPA, even though it is nowhere near Annex III. It is a per-campaign toggle, not an account-wide default — a consultant should check individual campaign settings rather than a global switch.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Write with AI (inline generation in the editor)","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Generative text produced directly in the email content editor, plus derivative SMS and social posts generated from an existing email. Output goes to real subscribers under the customer's brand, so the customer is the deployer of the published content even though Intuit is the provider of the generating system. There is no visible marking of AI-generated body copy in the delivered email today.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Stays outside Annex III in ordinary use — consumer marketing is not an Annex III domain, and Mailchimp does not decide anyone's access to a service. The live obligations are Art. 50: Intuit as provider of the generative features must mark output in a machine-readable format (Art. 50(2)), with the deadline for systems already on the market before 2 August 2026 running to 2 December 2026; the customer as deployer must disclose under Art. 50(4) only where AI-generated text is published to inform the public on matters of public interest, which ordinary promotional email is not. The genuine risk boundary is Art. 5(1)(a)-(b): predictive segmentation plus generative copy aimed at a segment the marketer knows to be vulnerable (age, disability, financial distress) to materially distort behaviour is a prohibited practice, not a high-risk one. If the account markets consumer credit and the AI-built segments determine who is offered credit at all, escalate to Annex III point 5(b).","docs_url":"https://mailchimp.com/solutions/ai-tools/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"brevo","name":"Brevo","vendor":"Brevo (formerly Sendinblue)","category":"Email Marketing / CRM Suite","walkthrough_category":"marketing","prevalence":3,"aliases":["brevo","sendinblue","aura","brevo aura","aura ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Aura","capabilities":["content_generation","basic_automation"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Brevo's AI assistant, launched May 2025 out of a stated €50m AI investment. Generates subject lines, body copy, images and complete ready-to-edit email designs from a described campaign goal, writes and refines SMS content, and builds automation workflows from a natural-language description of the desired flow. It sits inside the campaign editor rather than behind a separate purchase, but generation volume is limited by plan — confirm the plan's Aura allowance rather than recording it as unrestricted.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Aura contact data enrichment","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Automatically appends attributes Brevo's customer never collected — job title, company name, location — onto contact profiles to build richer segments. This is third-party data acquisition dressed as a convenience feature and is the part of Aura most likely to be missing from the customer's privacy notice and Art. 14 GDPR information duty. Establish where the enrichment data originates before signing it off.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Predictive sending and segmentation","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Per-contact send-time optimisation and AI-assisted audience segmentation, plus dynamic product recommendations rendered per recipient. Per-campaign settings rather than an account default. Standard behavioural profiling with no Annex III implication.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Not Annex III. Brevo is worth a specific note because it is one of the few marketing platforms in a European estate where the provider is EU-established (French), so the Art. 50(2) machine-readable marking duty on generated email, SMS and image output falls on an entity inside the Union rather than on a US vendor relying on a representative — which makes it materially easier to get a written answer about compliance status before the 2 December 2026 marking deadline. The customer remains a deployer with Art. 50(4) duties only where generated text is published to inform the public on matters of public interest. The contact-enrichment feature raises no AI Act issue but is the most likely GDPR finding in a Brevo review. Escalation to Annex III would require the customer to use Aura-built segments to determine eligibility for credit or an essential service, which the product is not designed for.","docs_url":"https://www.brevo.com/products/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"hootsuite","name":"Hootsuite","vendor":"Hootsuite Inc.","category":"Social Media Management","walkthrough_category":"marketing","prevalence":3,"aliases":["hootsuite","owlywriter","owlywriter ai","owlygpt","hootsuite wisdom","blue silk"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Wisdom (formerly OwlyWriter AI)","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Hootsuite's social AI assistant, now marketed as Wisdom and grounded in real-time social conversation data. Drafts posts and captions, rewrites existing posts, generates campaign ideas and whole content calendars for LinkedIn, Instagram, X and Facebook. It has historically been bundled into paid Hootsuite plans and appears inside the composer without a separate purchase, but Hootsuite's current platform page does not publish a plan-by-plan breakdown — confirm the tier before recording it as included. The name change from OwlyWriter matters: a customer's AI register may still say OwlyWriter.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Blue Silk AI","capabilities":["summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"The intelligence engine underneath the platform. Processes public social conversation at volume, classifies sentiment, forecasts trends up to 90 days ahead, and runs image and video recognition against a library of 30,000+ visual models. The visual recognition is object, logo and scene detection, not face matching, so it is not biometric identification — but confirm that, because it is the one place in a social suite where a biometrics question could arise. Sentiment is inferred over public posts, which are authored by identifiable natural persons.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Yeti Agent","capabilities":["summarization","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Agentic reporting layer that scans the account's social data unprompted and returns a plain-language report with citations, visualisations and recommended actions. Sold with the higher Hootsuite tiers rather than included across the board. Low regulatory weight on its own; the point for a consultant is that it produces narrative claims about performance that stakeholders may act on without checking the underlying figures.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI chatbot and smart replies in Inbox","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Automated handling of inbound social and messaging enquiries around the clock, with context-aware suggested replies for human agents and escalation to a human when the bot cannot resolve. This is the one Hootsuite component that talks directly to a natural person as if it were the brand, which is precisely what Art. 50(1) is written for. Sold as part of the advanced/service tiers, not present in a standard social-publishing subscription.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Never Annex III on its own. Two live Art. 50 duties: the Inbox chatbot must tell the person they are talking to a machine unless it is obvious from context (Art. 50(1)), and generated social posts fall under the provider marking duty in Art. 50(2), which is Hootsuite's obligation and runs to 2 December 2026 for systems already on the market. Note that platform-side AI labels on Meta and LinkedIn are not the same thing as Art. 50 compliance and do not discharge the deployer's duty. The genuine prohibition risk is narrow but real: Blue Silk sentiment analysis is fine on public brand conversation, but the moment it is pointed at employees' own posts or an internal social channel to read staff mood, it becomes inference of emotions in the workplace and is banned outright under Art. 5(1)(f), with no risk-assessment path out.","docs_url":"https://www.hootsuite.com/platform/ai","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"klaviyo","name":"Klaviyo","vendor":"Klaviyo, Inc.","category":"Ecommerce Marketing Automation / CDP","walkthrough_category":"marketing","prevalence":3,"aliases":["klaviyo","klaviyo ai","k:ai","klaviyo predictive analytics"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Klaviyo predictive analytics","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active","notes":"This is one of the few genuinely on-by-default AI features in the catalogue. Once an account crosses Klaviyo's thresholds — 500+ customers with non-refunded orders, an active ecommerce integration, 180 days of order history with orders in the last 30 days, and some customers with 3+ orders — Klaviyo automatically computes predicted CLV, churn risk (shown as a red/amber/green flag), expected date of next order and **predicted gender** on every customer profile, and retrains weekly. Nobody has to enable it. Predicted gender is inferred from first name plus census data, meaning Klaviyo manufactures an inferred attribute about identified individuals that the customer never collected and the data subject never supplied. That is the finding to put in front of a DPO.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Klaviyo AI Customer Agent","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Autonomous customer-facing agent released with public APIs in mid-2026, which Klaviyo markets as resolving a majority of conversations without a human. It can read a shopper's order history, segment membership and predictive scores mid-conversation, so the churn-risk flag and predicted gender can influence what a live customer is told or offered. Separately licensed. Ask explicitly whether the agent's offer logic is allowed to read the predictive fields.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI content generation (subject lines, email and SMS copy)","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Generative assistance inside the email and SMS editors, plus AI-assisted segment building from a natural-language description. Present in the UI but requires the marketer to invoke it; it does not generate and send anything unattended. Low regulatory weight compared with the predictive layer.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Not Annex III — ecommerce marketing is not a listed domain and Klaviyo does not gate access to any service. The urgent issue is GDPR rather than the AI Act: automatic inference of gender and churn risk on every profile without an admin action is profiling under Art. 4(4), requires a lawful basis and a privacy-notice entry, and predicted gender is an inferred attribute that is trivially wrong for many names and carries discrimination exposure if it drives differential offers. Under the AI Act itself, Art. 50(1) applies to the Customer Agent — it must disclose that it is AI to the shopper — and Art. 50(2) machine-readable marking of generated copy is Klaviyo's provider duty, with the 2 December 2026 deadline for systems already on the market. Escalate to Annex III point 5(b) only in the narrow case where a merchant offering buy-now-pay-later or instalment credit lets the churn or CLV score determine who is offered that credit, which turns a marketing score into a creditworthiness evaluation.","docs_url":"https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360020919731","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"salesforce-marketing-cloud","name":"Salesforce Marketing Cloud (Agentforce Marketing)","vendor":"Salesforce","category":"Marketing Automation / Campaign Management","walkthrough_category":"marketing","prevalence":3,"aliases":["marketing cloud","sfmc","salesforce marketing cloud","marketing cloud next","agentforce marketing","einstein marketing","exacttarget","pardot","account engagement"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Agentforce Marketing (formerly Marketing Cloud Next)","capabilities":["content_generation","basic_automation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Salesforce renamed Marketing Cloud Next to Agentforce Marketing during the 2026 releases — a customer's documentation will still say Marketing Cloud Next. Agents build campaigns, generate briefs and creative, and act on Data Cloud signals to choose channel, timing and offer. Spring '26 added business-unit partitioning (up to 50 units per org) and made Einstein features business-unit aware, which was the blocker for enterprise adoption. Consumed through Agentforce credits, so it is a distinct commercial decision from owning Marketing Cloud.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Einstein Engagement Scoring","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Scores every contact on likelihood to open, click, convert and unsubscribe, and buckets them into personas. This is straightforward automated profiling of identified individuals and is the feature most likely to be missing from the customer's ROPA. It has to be switched on and given a data window before it produces anything, so an untouched org will show it dormant. Ask what downstream automations consume the score — suppression rules built on a churn score are a decision about a person, not just a report.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Einstein Send Time Optimization","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Per-individual model that predicts the best hour to reach each contact and holds the send until then. Now scoped per business unit following the Spring '26 changes. Low harm potential, but it is per-person behavioural modelling and belongs in the AI inventory alongside the scoring models rather than being waved through as a scheduling feature.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Einstein generative content creation","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Inline generation of subject lines, email body copy and SMS within the campaign builder, grounded on brand and Data Cloud context. Requires the Einstein Generative AI features to be enabled at org level and, in most orgs, the Einstein Trust Layer configuration to be reviewed first. Output goes to named recipients under the customer's brand.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Marketing Cloud in its ordinary use is outside Annex III: propensity and engagement scoring for campaign targeting is GDPR profiling, not a high-risk AI system, because it does not determine access to a service. The boundary to name precisely is Annex III point 5(b): if a bank or lender uses Marketing Cloud scores to decide who is shown a credit offer at all, or at what price, that is evaluation of creditworthiness and the system becomes high risk with the customer as deployer under Art. 26 — and, if they trained the model themselves in Data Cloud, potentially as provider under Art. 25. Point 5(c) applies the same way to life and health insurance pricing. Independently of tier, Art. 50(2) machine-readable marking of Einstein-generated email and SMS copy is Salesforce's provider duty and runs to 2 December 2026 for systems already on the market, and any Agentforce agent that converses with a customer must disclose itself under Art. 50(1). Salesforce's own product pages block automated fetching, so this entry was verified from Salesforce release documentation rather than a live page render.","docs_url":"https://www.salesforce.com/marketing/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"semrush","name":"Semrush","vendor":"Semrush Holdings","category":"SEO / Search and AI Visibility","walkthrough_category":"marketing","prevalence":3,"aliases":["semrush","semrush ai toolkit","contentshake","contentshake ai","semrush one"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI Visibility toolkit","capabilities":["summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Measures how often and how favourably the brand appears in answers from ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini, Claude and Google AI Overviews, benchmarks against competitors, and researches which user prompts trigger a mention. The sentiment analysis is applied to how LLMs describe brands, not to natural persons, so it is not emotion recognition in the AI Act sense. It is a separately trialled and separately priced toolkit rather than something bundled into a standard Semrush seat.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI content creation (Semrush Content Toolkit)","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Generates SEO-ready article drafts from keyword and competitor data rather than from a blank prompt, and scores drafts for readability, tone and originality. Semrush's own features page markets this as 'content creation' within the current toolkit; the standalone product was previously sold as ContentShake AI and third-party sources report it has been folded into a renamed Content Toolkit, so verify the SKU name on the customer's invoice rather than trusting either name. Output is published under the customer's brand on the customer's own site.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Not an Annex III product and not a candidate for high-risk classification — it analyses search results and brands, never individuals. The single obligation that bites is Art. 50(4): AI-generated article text published on the customer's site counts as AI-generated text, and where the subject matter is a matter of public interest it must be disclosed unless a human reviewed it and a named person holds editorial responsibility. The provider-side machine-readable marking duty under Art. 50(2) sits with Semrush and, further upstream, with whichever foundation-model provider Semrush routes generation to; the marking deadline for pre-existing systems is 2 December 2026. Worth asking whether the customer's CMS strips metadata on publish, because that will destroy any provenance marking Semrush does apply.","docs_url":"https://www.semrush.com/features/ai-visibility/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"adobe-genstudio","name":"Adobe GenStudio for Performance Marketing","vendor":"Adobe","category":"Content Supply Chain / Performance Marketing","walkthrough_category":"marketing","prevalence":2,"aliases":["genstudio","adobe genstudio","genstudio for performance marketing","adobe experience cloud","cx enterprise","aem","marketo"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"GenStudio generative content creation","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Generates on-brand copy, image and video variants at volume for social, display, email and connected TV, using Firefly alongside Azure OpenAI and third-party or custom brand-trained models. 2026 added ChatGPT and CTV as activation channels, so generated creative now publishes straight into an AI assistant surface. Sold as a distinct GenStudio entitlement — an Experience Cloud customer does not have it by default. Whether Adobe attaches C2PA Content Credentials to every GenStudio variant, as opposed to Firefly output in Creative Cloud, is not stated on the product page and must be confirmed with Adobe rather than assumed.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Content Insights","capabilities":["summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"AI-driven analysis of creative performance by attribute — copy tone, keywords, colour, video scene — against CTR, CPA and spend, with proactive optimisation recommendations. The analysis subject is the creative asset, not the individual, so there is no person-level profiling here; the audience data it correlates against comes from the connected Experience Platform profile store, which is where the personal-data question actually lives.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Adobe Brand Intelligence and Firefly AI Assistant","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Adobe's 2026 platform layer: Brand Intelligence learns brand rules from approvals, rejections and annotations over time and feeds that judgement to agents across content production, while the Firefly AI Assistant lets a user describe an outcome and have it orchestrate multi-step work. Adobe was reported at Summit 2026 to be repositioning Experience Cloud under a 'CX Enterprise' banner — treat the naming as unstable and record whatever the customer's contract actually says.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"No Annex III exposure — GenStudio produces advertising creative and does not assess people. Article 50 is the whole story. Adobe is the provider of the generative models and carries the Art. 50(2) duty to mark output machine-readably, enforceable from 2 August 2026 with the extension to 2 December 2026 for systems already on the market; the customer is the deployer and carries Art. 50(4) for any generated image or video that depicts a realistic-looking person, which GenStudio's synthetic model and lifestyle imagery routinely does. Two practical traps to raise: variant generation at this volume means nobody reviews individual assets, so a deepfake-marking failure scales instantly; and publishing into ChatGPT and CTV strips or reformats metadata, which can silently destroy Content Credentials that were correctly applied at generation. Ask to see one delivered asset and check whether provenance metadata survived the round trip.","docs_url":"https://business.adobe.com/products/genstudio/performance-marketing.html","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"elevenlabs","name":"ElevenLabs","vendor":"ElevenLabs Inc.","category":"Synthetic Voice / Text to Speech","walkthrough_category":"marketing","prevalence":2,"aliases":["elevenlabs","eleven labs","voice cloning","ai voiceover","tts"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Instant Voice Cloning (IVC)","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Produces a usable clone of a voice from roughly one to five minutes of audio — ElevenLabs markets a lifelike result from a ten-second recording. The low sample requirement is the governance problem: any recorded town hall, podcast or webinar contains enough material to clone a colleague without their involvement. ElevenLabs states cloning requires the voice owner's explicit permission, but IVC does not enforce that technically the way Professional cloning does. A consultant should ask what the customer does to evidence consent, not whether the vendor requires it.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Professional Voice Cloning (PVC)","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"A dedicated hyper-realistic voice model trained on 30+ minutes of clean audio, described by ElevenLabs as virtually indistinguishable from the original. Access requires technological verification of the speaker, and cloning of celebrity and other high-risk voices is blocked. This is the tier used for brand voices, audiobooks and localisation, and the output will pass as a real named person to any listener.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Text to Speech generation","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Synthetic speech from a library of stock voices for voiceover, IVR, product audio and localisation. ElevenLabs publishes an AI Speech Classifier that detects whether a given clip was produced on its platform, and references C2PA and the Content Authenticity Initiative on its safety page — but that page addresses the EU Digital Services Act and does not mention the AI Act, so do not treat the safety page as evidence of Art. 50(2) compliance. Third-party reporting places ElevenLabs among the partners adopting Google's SynthID for audio watermarking; verify that directly with the vendor rather than relying on press coverage.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"No Annex III entry and not a high-risk system — voice synthesis does not assess or decide about anyone. Keep the two Art. 50 duties separate. Art. 50(2) is **ElevenLabs' obligation as provider**: mark generated audio machine-readably and detectably, effective 2 August 2026, with the grandfathering extension to 2 December 2026 for systems already on the market. A detection service that only ElevenLabs can run is not the same as an embedded machine-readable mark, so ask specifically whether the mark is in the delivered file and whether it survives MP3 transcoding and broadcast processing. Art. 50(4) is **the customer's obligation as deployer**: synthetic audio of a real person is a deepfake and must be disclosed; the artistic and satirical carve-out permits only a non-obtrusive mention and does not extend to advertising or corporate communications. Two additional boundaries worth stating plainly. First, an outbound or inbound voice agent built on ElevenLabs that speaks to customers triggers Art. 50(1) as well — the person must be told they are speaking to an AI system, and a synthetic voice that sounds human makes the 'obvious from the circumstances' exemption unavailable. Second, cloning a voice to impersonate someone for fraud is not an AI Act tier question at all; it is criminal, and the AI Act's transparency regime offers no shelter. Under GDPR, a voiceprint used to generate speech is personal data and, where it is used to identify the speaker, biometric data — but generation alone does not make this an Annex III biometrics system.","docs_url":"https://elevenlabs.io/safety","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"jasper","name":"Jasper","vendor":"Jasper AI, Inc.","category":"AI Content Generation / Marketing Copy","walkthrough_category":"marketing","prevalence":2,"aliases":["jasper","jasper ai","jarvis","jasper iq"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Jasper marketing agents (Optimization, Research, Translation, GEO)","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Purpose-built agents that execute marketing tasks end to end rather than returning a single completion. The GEO Agent continuously monitors and tries to influence how the brand is described inside ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini and AI Overviews; the Translation Agent localises campaigns. Everything runs on a paid Jasper subscription — a customer who has not bought Jasper has none of it. Ask who signs off agent output before publication, because the agents are designed to run without a human in every loop.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Jasper IQ (Brand IQ, Marketing IQ, brand voice, style guide, knowledge base)","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The context layer that injects brand voice, visual guidelines, audience profiles and product knowledge into every generation. Operationally this is where a customer's proprietary and sometimes personal data (customer research, persona documents) is uploaded into a US vendor's platform. Jasper MCP exposes the same brand context to external AI tools, which widens the data-flow question considerably.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Canvas and AI Studio (custom AI apps and content pipelines)","capabilities":["content_generation","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Canvas is the collaborative workspace; AI Studio lets non-developers build custom AI applications and multi-step content pipelines that run unattended. This is the feature that turns Jasper from a copywriting tool into something a compliance owner needs an inventory of, because individual marketers create durable automated systems inside it that nobody has registered.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"No Annex III exposure — Jasper generates marketing copy and does not evaluate people. Jasper is a non-EU provider of a generative system, so the Art. 50(2) machine-readable marking obligation is Jasper's, not the customer's, and is enforceable from 2 August 2026 with the grandfathering extension to 2 December 2026 for systems already on the market. The customer's own duty is Art. 50(4): where Jasper-generated text is published to inform the public on matters of public interest — a bank's rate-change explainer, an energy company's tariff notice, a public-sector campaign — it must be disclosed as AI-generated unless a human took editorial responsibility and reviewed it. Note that the human-review carve-out in Art. 50(4) is exactly what an unattended content pipeline removes, so a consultant should ask specifically whether pipeline output ever publishes without a named human approver.","docs_url":"https://www.jasper.ai/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"sprinklr","name":"Sprinklr Unified-CXM (Sprinklr AI+)","vendor":"Sprinklr, Inc.","category":"Unified Customer Experience Management / Social and Service","walkthrough_category":"marketing","prevalence":2,"aliases":["sprinklr","sprinklr ai+","sprinklr ai plus","unified-cxm","sprinklr social"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Sprinklr AI+","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The generative layer spanning Sprinklr Social, Marketing, Insights and Service. Sprinklr describes it as combining its own domain models with third-party LLMs, with model choice left to the customer including bring-your-own-LLM. Governance is unusually well documented for this category: zero data retention, automatic PII masking, input and output guardrails, approval workflows, usage logs and monitoring dashboards, and a statement that no customer data trains Sprinklr-owned or third-party models. Sprinklr positions AI+ as a distinct offering layered on the platform rather than a bundled feature — confirm the entitlement line on the order form, because a Sprinklr customer is not automatically an AI+ customer.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI+ Studio","capabilities":["content_generation","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"No-code workspace for building, bulk-testing and monitoring generative agents and prompt workflows across countries, languages and brands. This is where a marketing team creates durable automated systems without involving IT, so it is the highest-value place to ask for an inventory. Sprinklr's own framing is that it exists to give oversight of AI behaviour, which is a useful hook for an Art. 26 deployer-governance conversation even where nothing is high risk.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Sprinklr AI (predictive listening and classification)","capabilities":["summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"The long-standing predictive layer that ingests unstructured data from 30+ digital channels plus millions of news, blog and review sites, and classifies sentiment, intent and topic. It runs over content authored by identifiable natural persons and drives routing and prioritisation decisions. Distinct from AI+ and generally present in a Sprinklr Insights or Social deployment without a separate AI purchase.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Not Annex III as a marketing and social platform. Art. 50(1) applies to any Sprinklr Service conversational bot that handles inbound customer contact, and Art. 50(2) marking of generated posts and replies is Sprinklr's provider duty, running to 2 December 2026 for systems already placed on the market. Two escalation paths are worth naming concretely. First, sentiment and intent classification applied to employees — internal communities, employee advocacy channels, agent-performance monitoring — crosses into Art. 5(1)(f) prohibited emotion inference in the workplace. Second, if Sprinklr Service is the intake channel for an essential service and its AI triages or de-prioritises who gets human help, that is not itself Annex III point 5, which covers eligibility decisions rather than queueing — but if the routing determines whether a benefit claim or an insurance claim proceeds at all, it is, and the deployer takes on Art. 26 obligations including human oversight and logging.","docs_url":"https://www.sprinklr.com/products/platform/ai-plus/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"synthesia","name":"Synthesia","vendor":"Synthesia Limited","category":"AI Avatar Video Generation","walkthrough_category":"marketing","prevalence":2,"aliases":["synthesia","ai avatar video","synthesia studio","ai presenter"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Stock and customizable AI avatars","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"240+ ready-made photorealistic presenters with natural gesture and expression, plus prompt-driven outfit, setting and action generation. The presenter is not a real person but is indistinguishable from one to a viewer, which is exactly the definition the AI Act uses for a deepfake — the test in Art. 3(60) is that the content appears authentic, not that a specific identifiable person is depicted. Every video produced this way triggers the deployer's disclosure duty.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Personal and Studio Avatars with voice cloning","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Creates a synthetic double of a real, named individual — typically a CEO, a subject-matter expert or a trainer — from a photo, a short self-recorded video, or a professional capture session, optionally paired with a clone of that person's voice. This is the sharpest configuration in the whole catalogue: a realistic video of a real employee saying words they never said. Synthesia requires avatar consent and enforces identity-based controls, and holds ISO 42001, ISO 27001, ISO 27701 and SOC 2 Type II. The customer still needs its own written consent from the individual covering scope, duration and withdrawal, because the Synthesia consent is to Synthesia, not to the employer's future use.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI Dubbing and translation","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Translates an existing video into 140+ languages with lip-sync matched to the new audio. Applied to footage of a real person, this is manipulation of authentic material rather than generation from scratch, and it is the case regulators point to most often: the person's mouth is made to form words in a language they do not speak. Useful and legitimate for internal training and multilingual comms, but it is unambiguously a deepfake under Art. 3(60).","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Interactive Avatars","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Real-time responsive avatars that connect to a customer-supplied language model, so the avatar holds a live conversation rather than reading a script. This stacks two separate transparency duties on the same interaction — the person is both talking to an AI system and watching synthetic imagery of an apparent human. Also note the customer, not Synthesia, chooses the model behind it, which changes who the provider is for the conversational component.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Synthesia is not a high-risk system and has no Annex III entry — video generation neither evaluates nor decides about anyone. Get the two Art. 50 limbs the right way round, because customers consistently confuse them. Art. 50(2) obliges **Synthesia as provider** to mark its output in a machine-readable, interoperable and reliably detectable format; Synthesia is a Content Authenticity Initiative member and labels its own site content as AI-generated with an explicit Art. 50 reference, but the customer must still get written confirmation of what marking is embedded in delivered MP4s and whether it survives re-encoding for LinkedIn or an LMS. Art. 50(4) obliges **the customer as deployer** to disclose that the content is artificially generated or manipulated, clearly and distinguishably, at first exposure — and the exemptions do not help here: the artistic, satirical and fictional carve-out reduces the duty to a non-obtrusive mention but does not apply to corporate marketing, and the criminal-law exemption is irrelevant. Where the avatar is a real employee, add GDPR: their likeness and voiceprint are personal data, an employment relationship makes freely given consent hard to establish, and the avatar must be destroyable on withdrawal. One boundary that is often over-claimed: an avatar of a real person is not Annex III biometrics, because the system generates a likeness rather than identifying or categorising anyone from biometric data. Education (Annex III point 3) is likewise not triggered by delivering training videos — it would require the system to assess learning outcomes or determine access to a programme, which Synthesia does not do.","docs_url":"https://www.synthesia.io/ethics","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"github-copilot","name":"GitHub Copilot","vendor":"GitHub (Microsoft)","category":"AI Coding Assistant / Developer Platform","walkthrough_category":"dev_tools","prevalence":4,"aliases":["github copilot","gh copilot","copilot coding agent","copilot cloud agent","copilot workspace","copilot for business","github copilot chat"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Copilot code completion","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Inline code suggestion in VS Code, Visual Studio, JetBrains IDEs and Neovim. Sold as a per-seat SKU (Copilot Business / Copilot Enterprise) that an org owner must purchase and then assign to named users, so a GitHub customer who has not bought Copilot has nothing running. There is a free individual tier, which is the usual route by which it appears in an estate without procurement noticing. GitHub states that Business and Enterprise prompts and suggestions are not used to train its models — confirm against the current Copilot Trust Center rather than taking it from a slide.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Copilot Chat","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Conversational assistant in the IDE, on github.com and in the CLI, included with a Copilot seat rather than bought separately. Enterprise and organisation owners control it through Copilot policies, and for most policies the enterprise can enable, disable, or delegate the choice to each organisation. The consultant question is not 'do you have Copilot' but 'who set the enterprise policy and when was it last reviewed'.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Copilot cloud agent","capabilities":["content_generation","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"An asynchronous agent that takes an assigned issue, works in a GitHub-hosted environment and opens a pull request. GitHub's docs state it is disabled by default and must be enabled by an administrator; at enterprise level the policy can be set to enable it only for named organisations. GitHub has used several names for this capability — the standalone Copilot Workspace preview, then Copilot coding agent, now documented as Copilot cloud agent — so match the term to the docs version you are reading. Output still lands in a human-reviewed PR, which is the control that keeps this ordinary rather than autonomous.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Ordinary use is minimal risk: the system generates code for a developer who reviews it, and no natural person is profiled or decided about. It still counts for the Article 4 AI-literacy duty and belongs in the Article 26-style inventory. Two things move it: (1) if the code being generated is itself an Annex III system, the developer becomes a provider and the obligation attaches to the product, not to Copilot; (2) if Copilot seat-usage, acceptance-rate or engagement telemetry is fed into individual developer performance review, that is monitoring and evaluating workers under Annex III point 4(b) and the tooling around Copilot — not Copilot — becomes the high-risk system. Ask the platform owner whether Copilot metrics leave the engineering-productivity dashboard and reach a line manager.","docs_url":"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/policies","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"claude-code","name":"Claude Code","vendor":"Anthropic","category":"Agentic Coding Tool","walkthrough_category":"dev_tools","prevalence":3,"aliases":["claude code","anthropic claude code","claude cli","claude agent sdk"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Claude Code agent (terminal, IDE, desktop, web)","capabilities":["content_generation","chatbot_assistant","summarization","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"An agentic tool that reads a codebase, edits files, runs shell commands, drives git, opens pull requests and calls MCP servers. Access requires a paid Claude subscription (Pro, Max, Team, Enterprise) or an Anthropic Console/API account, so it is never present without someone paying for it — but individual Pro subscriptions on expenses are the usual arrival route, exactly as with Cursor. Behaviour is configurable through CLAUDE.md instruction files, hooks and settings files, which is also how an organisation constrains it.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Routines and scheduled cloud sessions","capabilities":["basic_automation","content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Claude Code sessions can be scheduled to run in the cloud on a cron-like basis or triggered by GitHub events, continuing without a developer present. This is the feature that changes the risk conversation: an unattended agent with repository write access and MCP connections to Jira, Drive or Slack has a materially wider blast radius than an in-editor assistant. Ask which MCP servers are configured and what credentials they hold.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Minimal risk under the AI Act — code generation for a reviewing developer, no natural person profiled. Article 4 literacy applies and it belongs in the inventory. Anthropic's commercial terms state that customer inputs and outputs are not used to train models by default, but consumer and commercial plans differ, so confirm which plan the developers are actually on before repeating that to a client. The genuine escalation is operational rather than regulatory: unattended agents with tool access and repository write rights need change-control and audit treatment, and if such an agent is ever pointed at a production system that qualifies as a safety component of critical digital infrastructure, that deployment — not the tool — is the Annex III point 2 question.","docs_url":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/overview","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"cursor","name":"Cursor","vendor":"Anysphere","category":"AI Coding Assistant / AI-Native IDE","walkthrough_category":"dev_tools","prevalence":3,"aliases":["cursor","cursor ide","anysphere","cursor ai","cursor tab","cursor agent"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Cursor Agent and Tab","capabilities":["content_generation","chatbot_assistant","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"A VS Code-derived editor where the AI is the product rather than a plugin: autocomplete, multi-file agentic edits and terminal command execution. Paid per seat on Pro, Teams and Enterprise plans, so it does not appear in an estate unless someone bought it — but individual developers expensing Pro seats is the single most common way this arrives without IT knowing. Admin controls on Teams/Enterprise cover SSO, SCIM, audit logging, MDM deployment and model restrictions.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Minimal risk as an AI Act matter — no profiling of natural persons, no Annex III domain. The real exposure is contractual and data-protection: code, and anything embedded in it (credentials, customer identifiers, personal data in fixtures and logs), is transmitted to Anysphere and onward to model providers. Article 4 literacy applies. It only escalates if the organisation ships an Annex III system built with it, in which case the obligation sits on that system. Treat Cursor primarily as a shadow-IT and source-code-egress finding, and record it in the inventory so the Article 4 training list is complete. On Privacy Mode: Cursor states that with it enabled it will not train on your data, that it is available to free and Pro users alike, and that new team members inherit the team setting — but it does not state that it is on by default for individual accounts. Check it first, and enforce it at team level rather than per user; without it, source code sent for completion and indexing is outside the no-training commitment. That is an IP and GDPR question rather than an AI Act one, but it is the question the client asks.","docs_url":"https://cursor.com/security","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"gitlab-duo","name":"GitLab Duo","vendor":"GitLab","category":"DevOps Platform / AI Coding Assistant","walkthrough_category":"dev_tools","prevalence":3,"aliases":["gitlab duo","duo","duo chat","duo core","duo pro","duo enterprise","duo agent platform","gitlab ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"GitLab Duo Core (Code Suggestions and Duo Chat)","capabilities":["content_generation","chatbot_assistant","summarization"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Duo Core is included with Premium and Ultimate subscriptions rather than sold as an add-on, and GitLab's own documentation states plainly that 'GitLab Duo is on by default'. This is the entry in this half of the catalogue most likely to surprise a customer: a Premium/Ultimate customer who never bought AI probably has code suggestions and Duo Chat live. Top-level group owners on GitLab.com and instance admins on Self-Managed can set availability to Always on / Always off; group owners can override downward. Experimental and beta Duo features stay off until separately enabled.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"GitLab Duo Pro / Duo Enterprise / Duo Self-Hosted","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization","document_analysis","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The deeper capabilities — vulnerability explanation and resolution, merge-request summaries, root-cause analysis, self-hosted model deployment — are paid add-ons requiring an active add-on licence or GitLab Credits. Duo Self-Hosted is the relevant option where a customer will not accept prompts leaving their infrastructure, which in Nordic public-sector work is a common precondition.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"GitLab Duo Agent Platform","capabilities":["content_generation","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Agentic execution of multi-step development tasks, available on GitLab Dedicated and Dedicated for Government per GitLab's docs. Scope and availability have moved repeatedly through 2025-2026; check the version of the docs matching the customer's instance rather than the marketing page.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Minimal risk in normal use; Article 4 literacy applies, and it belongs in the inventory. Worth surfacing precisely because it is on by default — an organisation that has told its regulator or works council 'we do not use generative AI in engineering' may be wrong without knowing it. It does not become high-risk through Annex III on its own. The boundary to watch is the same as for Copilot: if Duo activity data is used to evaluate individual developers, the evaluation layer is Annex III point 4(b), not Duo.","docs_url":"https://docs.gitlab.com/user/gitlab_duo/turn_on_off/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"jetbrains-ai","name":"JetBrains AI Assistant and Junie","vendor":"JetBrains","category":"IDE / AI Coding Assistant and Agent","walkthrough_category":"dev_tools","prevalence":3,"aliases":["jetbrains ai","ai assistant","junie","intellij ai","rider ai","jetbrains ai assistant","jetbrains ai pro"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"JetBrains AI Assistant","capabilities":["content_generation","chatbot_assistant","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Completion, chat, commit-message generation and refactoring inside IntelliJ IDEA, Rider, PyCharm and the rest of the family. The plugin ships with the IDEs and a JetBrains AI subscription tier is bundled with paid IDE subscriptions, so activation is a user action rather than a purchase. JetBrains' AI Terms of Service state it will not use customer inputs, outputs or suggestions to train code- or text-generating models unless the user expressly agrees.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Junie","capabilities":["content_generation","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"JetBrains' autonomous coding agent: it plans and executes multi-step work, makes project-wide edits, runs tests and terminal commands, drives the debugger and calls MCP servers. Requires a JetBrains AI subscription and installation. By default Junie asks for approval before sensitive operations; 'Brave mode' removes that gate, which is the setting to ask about because it turns a supervised assistant into an unsupervised one running shell commands on a developer machine.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Minimal risk; relevant for Article 4 literacy and inventory completeness. The governance gap specific to JetBrains is licence-scoped: organisation administrators can enable or disable JetBrains AI services (AI Assistant and Junie) for the whole company or selected teams, but per JetBrains' own knowledge base that control only applies to users who activated an IDE licence provided by the organisation. Developers running personal JetBrains licences on corporate machines are outside the tenant-wide switch entirely. Ask whether IDE licences are centrally issued before accepting 'we disabled it' as an answer.","docs_url":"https://www.jetbrains.com/help/ai-assistant/junie-agent.html","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"snyk","name":"Snyk","vendor":"Snyk","category":"Application Security / SAST, SCA and AI Code Security","walkthrough_category":"dev_tools","prevalence":3,"aliases":["snyk","deepcode","deepcode ai","snyk code","snyk agent fix","deepcode ai fix"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"DeepCode AI engine (Snyk Code)","capabilities":["document_analysis","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"DeepCode AI is not a toggle sitting beside Snyk Code — it is the scanning engine, combining symbolic analysis, machine learning and generative models. Any customer licensed for Snyk Code is running it, with no separate purchase or switch. Snyk states on its own site that the engine is trained on millions of permissively licensed open-source projects with verified fixes and never on customer data, which is the direct answer to the IP question customers pair with the AI Act one. Note that 'recommendation_profiling' here means profiling code, not persons.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Snyk Agent Fix (DeepCode AI Fix)","capabilities":["content_generation","document_analysis"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Generates security fixes for detected vulnerabilities, marketed at roughly 85% accuracy, surfaced in the IDE and as pull requests. Requires enabling and, in most estates, a decision about whether fixes may be auto-raised against repositories. Human review of the resulting PR is the meaningful control.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Minimal risk. It analyses code, not people, so no Annex III domain applies and it is not an automated decision about a natural person. It belongs in the inventory and in the Article 4 literacy scope for the AppSec team. It is worth cataloguing here rather than under security because the affected population is developers and the failure mode is a bad autofix, not a wrongly scored human. The only boundary worth naming: if Snyk findings are attributed to individual developers and used in performance conversations, the attribution layer — not Snyk — is Annex III point 4(b).","docs_url":"https://snyk.io/platform/deepcode-ai/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"amazon-q-developer","name":"Amazon Q Developer (succeeded by Kiro)","vendor":"Amazon Web Services","category":"AI Coding Assistant / Cloud Developer Tooling","walkthrough_category":"dev_tools","prevalence":2,"aliases":["amazon q developer","q developer","codewhisperer","amazon codewhisperer","kiro","aws q"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Amazon Q Developer IDE plugins and Pro subscription","capabilities":["content_generation","chatbot_assistant","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The renamed successor to Amazon CodeWhisperer: completion, chat, agentic coding and transformation in VS Code, JetBrains and the CLI, licensed per user as Q Developer Pro. AWS has announced this line is being retired — new signups (including Free Tier) were blocked from 15 May 2026 and IDE plugins and paid subscriptions reach end of support on 30 April 2027, a twelve-month transition window. An estate still carrying Q Developer Pro seats has a migration decision, not a steady state.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Amazon Q Developer in the AWS Management Console","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"The console-side assistant, documentation Q&A, console mobile app and Slack/Teams integrations are explicitly stated by AWS to be unaffected by the IDE sunset and remain available. In practice this means AWS account users see a Q assistant in the console without anyone having procured it, which is the inventory entry customers forget. Verify what your specific accounts expose, because the free-tier boundary moved with the Kiro transition.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Kiro","capabilities":["content_generation","chatbot_assistant","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"AWS's replacement agentic IDE, built around spec-driven development — structured specifications, hooks, steering files and custom subagents — and carrying over Q Developer's agentic coding, inline chat and terminal integration. Sold on its own paid tiers. If the client is planning the Q Developer migration, Kiro is the entry that should be in next year's inventory.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Minimal risk; Article 4 literacy and inventory only. The reason to keep this entry despite low risk is lifecycle: this vendor has renamed the same capability three times (CodeWhisperer, then Amazon Q Developer, now Kiro) and is mid-sunset, so any inventory naming it will be wrong within a year. No Annex III domain applies unless the generated system is itself in one.","docs_url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/devops/amazon-q-developer-end-of-support-announcement/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"sourcegraph-amp","name":"Amp (and Sourcegraph Cody Enterprise)","vendor":"Amp Code / Sourcegraph","category":"Code Search / Agentic Coding","walkthrough_category":"dev_tools","prevalence":2,"aliases":["amp","ampcode","sourcegraph","cody","sourcegraph cody","amp code"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Amp","capabilities":["content_generation","chatbot_assistant","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"An agentic coding tool available as CLI, web app and IDE extension, routing across multiple frontier models. Billed as a monthly subscription plus pay-as-you-go usage at provider API cost, with workspaces for shared threads and pooled billing. Enterprise adds SSO (Okta, SAML), managed settings that let an administrator enforce policy, group-scoped thread visibility and per-user cost entitlements. Zero data retention for text inputs in LLM inference is documented as an Enterprise-only control — for anyone below Enterprise, assume prompts containing source are retained by default.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Sourcegraph Cody Enterprise","capabilities":["content_generation","chatbot_assistant","document_analysis"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The original context-aware assistant built on Sourcegraph's code index. Sourcegraph stopped new signups for Cody Free and Cody Pro on 25 June 2025 and shut both down on 23 July 2025, and removed Cody from Enterprise Starter; Cody Enterprise remains supported for Sourcegraph Enterprise customers. Individuals and small teams were migrated to Amp with credit grants. Reporting in late 2025 indicated Amp had been separated from Sourcegraph into an independent company — check which legal entity the customer actually contracts with, since it changes the DPA.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Minimal risk; Article 4 literacy and inventory. The practical finding here is almost never regulatory — it is that a Cody entry in an inventory written in 2024 is now either dead (Free/Pro) or a different product under a possibly different vendor (Amp). Treat data retention as the material control: without the Enterprise zero-retention setting, source code in prompts is retained, which is an IP and confidentiality issue for consultancies and product companies regardless of the AI Act.","docs_url":"https://ampcode.com/manual","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"google-gemini-code-assist","name":"Gemini Code Assist","vendor":"Google Cloud","category":"AI Coding Assistant","walkthrough_category":"dev_tools","prevalence":2,"aliases":["gemini code assist","code assist","duet ai for developers","google code assist","antigravity"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Gemini Code Assist Standard and Enterprise","capabilities":["content_generation","chatbot_assistant","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Completion, chat and agent mode across VS Code, JetBrains IDEs, Cloud Workstations and the Google Cloud console. Standard and Enterprise are paid, per-user editions that a Google Cloud admin must enable and licence; nothing runs for a Google Cloud customer who has not subscribed. Google's docs state the models are trained on publicly available code and Google Cloud material rather than customer code, and both paid editions carry IP indemnification and source citation for generated code — which is the answer to the IP question customers ask alongside the AI Act one.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Code customization (Enterprise)","capabilities":["content_generation","document_analysis"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Enterprise edition can index an organisation's private repositories so suggestions reflect internal libraries and patterns. This is an explicit, admin-configured act of sending private source into a Google-managed index, so it needs its own data-protection sign-off and, for regulated customers, VPC Service Controls scoping. Do not assume it is on merely because Enterprise is licensed.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Minimal risk; Article 4 literacy and inventory only. Branding drift matters more than risk here: Google discontinued the individual tiers (Gemini Code Assist for individuals, and access via Google AI Pro / Ultra) on 18 June 2026 and directs those users to Antigravity and the Antigravity CLI, while Standard and Enterprise continue. If a client's estate documentation says 'Gemini Code Assist free tier', that entry is now stale and the developers have most likely moved to Antigravity, which is a separate product and needs its own inventory line.","docs_url":"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/docs/codeassist/overview","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"microsoft-defender-xdr","name":"Microsoft Defender XDR with Microsoft Security Copilot","vendor":"Microsoft","category":"XDR / SIEM and Security AI Assistant","walkthrough_category":"security_it","prevalence":5,"aliases":["defender","defender xdr","microsoft 365 defender","security copilot","copilot for security","mdo","mde","sentinel","defender for endpoint"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Microsoft Security Copilot (standalone and embedded)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"active","notes":"This is the rare genuine 'active' in the dataset and the single most important finding in this category. Microsoft's own documentation states Security Copilot is included for all eligible Microsoft 365 E5 and E7 customers, provisioned by zero-click activation with no Azure setup and no capacity provisioning, at 400 Security Compute Units per month per 1,000 paid user licences up to 10,000 SCUs. Rollout began 18 November 2025 and continued in phases through 2026. Preselected settings include data-sharing with Microsoft turned off but Security Copilot access to Microsoft 365 service data turned on. An E5 customer who never bought Security Copilot very likely has it live in Defender, Entra, Intune and Purview.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Security Copilot agents in Defender (Phishing Triage Agent, Security Alert Triage Agent, Security Analyst Agent)","capabilities":["summarization","recommendation_profiling","document_analysis","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"The Phishing Triage Agent performs semantic analysis of user-reported emails, URLs and files to classify them as phishing or false alarm, learns from analyst feedback and publishes a decision map for each verdict; it is GA and needs Defender for Office 365 P2. The Security Analyst Agent (public preview, April 2026) runs multi-step investigations across Defender and Sentinel telemetry. Critically, Microsoft's FAQ answers 'Will Security Copilot agents be automatically enabled?' with 'No' — the platform is auto-provisioned but each agent must be deployed by the organisation. That gap between provisioned capacity and deployed agents is where most tenants sit.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Defender XDR machine-learning detection and incident correlation","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Behavioural and ML-based detection in Defender for Endpoint, Office 365, Identity and Cloud Apps, with automatic correlation of alerts into incidents and automated investigation and response. There is no version of these products with the ML switched off; what varies is which workloads are licensed. Defender signals also feed Entra ID Protection risk detections and Purview Insider Risk Management, which is the linkage consultants miss.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"As deployed for security operations this is limited or minimal risk: the subjects of analysis are machines, sessions and messages, and the outputs go to analysts. It is not Annex III on its own. Three boundaries matter. First, Article 4 literacy plainly applies to every SOC analyst acting on Copilot output, and Article 14-style human oversight discipline is good practice even where not legally mandated. Second, Defender for Endpoint device and user activity is a documented signal source for Microsoft Purview Insider Risk Management — the moment that connector is used to evaluate employees, the combined system is Annex III point 4(b), and the Defender licence was the thing that made it possible. Third, where Defender protects the operational technology of an entity in Annex III point 2 (safety components in the management and operation of critical digital infrastructure), assess that deployment specifically rather than the product generally.","docs_url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/copilot/security/security-copilot-inclusion","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"crowdstrike-falcon","name":"CrowdStrike Falcon","vendor":"CrowdStrike","category":"Endpoint Protection / XDR","walkthrough_category":"security_it","prevalence":4,"aliases":["crowdstrike","falcon","charlotte ai","falcon complete","crowdstrike falcon","agentworks"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Falcon sensor machine-learning and behavioural detection","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"On-sensor and cloud machine-learning models plus indicator-of-attack behavioural analytics are the product; every Falcon customer runs them and there is no non-AI Falcon. Marked active for that reason rather than because a vendor calls it AI. The subjects are processes and endpoints, not people, though the telemetry is attributable to a named user account.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Charlotte AI","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Conversational analyst assistant over Falcon data. Licensed as a separate module on a credit model, with a monthly credit cap derived from the number of sensors purchased; the module SKU covers AI Chat, embedded features, Fusion SOAR agentic workflows, AgentWorks, Detection Triage and Agentic Response. A Falcon customer without the Charlotte module has none of it, so confirm the SKU rather than the platform.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Charlotte AI Detection Triage and Agentic Response","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","summarization","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Autonomously triages endpoint, identity and cloud detections and filters false positives before a human sees them; CrowdStrike publishes a claimed 98%+ agreement rate with its own Falcon Complete human analysts. The Response Agent then orchestrates actions across the estate. This is the feature that removes a human from the loop for a class of alerts, so the oversight question is not 'is the AI accurate' but 'what happens to the 2% and who reviews suppressed detections'. AgentWorks, launched at RSA 2026, is a no-code workspace for building custom security agents on models including Anthropic Claude, NVIDIA Nemotron and OpenAI GPT — custom agents built there are the customer's own systems and need their own inventory lines.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Not Annex III as deployed for endpoint security — the analysis targets machines and processes. Article 4 literacy applies to the SOC. Two boundaries: (1) AgentWorks makes the customer a builder of AI systems, and an agent built there that touches HR data, access decisions or employee behaviour inherits whatever tier that use case sits in — inventory each custom agent separately; (2) Falcon Identity Protection scores user risk, which sits on the same line as Entra ID Protection described elsewhere in this catalogue: securing a credential is not evaluating a worker, but exporting those scores into a people process is. Nothing here approaches Article 5 unless someone bolts emotion or sentiment inference onto the telemetry.","docs_url":"https://www.crowdstrike.com/en-us/platform/charlotte-ai/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"microsoft-entra-id-protection","name":"Microsoft Entra ID Protection","vendor":"Microsoft","category":"Identity Security / Risk-Based Access","walkthrough_category":"security_it","prevalence":4,"aliases":["entra id protection","identity protection","azure ad identity protection","aadip","risky users","risky sign-ins","user risk","sign-in risk"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Real-time risk detection and user risk scoring","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Microsoft's documentation states that during each sign-in, ID Protection runs all real-time sign-in detections and generates a session risk level, derived from analysis of trillions of daily signals. Detections include anonymous IP use, password spray, leaked credentials, impossible travel, suspicious inbox rules and mass access to sensitive files. Crucially, the scoring runs regardless of licence tier — Free and P1 tenants still see risky users at medium and high risk, they just lose the detail drawer, risk history and risk detections report. Full reports, Graph API access and alerting need Entra ID P2 or the Entra Suite. So: nearly every Microsoft tenant has a live per-user risk score whether or not anyone bought identity protection.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Risk-based Conditional Access policies","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Policies that require MFA, force a secure password reset, or block access outright when user risk or sign-in risk crosses a threshold, with automatic remediation if the user passes the challenge. Requires P2. Where user remediation is not enabled, an administrator must manually dismiss, confirm safe or confirm compromise — and 'confirm compromise' writes a persistent judgement onto the user object. Tagged automated_decision_making because a machine-derived score can lock a named employee out of their work systems without human involvement.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Security Copilot agents in Entra (Conditional Access Optimization Agent, Access Review Agent)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","summarization","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"The Conditional Access Optimization Agent (GA since July 2025) runs autonomously to find policy gaps and proposes one-click remediations; the Access Review Agent analyses ongoing access reviews and flags unusual patterns. Both are Security Copilot agents, so for M365 E5/E7 tenants the capacity is already provisioned — but Microsoft states agents are not automatically enabled and must be deployed. The Access Review Agent is the one to watch: it influences who keeps access to what, and 'unusual pattern' judgements about a person's access are a short step from judgements about the person.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Be careful and be precise here, because this is where consultants over- and under-call. Blocking a sign-in because a credential looks compromised is an access-control decision about an account: the system is judging the authenticity of a session, not the competence, reliability or conduct of a worker. That is not Annex III point 4(b), and calling it high-risk will cost you credibility. It becomes employment monitoring when the *user*-level artefacts leave the security context: the risky-users report exported to HR, 'confirm compromise' flags cited in disciplinary meetings, risk history used to decide who gets privileged access or who is trusted with sensitive projects, or user risk piped into Purview Insider Risk Management — which Microsoft explicitly supports. At that point the combined pipeline is Annex III point 4(b) and Article 26 deployer duties attach. The practical question for the system owner: who outside the security team can see the risky-users list with names, and has any HR process ever cited it? Separately, GDPR Article 22 is engaged by fully automated blocking even where the AI Act is not.","docs_url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/id-protection/overview-identity-protection","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"palo-alto-cortex","name":"Cortex XSIAM","vendor":"Palo Alto Networks","category":"SecOps Platform / SIEM, SOAR and XDR","walkthrough_category":"security_it","prevalence":3,"aliases":["palo alto","xsiam","cortex xsiam","cortex xdr","agentix","precision ai","panw"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"XSIAM analytics and ML detection models","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Palo Alto's XSIAM product page cites 2,600+ analytics models and 13,300+ detections with ML-powered prioritisation, automated triage and guided response as foundational platform capabilities rather than options. An XSIAM customer is running them. Analysis subjects are hosts, identities and network sessions.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Cortex AgentiX","capabilities":["basic_automation","recommendation_profiling","summarization","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Agentic automation embedded natively in Cortex XSIAM and Cortex Cloud, trained on Palo Alto's corpus of playbook executions; agents plan, reason and act with what the vendor calls enterprise-grade guardrails and a governance model for auditability. Cortex XDR support and a standalone AgentiX platform were announced for early 2026. Because customers author their own agents, each agent is effectively a bespoke AI system: inventory them individually and record what each is permitted to execute without confirmation.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Not Annex III for SOC use. Article 4 literacy applies. The governance weight here sits on AgentiX rather than on the platform: an organisation building its own agents becomes responsible for what they do, and an agent wired into identity, HR or access-provisioning systems inherits the tier of that use case regardless of the fact that it lives in a security console. Naming caution — 'Precision AI' is Palo Alto's umbrella marketing term across its NGFW and Cortex lines and does not appear as a distinct capability on the current XSIAM 3.0 page, so do not record it as a discrete system in an inventory; record the specific feature. XSIAM 3.0 adds exposure management and advanced email security, and identity threat detection is listed as a future extended capability — when ITDR lands, it will score user behaviour and should be assessed against the same account-versus-worker boundary described for Entra ID Protection.","docs_url":"https://www.paloaltonetworks.com/cortex/cortex-xsiam","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"darktrace","name":"Darktrace ActiveAI Security Platform","vendor":"Darktrace","category":"Network Detection and Response / Behavioural AI","walkthrough_category":"security_it","prevalence":3,"aliases":["darktrace","cyber ai analyst","antigena","self-learning ai","activeai","darktrace detect","darktrace respond"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Self-Learning / Adaptive AI behavioural modelling","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Unsupervised machine learning that builds a behavioural baseline — a 'pattern of life' — for every user, device, credential and, now, AI interaction in the environment, then flags deviation. This is the entire product; there is no Darktrace deployment without it. Of all the network and endpoint tools in this catalogue, it is the one that most explicitly models individual human behaviour rather than machine state, and it does so continuously and by default. That distinction is what a consultant should carry into the works-council conversation.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Cyber AI Analyst","capabilities":["summarization","recommendation_profiling","document_analysis"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Autonomously investigates alerts — including alerts from third-party tools — performing what Darktrace describes as Level 1 and Level 2 triage, grouping activity into incidents and writing natural-language narratives with its decision logic. Darktrace markets it as leaving fewer than 4% of investigations needing human review. It ships as part of the platform for most deployments rather than as a separate purchase, but confirm the modules on the order form. 2026 models named on the product page are DEMIST-2 and DIGEST.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Autonomous Response","capabilities":["basic_automation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Takes targeted action to interrupt malicious activity — the capability previously branded Antigena. Most customers run it in human-confirmation mode before granting full autonomy, and which mode is live is the first thing to establish; the product page's marketing describes autonomous action, the deployment reality is often supervised. Actions can block a specific user's connections, so the affected party is a person even when the target is a session.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Not Annex III when used for network security: the purpose is detecting compromise, not evaluating staff. But be precise with clients about why, because the mechanism looks like employee monitoring — it profiles every named user's behaviour continuously and flags 'unusual' activity by that individual. The legal distinction is purpose and use: an anomaly routed to the SOC to investigate a possible compromise is security; the same anomaly routed to a manager as evidence that someone is browsing oddly, working strange hours or exfiltrating files is monitoring and evaluating a worker under Annex III point 4(b). Ask who can read the user-level pattern-of-life views and whether HR has ever been given access. If any Darktrace output is used in a disciplinary process, treat that pathway as the high-risk system. Nothing in the documented feature set infers emotional state, so Article 5(1)(f) is not engaged. Branding note: 'Antigena' is retired, and in 2026 Darktrace uses both 'ActiveAI Security Platform' and 'Behavioral Defense Platform' on its own site.","docs_url":"https://www.darktrace.com/cyber-ai-analyst","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"datadog","name":"Datadog","vendor":"Datadog","category":"Observability / Cloud Monitoring and Security","walkthrough_category":"security_it","prevalence":3,"aliases":["datadog","bits ai","bits","datadog bits","bits ai sre","watchdog"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Bits AI","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization","basic_automation","content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Datadog's agent family, documented as Bits Investigation, Bits Code, Bits Security Analyst, Bits Chat, Bits Data Analysis and Bits Detection. Bits AI SRE reached general availability on 2 December 2025 after testing across more than 2,000 customer environments. Billing runs on Datadog AI Credits stacked on top of existing host, APM, logs and RUM licensing and charged per conclusive investigation, so it is a deliberate spend rather than a bundled feature. Not supported on Datadog's US government sites.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Bits Security Analyst","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Triages security signals from Datadog Cloud SIEM and related products. Subjects are cloud workloads, services and signals rather than people. Worth listing separately from the SRE agent because it is the piece that lands in a security review, and because it draws on the same AI Credits pool — a runaway triage agent is a cost incident before it is a compliance one.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Minimal to limited risk and genuinely undramatic: Datadog's AI reasons over telemetry from machines and services, not about natural persons, and nothing in the documented feature set profiles employees. Article 4 literacy applies to the SRE and platform teams, and it belongs in the inventory for completeness. Two things to check rather than escalate: whether Bits Code can open pull requests against production repositories unattended, and whether logs ingested into Datadog contain personal data that then flows into LLM prompts — which is a GDPR transfer question about the AI vendors behind Bits, not an AI Act tiering question. If Datadog is monitoring systems that are safety components of Annex III point 2 critical digital infrastructure, assess that deployment specifically.","docs_url":"https://docs.datadoghq.com/bits_ai/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"microsoft-purview-insider-risk-management","name":"Microsoft Purview Insider Risk Management","vendor":"Microsoft","category":"Insider Risk / Workforce Behaviour Monitoring","walkthrough_category":"security_it","prevalence":3,"aliases":["insider risk management","irm","purview irm","insider risk","adaptive protection","communication compliance","microsoft purview insider risk"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Insider risk policies and user risk scoring","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","document_analysis"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Scores named employees for risky behaviour using Microsoft 365 and Graph logs plus Defender for Endpoint signals, against templates including Data theft by departing users, Data leaks by risky users, Security policy violations by priority users, Risky AI usage and Risky browser usage. It needs Microsoft 365 E5 Compliance for every user in scope, an admin to create policies, and for several templates an HR connector feeding employment events — resignation, demotion, performance-plan placement — into the scoring. Users are pseudonymised by default and there are role-based controls and an audit log, but pseudonymisation is reversible by an authorised reviewer. Forensic evidence capture can additionally record screen activity on the endpoint. This is a genuine high-risk candidate hiding inside a compliance licence, and the licence entitlement is far more widespread than the deployment.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Insider risk analytics","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Microsoft's own words: this evaluates potential insider risks in the organisation 'without configuring any insider risk policies'. It is a one-click opt-in that produces an organisation-wide picture of where risky activity is concentrated, before any policy, notice or works-council process exists. Compliance admins switch it on to scope a business case, which means the first behavioural analysis of the workforce frequently happens before anyone has told the workforce.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Adaptive Protection","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Links a user's insider risk level to DLP policy severity so that controls tighten automatically as an individual's score rises — a machine-assigned behavioural score directly changing what that named person is permitted to do with company data, with no human in the loop. Requires E5 Compliance. This is the clearest automated decision about a worker anywhere in the Microsoft security stack.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Triage Agent in Insider Risk Management","capabilities":["summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"A Security Copilot agent that triages insider risk alerts ahead of a human reviewer, with its own dashboard alongside the standard alert dashboard. For M365 E5/E7 tenants the Security Copilot capacity is already auto-provisioned, so enabling this is a deployment step rather than a purchase. An AI pre-filtering which employees get investigated is precisely the human-oversight question Article 14 exists for.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"Say this plainly to the client: continuous behavioural scoring of employees is Annex III point 4(b) — AI systems intended to monitor and evaluate the behaviour of persons in a work-related contractual relationship. IRM is not a borderline case dressed up for effect; it ingests HR events, scores named individuals, escalates them to investigation, and through Adaptive Protection changes what they are allowed to do based on that score. Deploy it and you are a deployer of a high-risk system: Article 26 obligations (human oversight by competent people, input-data relevance, log retention, and Article 26(7) — informing workers and their representatives *before* putting it into service), and Article 27 fundamental rights impact assessment if you are a public body or otherwise in scope. In Germany, the Netherlands and across the Nordics the works-council and GDPR Article 88 process must complete before any of this, and it is usually the harder gate. On Article 5: IRM as documented infers behaviour, not emotion, so it is not a prohibition on its face — but the Communication Compliance integration that brings users into scope classifies 'threatening, harassing, or discriminatory language', which is adjacent to affect analysis, and bolting any mood, stress or sentiment inference onto this pipeline turns a high-risk question into an Article 5(1)(f) prohibition question with Article 99(3) penalties. Two things to check on every engagement: whether E5 Compliance is licensed (the entitlement often exists unnoticed), and whether insider risk analytics has ever been run.","docs_url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/purview/insider-risk-management","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"proofpoint","name":"Proofpoint (Nexus, Zen, Satori)","vendor":"Proofpoint","category":"Email Security / Human-Centric Security","walkthrough_category":"security_it","prevalence":3,"aliases":["proofpoint","nexus","proofpoint nexus","very attacked people","vap","proofpoint zen","satori","tap","proofpoint essentials"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Proofpoint Nexus detection models","capabilities":["spam_filter","document_analysis","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Proofpoint describes Nexus as its portfolio of AI, machine learning and real-time threat intelligence detection models, applied pre-delivery, post-delivery and at click time across the attack chain, including large language models for intent detection. There is no Proofpoint deployment with Nexus switched off — it is the detection engine — so any Proofpoint customer is running AI content analysis over all inbound and, in many configurations, internal and outbound mail.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"People-risk insights and Very Attacked People","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Proofpoint's people-centric model scores individual employees for attack exposure and risk, surfacing 'Very Attacked People' and building individual risk profiles that incorporate behavioural analysis and Microsoft 365 signals. This is per-person scoring inside a security product, and it is available to most customers with the relevant modules without a separate AI purchase. It measures how targeted and how susceptible a named employee is — not their performance — but it is a personal profile and it names individuals to security and often to their managers.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Proofpoint Zen and Satori agents","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","basic_automation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Zen is the end-user-facing layer that warns and nudges employees in the moment; Satori is Proofpoint's AI-agent layer that automates security tasks and offers MCP access for integration with tools such as Microsoft Copilot and CrowdStrike Charlotte. Both are newer platform additions rather than baseline entitlements — confirm which modules the customer actually holds, because Proofpoint's packaging changed substantially in 2025-2026.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Email filtering is minimal risk and is not Annex III. The entry worth a conversation is people-risk scoring. Scoring an employee's exposure to attack, and in some configurations their susceptibility as evidenced by clicking simulated phish, is profiling of a natural person in an employment context. It is not automatically Annex III point 4(b), because the purpose is protecting the person rather than evaluating them — but the boundary is thin and it is crossed the moment those scores are used for anything with a consequence for the individual: mandatory training assignments tracked by manager, access restrictions, or appearing in a performance conversation. Ask directly whether phishing-simulation failure rates or VAP scores are ever reported to line managers by name. Also note Article 50 transparency: Zen's end-user nudges are AI-generated content shown to a natural person, so users should be told what is generating them.","docs_url":"https://www.proofpoint.com/us/platform","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"sentinelone-singularity","name":"SentinelOne Singularity Platform","vendor":"SentinelOne","category":"Endpoint Protection / XDR and AI SIEM","walkthrough_category":"security_it","prevalence":3,"aliases":["sentinelone","s1","singularity","purple ai","singularity complete","athena","sentinel one"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Singularity agent static and behavioural AI engines","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"On-agent static and behavioural machine-learning detection with autonomous kill, quarantine and rollback. As with Falcon, this is the product rather than an optional AI layer — every Singularity customer runs it. Subjects are processes and endpoints.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Purple AI (Athena release)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"SentinelOne states Purple AI's core capabilities — natural-language hunting, accelerated investigation, auto-reporting — are now included in Singularity Complete rather than sold separately, which is a meaningful change from the earlier add-on model. The Athena release (2026) adds deep security reasoning, Auto-Triage (GA), Auto-Investigations, novel detection-rule creation and data-source-agnostic connection to third-party SIEMs including Splunk. Agentic investigation consumption draws on Singularity Credits. Treat it as bundled-but-must-be-turned-on: a Complete customer has the entitlement, the SOC still has to switch it on and set guardrails.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Singularity Hyperautomation closed-loop response","capabilities":["basic_automation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Purple AI reaches a verdict on an alert and Hyperautomation executes the response within limits the security team sets. This is the autonomy boundary: the customer defines the limits, so the governance artefact you need is the written scope of what the agent may execute unattended, plus the audit trail. Capability tagged automated_decision_making because it acts on systems and can lock out accounts, affecting a person's ability to work — even though the decision target is a machine state.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Not Annex III for endpoint and cloud security work. Article 4 literacy applies. The concrete escalation is autonomous containment: when Hyperautomation disables an account or isolates a device without human confirmation, a person is prevented from working by an automated process. That is not high-risk under Annex III — it is an operational control decision, not an evaluation of the worker — but it is a GDPR Article 22 conversation and a works-council conversation in Nordic and German entities, and it needs a documented rollback and appeal path. It would only become Annex III point 4(b) if the record of who triggers containments were used to assess employees.","docs_url":"https://www.sentinelone.com/platform/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"splunk-enterprise-security","name":"Splunk Enterprise Security","vendor":"Splunk (Cisco)","category":"SIEM / Security Analytics","walkthrough_category":"security_it","prevalence":3,"aliases":["splunk","splunk es","splunk enterprise security","splunk ai assistant","splunk uba","splunk ueba","mltk","machine learning toolkit","cisco splunk"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Splunk AI Assistant in Enterprise Security","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Generates SPL from natural language, summarises investigations and drafts reports inside the ES workflow. Splunk lists AI Assistant in the Essentials edition of Enterprise Security alongside SIEM, threat intelligence, Detection Studio and Exposure Analytics — so most ES customers have the entitlement, but it is a console feature the team switches on rather than something running silently.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Splunk UEBA (user and entity behaviour analytics)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Machine learning that baselines individual users and entities and scores anomalies and behavioural change to surface insider and advanced threats. Splunk places UEBA in the Premier edition, together with SOAR and Automated Threat Analysis, so an Essentials customer does not have it. This is the feature in the Splunk stack that scores people rather than machines, and it should be treated as a distinct governance item from the SIEM itself.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Risk-Based Alerting and ML detections","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"RBA attributes risk scores to users and systems and only raises an alert when accumulated risk crosses a threshold; Splunk claims up to 90% noise reduction. It is configured by the detection engineering team, not shipped on. Note that RBA is literally a running risk score attached to a named employee, which is a fact worth stating plainly to a data protection officer.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Splunk AI Toolkit (formerly Machine Learning Toolkit)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"A downloadable app for Splunk Enterprise and Splunk Cloud giving SPL machine-learning commands, 30+ built-in algorithms, 300+ open-source algorithms via the Python for Scientific Computing library, and guided assistants including LLM integrations for extraction, summarisation and anomaly detection. Anyone with the right role can install it and build models against any indexed data — including HR data if it happens to be in Splunk. That is an ungoverned model-building capability sitting inside a security tool, and it is worth asking who has installed it.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"The SIEM itself is not Annex III. UEBA is the entry that needs care and it is why this product has an 'employment' domain on one feature rather than none at all: it builds behavioural baselines for named employees and scores deviation. Used purely to detect account compromise and routed only to the SOC, that remains security. Used to identify 'risky employees', feed HR investigations, or rank staff by risk, it is monitoring and evaluating the behaviour of workers under Annex III point 4(b) and pulls Article 26 deployer duties — human oversight, worker information, logging — plus a GDPR Article 88 and works-council process that in the Nordics has to happen first. The AI Toolkit is a separate governance concern: it lets teams build models on any indexed data with no central review. Branding drift: Cisco is restructuring the portfolio (ES Essentials/Premier editions, Cisco Data Fabric, and an AI Canvas integration slated for 2026), so Splunk AI naming will move again.","docs_url":"https://www.splunk.com/en_us/products/enterprise-security.html","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"elastic-security","name":"Elastic Security","vendor":"Elastic","category":"SIEM / Security Analytics","walkthrough_category":"security_it","prevalence":2,"aliases":["elastic","elastic security","elk","elastic siem","attack discovery","elastic ai assistant","agent builder"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Elastic AI Assistant for Security","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Chat-based alert investigation, query generation and remediation suggestions. Requires an Enterprise subscription on the self-managed stack, or an Elastic Security Serverless project on the EASE or Security Analytics Complete tier. An administrator must create a generative AI connector before it works, though Elastic Managed LLMs are available without manual key management; alternatively customers point it at OpenAI, Azure OpenAI, Amazon Bedrock or Google Vertex AI. It is not on by default — a user has to open it. Note that from version 9.4 Agent Builder became the default chat experience, so which assistant is live depends on the stack version.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Attack Discovery","capabilities":["summarization","recommendation_profiling","document_analysis"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Uses an LLM to correlate alerts across users, hosts and time into discrete attack chains, on demand or on a schedule, with notification to Slack, Teams, PagerDuty or email. Requires the same licence tier and a configured LLM connector. Because it can run on a schedule and push conclusions to a channel, it is the Elastic feature most likely to be generating AI output that nobody in the SOC has consciously reviewed.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Agent Builder and Automatic Migration","capabilities":["basic_automation","content_generation","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Agent Builder lets teams create custom agents over Elastic data and is the default chat experience from 9.4, including a built-in threat-hunting agent that draws on alerts, Attack Discovery output and entity risk scores. Automatic Migration uses AI to translate Splunk detections and dashboards into Elastic format — useful, and also a case where AI is silently rewriting detection logic that the security team owns; the translated rules need review, not acceptance.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Not Annex III for SIEM use, and the licensing gate plus mandatory LLM connector means it is rarely running unnoticed. Article 4 literacy applies. Two boundaries: Elastic's entity risk scoring assigns risk to users as well as hosts, which puts it on the same account-versus-worker line as Splunk UEBA and Entra ID Protection — security use is fine, HR use is Annex III point 4(b). And because the customer chooses the LLM connector, the data-transfer analysis depends on which provider they picked; a self-hosted or Elastic-managed model and an Azure OpenAI deployment are very different GDPR answers, and the customer, not Elastic, made that choice.","docs_url":"https://www.elastic.co/docs/solutions/security/ai","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"teramind","name":"Teramind","vendor":"Teramind","category":"Employee Monitoring / User Activity Monitoring and Insider Risk","walkthrough_category":"security_it","prevalence":2,"aliases":["teramind","uam","user activity monitoring","employee monitoring","omni","teramind omni","insider threat monitoring","activtrak","veriato"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"OMNI insider risk scoring and behavioural baseline drift","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"OMNI is Teramind's AI layer over its activity capture: it quantifies each employee's risk level from behavioural patterns, builds a per-user baseline and flags drift over time, and presents it as a feed of people worth looking at. It is the platform's primary interface rather than a separately purchased module, so a customer who deployed Teramind for 'productivity' is running behavioural risk scoring on staff. Confirm the tier, but assume it is on.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Worker text toxicity analysis / employee sentiment analysis","capabilities":["emotion_recognition","document_analysis","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"The OMNI product page describes scoring 'worker text toxicity' across chat, email and internal communication platforms in order to surface issues before they escalate, and Teramind publishes a dedicated Employee Sentiment Analysis solution. Whether it is active depends on which communication channels are under monitoring. Tagged emotion_recognition deliberately: inferring tone, hostility or sentiment from an employee's messages is affect inference about a natural person at work, whatever the vendor calls it. Do not let 'it's just toxicity detection' close this conversation.","domains":["employment"]},{"feature_name":"Behavioural authenticity modelling","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Builds a model of each user from subconscious interaction patterns — shift-key usage, typing nuances, mouse movement — to detect when someone other than the account holder is at the keyboard. Keystroke dynamics used to distinguish one individual from another is behavioural biometric processing; under GDPR Article 4(14) it is biometric data when processed for unique identification, and it triggers Article 9. Most customers do not realise the productivity tool they bought is doing continuous biometric verification of their staff.","domains":["employment","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Productivity analysis","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Classifies applications, websites and time as productive or unproductive per employee and reports active, idle and focus time, engagement and performance impact to managers. Plain workforce performance evaluation, delivered as a dashboard.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["employment"],"escalation_note":"This is the bluntest entry in the catalogue and should be treated that way. Continuous behavioural scoring of employees is Annex III point 4(b) — monitoring and evaluating the behaviour and performance of workers — so a deployer takes on Article 26 duties including human oversight, logging, and informing workers and their representatives before the system goes into service, plus Article 27 FRIA where in scope. The sentiment and toxicity analysis is worse than high-risk: Article 5(1)(f) prohibits AI systems that infer the emotions of a natural person in the workplace, with only medical and safety exceptions, and inferring tone or hostility from employee messages is the paradigm case the drafters had in mind. That is a prohibition, not a tier, and it carries the Article 99(3) penalty band of up to EUR 35 million or 7% of worldwide turnover. Behavioural biometrics for identity verification adds Annex III point 1 and GDPR Article 9 on top. In Nordic, German and Dutch entities none of this is lawful without works-council agreement and a DPIA regardless of the AI Act, and that is usually where the engagement should start. The same analysis applies to the near-substitutes: ActivTrak markets an AI Coach that scores productivity patterns, workload balance and burnout risk, and Veriato's own product page describes generative-AI risk scoring across 130+ data points including email and messaging content, keystrokes, screenshots and 'sentiment and language analysis' of communication tone — Veriato is the most explicit emotion-inference case of the three. Establish which product is actually deployed and which modules are licensed before writing the finding.","docs_url":"https://www.teramind.co/solutions/omni/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"creditsafe","name":"Creditsafe","vendor":"Creditsafe Group","category":"Credit Bureau (predominantly B2B)","walkthrough_category":"essential_services","prevalence":3,"aliases":["creditsafe","credit safe","creditsafe risk score","creditsafe consumer credit report","creditsafe international score"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Creditsafe Risk Score and International Score","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"active","notes":"A 0-100 score predicting the likelihood of a company failing within 12 months, plus an A-E International Score for cross-border comparison. Creditsafe states it applies machine-learning techniques to new and alternative data sources for scoring accuracy, while its published scorecard-methodology notes speak more conservatively of 'state-of-the-art statistical and modelling techniques' — treat the ML claim as marketing-grade until the customer gets model documentation. Returned on every report; no toggle. Crucially this scores *companies*, not people.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Consumer credit reports and consumer credit score (UK)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"active","notes":"A separate UK product that credit-checks a named individual using electoral roll, address history, search history, CCJs and bankruptcies, and returns a consumer credit score used to decide whether to extend credit or open an account. Creditsafe explicitly states the score uses only factual information and never gender, religion, race or ethnic origin — useful evidence for a fairness assessment but not a substitute for one. No AI or ML branding appears on the consumer product page, so the Article 3(1) question is live. Availability is market-specific: the consumer product is a UK offering, whereas most Creditsafe country instances (including the Nordic ones) are business-only.","domains":["essential_services"]}],"typical_domains":["essential_services"],"escalation_note":"This is the entry where the natural-person line matters most. Creditsafe's core business is B2B: scoring limited companies for trade credit and supplier risk, which is entirely outside Annex III point 5 because point 5 concerns natural persons only — a supplier-vetting workflow needs no high-risk treatment. The UK consumer credit report is the exception and is point 5(b) territory where it is used to decide whether an individual gets credit or a service. Two grey zones to raise explicitly with the client: sole traders and unincorporated businesses, where the 'company' being scored is legally a natural person; and personal guarantor checks attached to a corporate facility. If the customer uses Creditsafe purely for procurement and trade-credit limits on incorporated counterparties, tell them plainly it is out of scope rather than letting a bureau name trigger a false positive.","docs_url":"https://www.creditsafe.com/gb/en/credit-risk/credit-reports/consumer-credit-reports.html","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"dun-bradstreet-risk-analytics","name":"D&B Risk Analytics","vendor":"Dun & Bradstreet","category":"Business Information / Third-Party Risk (absorbed Bisnode)","walkthrough_category":"essential_services","prevalence":3,"aliases":["dnb","d&b","dun and bradstreet","dun & bradstreet","bisnode","d&b ask procurement","d&b risk analytics","duns"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"D&B Ask Procurement","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization","document_analysis"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Generative AI chat assistant built with IBM watsonx Orchestrate and watsonx.ai, sitting on the D&B Data Cloud (580m+ entities). It answers natural-language questions about supplier financials, ESG, beneficial ownership, legal filings and risk events, generates risk summaries and compares suppliers. It profiles *organisations*, not people — the only individuals it touches incidentally are beneficial owners and directors surfaced in ownership data. Sold as an add-on to D&B Risk Analytics – Supplier Intelligence, not included in a base data subscription.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Deliberately left empty, and that is the finding. Annex III point 5 applies to natural persons, and every AI feature D&B publishes on its own domain is aimed at assessing companies and supply chains — supplier risk, compliance and third-party due diligence. Do not let 'credit bureau' as a category trigger a high-risk flag here by reflex. Two caveats to put to the client. First, beneficial-ownership and adverse-media screening does profile named individuals; that is a GDPR and AMLR problem more than an Annex III one, but if the output ever decides whether a person gets onboarded as a customer it moves toward point 5. Second, D&B absorbed Bisnode in 2020 and inherited its Nordic consumer data holdings — D&B Sweden markets data on roughly 17 million Nordic consumers and Nordic lenders do buy person-level credit reports from it as an alternative to UC. I could not verify on D&B's own domain that any AI or ML model sits behind that Nordic consumer score, so I have not listed it as an AI feature; ask the Nordic account team directly, because if it is model-driven it is point 5(b) high-risk and it will not be in anyone's inventory.","docs_url":"https://www.dnb.com/en-us/products/dnb-ask-procurement.html","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"enento-uc-asiakastieto","name":"Enento Credit Information Services (UC, Asiakastieto)","vendor":"Enento Group","category":"Credit Bureau (Nordic)","walkthrough_category":"essential_services","prevalence":3,"aliases":["uc","uc ab","uc risk","asiakastieto","suomen asiakastieto","enento","allabolag","proff","kreditupplysning","luottotiedot","personupplysning"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Consumer credit information and scoring (UC Risk / consumer credit classification)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"active","notes":"The Nordic bureau product: a report and a risk classification on a named natural person, built from payment defaults, debt-collection records, income and address history, and consumed by banks, telcos, landlords and BNPL providers as a gate on the application. Enento states at group level that AI and machine learning are applied across its data production and that it develops proprietary scoring models, but neither UC's nor Asiakastieto's public consumer pages brand the score as 'AI' — do not expect the vendor to volunteer that it is a model. Rated 'active' because the classification is returned on every lookup; there is nothing to switch on. This is the highest-volume automated assessment of natural persons in the Nordics and it is almost never in a customer's AI inventory.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Advanced analytics, decisioning and monitoring services","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Enento sells scoring, decisioning and monitoring on top of the raw report, including Decision-as-a-Service style offerings in Finland, where lenders outsource the credit decision logic itself rather than just buying data. Continuous monitoring re-evaluates an existing customer and pushes alerts when their risk profile changes, which is profiling without a triggering application. Bought as a separate engagement. Ask specifically whether the customer buys a score, a decision, or both — the answer changes who is the provider.","domains":["essential_services"]}],"typical_domains":["essential_services"],"escalation_note":"Squarely Annex III point 5(b): establishing the credit score of natural persons. Two Nordic-specific traps. First, the client will often say 'we just buy a credit check, there is no AI' — Article 3(1) turns on whether the system infers outputs from input, not on whether the vendor uses the word AI, so a statistically-derived risk classification needs assessing on its merits; get the model documentation from Enento in writing. Second, the same lookup is routinely used by landlords and telcos, and a refusal of housing or a mobile contract on the back of the classification engages point 5 as access to essential private services even where no credit is extended. Enento is the provider of the score; the bank, landlord or telco is the deployer. Enento's company/business information (allabolag, Proff, business credit ratings) is outside point 5 because it concerns legal persons. Enento reorganised into country segments (Finland / Sweden / Norway-Denmark) in Q1 2026, so product naming differs by market.","docs_url":"https://enento.com/enento-group-in-brief/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"equifax-ignite","name":"Equifax Ignite","vendor":"Equifax","category":"Credit Bureau / Analytics & Decisioning Platform","walkthrough_category":"essential_services","prevalence":3,"aliases":["equifax","ignite","equifax ignite","interconnect","equifax interconnect","neurodecision","ndt","efx.ai","amplify ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"NeuroDecision Technology (NDT)","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Equifax's patented explainable-AI credit scoring method: a neural network constrained to be monotonic so that the score and the adverse-action reason codes come out of the same model. It exists precisely so a lender can decline a person using a neural net and still tell them why. Delivered inside specific Equifax scores rather than sold standalone — you buy the score, you get NDT. This is the single best example in the market of a bureau shipping a neural network into a consumer credit decision, and it should be treated as Annex III high-risk on sight.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Equifax Ignite","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Analytics workbench combining Equifax bureau data, the customer's internal data and third-party/alternative data (utilities, telco, wealth signals) for model development and portfolio analysis. Customers build predictive models here and then push them into InterConnect for execution. Equifax states that 100% of its new models and scores are now built using AI or ML, so 'is it a model or a rule' is rarely a real question with Equifax attributes. Separate subscription; alternative-data attributes are usually further add-ons.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Equifax InterConnect","capabilities":["automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The decisioning execution layer: hosts the rules and models and returns real-time approve/decline/limit outcomes on live consumer applications. Functionally Equifax's answer to Experian PowerCurve. It is where an Ignite-built ML model stops being an experiment and starts deciding whether a person gets a loan. Ask which models are currently deployed, when each was last validated, and whether any run in shadow mode.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Fraud risk scoring (Modern FraudCheck / Global Fraud Risk Score)","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"AI-driven fraud probability and identity trust scoring applied at application and at other customer touchpoints, blending device, identity and bureau signals. It can stop an application dead before any creditworthiness assessment happens, which means a person can be refused a bank account by a model that never assessed their credit at all. Separately licensed. Names have moved around regionally — confirm the exact SKU in the contract rather than trusting the marketing name.","domains":["essential_services"]}],"typical_domains":["essential_services"],"escalation_note":"NeuroDecision-backed scores and any model deployed through InterConnect fall under Annex III point 5(b) — creditworthiness evaluation and credit scoring of natural persons. The fraud scores are a genuinely different case: point 5(b) expressly excepts AI systems used for the purpose of detecting financial fraud, so those sit outside that limb, but the carve-out is narrow and does not survive if the fraud score is also used as a credit-risk input or to justify de-risking a customer. Equifax is the provider of its own scores; the lender is the deployer and owes Article 26 duties including human oversight and informing the affected person. Equifax's commercial/business scoring (a separate database and separate scorecards) is outside point 5 because it assesses companies. Equifax's own explainability push is useful evidence but is not a substitute for the deployer's Article 13/26 documentation.","docs_url":"https://www.equifax.com.au/knowledge-hub/news-and-media/how-equifax-uses-ai","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"experian-ascend-platform","name":"Experian Ascend Platform","vendor":"Experian","category":"Credit Bureau / Analytics & Decisioning Platform","walkthrough_category":"essential_services","prevalence":3,"aliases":["experian","ascend","ascend platform","ascend intelligence services","ascend sandbox","experian assistant","ais"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Ascend Intelligence Services","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Experian's managed AI/ML analytics service: Experian data scientists build custom scorecards and ML models on bureau plus client data and deploy them straight into the customer's decisioning flow. The output is a score or an accept/decline/limit recommendation attached to a named natural person applying for credit. Sold as a service engagement on top of the Ascend subscription, not something an admin flips on. Ask the system owner which specific scorecards are ML-derived versus classic logistic regression — that distinction changes the Article 3(1) analysis and the documentation burden.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Ascend ML Builder / Analytical Sandbox","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Point-and-click and notebook environment where the customer's own analysts build, train and test predictive models on de-identified bureau data before promoting them to production via Ascend Ops. This makes the customer the *provider* of any model they build here, not merely a deployer — a distinction that decides who owns the Annex III obligations. Separate SKU from the core bureau data feed. Consultants should ask whether models built in the sandbox are ever put in front of live consumer applications.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Experian Assistant","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"GenAI natural-language interface over the Ascend analytics environment, aimed at the customer's analysts rather than at consumers. It answers questions about portfolios and helps assemble model attributes; it does not itself issue a credit decision. Requires the Ascend Platform and admin enablement. Low direct Annex III exposure, but it is a route by which unreviewed model logic reaches a production strategy, so check change-control.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Agent Operating System","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","summarization","basic_automation"],"default_state":"in_exploration","notes":"Announced June 2026 as a core capability of the Ascend Platform: an orchestration and trust layer letting Experian, client and partner AI agents co-operate across acquisition, fraud, credit decisioning, portfolio monitoring and regulatory reporting. Experian markets human-in-the-loop validation for high-impact decisions, which implies the default posture is agent-proposes/human-approves — verify that per workflow rather than believing the brochure. Very new; most European estates will not have it in production in 2026. If agents are ever allowed to close a credit decision without a human, that workflow is squarely Annex III point 5(b).","domains":["essential_services"]}],"typical_domains":["essential_services"],"escalation_note":"Annex III point 5(b) covers AI systems intended to evaluate the creditworthiness of natural persons or establish their credit score — that is exactly what Ascend Intelligence Services and customer-built scorecards do, so those are high-risk. Point 5(b) carries an explicit carve-out for AI used for the purpose of detecting financial fraud, so the fraud-scoring parts of Ascend fall outside that limb (they may still be caught elsewhere, and by GDPR Article 22). Note the provider/deployer split: where Experian builds and sells the model, Experian is the provider and the bank is the deployer with Article 26 duties; where the bank builds its own model in Ascend ML Builder, the bank becomes the provider and takes on Articles 9-15 in full. B2B scoring of companies is out of scope entirely — point 5 concerns natural persons.","docs_url":"https://www.experian.com/business/products/ascend","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"experian-powercurve","name":"Experian PowerCurve","vendor":"Experian","category":"Credit Decisioning Engine","walkthrough_category":"essential_services","prevalence":3,"aliases":["powercurve","power curve","powercurve originations","powercurve customer management","powercurve collections","pco","experian decisioning"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"PowerCurve Originations","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active","notes":"The application-decisioning module: it ingests the application, pulls bureau and open-banking data, runs the lender's strategy and returns accept / decline / refer plus limit and price, in real time and usually with no human in the loop for straight-through cases. Notably, Experian's own UK PowerCurve pages describe it as a component-based decision engine and do NOT market it as AI or ML — the intelligence sits in the scorecards the lender deploys into it. Rated 'active' because in any deployment the automated decisioning is the product; there is no AI toggle to leave off. Ask the system owner which scorecards are loaded and how each was derived.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"PowerCurve Customer Management and Collections","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Post-origination modules that re-score existing customers to drive credit-limit increases and decreases, retention offers, and collections treatment paths including 'personalised fair treatment' segmentation of people in arrears. These are decisions about a named individual's continued access to credit, made on a schedule rather than on application. Collections segmentation is often overlooked in AI Act scoping because nobody thinks of it as 'scoring', but a model that routes a person into a harsher recovery path is profiling with legal effect. Included in the module licence; runs continuously once configured.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"PowerCurve Strategy Management","capabilities":["basic_automation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Design-studio and champion/challenger environment where risk teams author, simulate and publish decision strategies without IT. Its relevance to compliance is that it is the change-control surface: a strategy edit here silently alters how thousands of people are treated. Requires the Pro/self-serve tier. Consultants should ask to see the approval workflow and version history for strategy publishes — this is normally the weakest evidence in an Article 26 file.","domains":["essential_services"]}],"typical_domains":["essential_services"],"escalation_note":"PowerCurve is the classic Annex III point 5(b) system: it establishes creditworthiness outcomes for natural persons. The interesting boundary is Article 3(1) — a pure deterministic rules tree arguably is not an 'AI system' at all, while an ML-derived scorecard executing inside the same engine plainly is. In practice almost every real PowerCurve installation embeds statistically-derived scorecards, so assume in scope and make the lender prove otherwise. The lender, not Experian, is normally the provider of the strategy it authored, even though Experian provides the engine. SME and corporate lending strategies running in the same instance are out of point 5 scope because they assess companies, not natural persons — but a sole trader or personal guarantor is a natural person, so mixed portfolios need line-by-line scoping.","docs_url":"https://www.experian.co.uk/business/platforms/powercurve","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"fico-platform","name":"FICO Platform","vendor":"FICO","category":"Decisioning & Analytics Platform / Fraud Analytics","walkthrough_category":"essential_services","prevalence":3,"aliases":["fico","fico platform","blaze advisor","fico blaze","falcon fraud manager","fico falcon","fico decision management"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI & Analytics (interpretable machine learning)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"FICO's modelling layer, deliberately positioned around interpretable ML rather than raw accuracy because its customers must produce reason codes in regulated lending. It produces the scores that the Decisioning layer then acts on, across origination, credit-limit management and collections. Licensed as a platform capability, and in practice implemented with FICO or partner professional services. Ask which models are FICO-supplied versus client-built, because that determines who is the AI Act provider.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Decisioning (including FICO Blaze Advisor)","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Blaze Advisor is FICO's long-standing business rules management system — decision trees, decision tables, scorecards and graphical decision flows — and remains the rules-authoring workhorse inside FICO Platform decisioning. On its own it is deterministic rules, which raises a real Article 3(1) question about whether it is an 'AI system' at all; in practice it almost always executes ML-derived scorecards, which settles the question. It is the layer that turns a score into an accept, a decline, a price or a limit for a named person. Separate licence from the analytics layer in most estates.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Falcon fraud models and the Falcon Intelligence Network","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Neural-network fraud models trained on consortium data contributed by over 10,000 financial institutions, scoring card, debit, commercial card, digital payment and authorised-push-payment transactions in milliseconds and triggering declines, step-up authentication and case creation. The consortium design means a European cardholder is scored by a model trained largely on other institutions' data — a data-governance and Article 10 question worth raising. FICO markets explainable AI and auditability on these models. Falcon is normally a distinct licence from the rest of FICO Platform even where deployed on the same architecture.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Contextual Profiles and Optimization","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Contextual Profiles maintain a continuously updated behavioural profile of each customer from streaming transaction data; Optimization applies mathematical solvers to choose the best action per person subject to portfolio constraints — for example how to allocate credit limit increases across a book. This is profiling of natural persons with direct financial consequence, and it runs without any application event. Licensed per capability. Worth asking whether optimisation objectives have ever been tested for disparate outcomes across protected groups.","domains":["essential_services"]}],"typical_domains":["essential_services"],"escalation_note":"Credit origination, limit-setting and collections decisioning on FICO Platform is Annex III point 5(b) high-risk — creditworthiness evaluation and credit scoring of natural persons. Falcon is the textbook illustration of the point 5(b) carve-out: the limb expressly excludes AI systems used for the purpose of detecting financial fraud, so pure transaction-fraud scoring is not high-risk under that heading. The carve-out is purpose-bound, not product-bound: the moment a Falcon-derived risk signal is fed into a creditworthiness model, or used to close accounts and de-risk customers, the exclusion no longer covers that use and you are back in point 5(b), with GDPR Article 22 running alongside throughout. Note also that FICO is the provider of Falcon consortium models and of its packaged scores, whereas a bank authoring its own Blaze strategies and scorecards is the provider of those — a single FICO installation routinely contains both, and the AI Act roles differ per model, not per system.","docs_url":"https://www.fico.com/en/fico-platform","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"guidewire-insurancesuite","name":"Guidewire InsuranceSuite","vendor":"Guidewire Software","category":"Insurance Core System (P&C)","walkthrough_category":"essential_services","prevalence":3,"aliases":["guidewire","insurancesuite","policycenter","claimcenter","billingcenter","guidewire predict","claims intel","industry intel","pronavigator","jutro","qusar"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Guidewire Predict and Industry Intel","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Predictive scoring built on Guidewire's pooled Industry Intel dataset (Guidewire cites $326bn+ of industry data) and surfaced directly inside PolicyCenter and ClaimCenter so the score reaches the underwriter or adjuster at the point of decision rather than in a separate dashboard. It drives risk selection and pricing on P&C business and includes property-level hazard data. Separately licensed analytics on top of the core subscription. The pooled-data design means a policyholder is scored partly by other carriers' loss experience — an Article 10 data-governance question.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Claims Intel","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"AI/ML models in ClaimCenter that score claims for severity, litigation propensity and reserve adequacy, with Guidewire publishing loss-ratio improvements in Auto Bodily Injury, Collision and Workers' Compensation. These models influence how an individual claimant's claim is triaged, reserved and settled. Add-on to ClaimCenter. Worth asking whether any model output is visible to the adjuster as a settlement anchor, which is where fairness concerns concentrate.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Agentic Framework, Developer Assistants and Agentic FNOL","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","chatbot_assistant","content_generation","summarization","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Shipped in the Qusar cloud release (2026): a model-agnostic framework for building and governing AI agents with native access to Guidewire systems, AI coding assistants for Gosu, Jutro and integrations, context-aware claim summarisation for adjusters, and Agentic FNOL — a voice-driven intake that walks a policyholder through reporting a loss. Agentic FNOL is customer-facing and speaks to a natural person, so Article 50 disclosure applies. Included in the cloud platform but requires deliberate enablement and agent configuration; Guidewire does not publish whether individual agents carry incremental cost.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"ProNavigator embedded assistant","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","document_analysis"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"AI assistant embedded across InsuranceSuite applications, answering staff questions against policy wordings, guidelines and documentation. Staff-facing knowledge retrieval rather than decisioning. Arrived in the Palisades/Olos releases and carried forward. Low intrinsic risk, but it does shape coverage interpretations told to customers, so accuracy governance is worth a question.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Left empty deliberately, and this is the distinction consultants most often get wrong. Annex III point 5(c) covers AI used for risk assessment and pricing in relation to natural persons in the case of LIFE and HEALTH insurance only. Guidewire is a Property & Casualty core system — motor, home, commercial property, liability, workers' compensation — so pricing and risk selection on a Guidewire book is NOT automatically Annex III high-risk, however consequential it is for the individual. Do not flag Guidewire Predict as high-risk on reflex. Three routes to escalation. First, carriers that write accident and health lines on a P&C core: those specific products fall inside 5(c) and must be scoped separately from the motor and property book. Second, Agentic FNOL and any customer-facing assistant trigger Article 50 transparency regardless of tier. Third, if the carrier uses claims models to make decisions about a claimant's access to a benefit under a health policy, look at 5(c) again. Claims handling and reserving on P&C are otherwise outside Annex III altogether — subject to insurance conduct regulation and GDPR Article 22, not the high-risk regime.","docs_url":"https://www.guidewire.com/products/technology/insurance-ai-from-guidewire","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"nice-actimize-xceed","name":"NICE Actimize Xceed","vendor":"NICE Actimize","category":"Financial Crime / Fraud and AML Decisioning","walkthrough_category":"essential_services","prevalence":3,"aliases":["nice actimize","actimize","xceed","x-sight","actimizewatch","ifm","integrated fraud management","sam","suspicious activity monitoring","framl"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Xceed AI agents (unified fraud and AML detection)","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Purpose-built AI agents trained on financial crime intelligence that monitor transactions in real time across channels, block or hold suspicious payments, classify customer risk and generate AML alerts. AI is core to Xceed rather than an option — the platform is sold as a replacement for rules-only legacy tools, so a customer who has Xceed has the models running. Positioned at credit unions, community banks and mid-sized institutions; NICE Actimize's enterprise-tier equivalent is the X-Sight family, which is what large European banks run. Rated 'active' on the basis that once licensed there is no non-AI operating mode.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"ActimizeWatch","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Managed analytics service that applies machine learning across a pooled, cross-institution view to monitor model performance and adapt models and thresholds proactively, chiefly to cut false positives in suspicious-activity monitoring. Two things to flag: the customer's model behaviour is being changed by a process outside their own change-control, and the training signal comes partly from other institutions' data. Opt-in service, contracted separately. Ask who approves a model or threshold change and whether the customer can decline one.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"X-Sight AI Assist and AI Narrate","capabilities":["summarization","content_generation","document_analysis"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Generative AI that summarises alerts for investigators and drafts suspicious activity report narratives, with NICE Actimize claiming up to 70% faster SAR drafting. The output is a regulatory filing about a named individual, generated by a model and signed by a human — the human-oversight evidence needs to show the reviewer actually verified the facts, not just the prose. Separate add-on to the X-Sight platform. Also relevant: X-Sight Entity Risk produces a single consolidated trust score per entity, including individuals.","domains":["essential_services"]}],"typical_domains":["essential_services"],"escalation_note":"Get the carve-out right before flagging anything here. Annex III point 5(b) expressly excludes AI systems used for the purpose of detecting financial fraud, and AML transaction monitoring is a legal obligation under the AML framework rather than an Annex III use case — so on a plain reading, mainstream Xceed and X-Sight fraud and AML detection is NOT high-risk under the Annex, notwithstanding how consequential it is. What escalates it is the downstream use. Where an AI-driven risk score leads the bank to close an account, refuse a payment, or de-risk a retail customer, the system is determining a natural person's access to an essential private service and should be scoped as such; that is the question to put to the MLRO, because the tooling itself will not distinguish 'alert' from 'exit decision'. Also watch for the score leaking into creditworthiness assessment, which removes the fraud exception for that use. NICE Actimize is the provider of its consortium and packaged models, and ActimizeWatch specifically means model changes originate with the vendor — a fact that must be reflected in the deployer's Article 26 arrangements and in the model-risk policy. Individual-level trust scoring in X-Sight Entity Risk is worth a separate look under GDPR Article 22 regardless of AI Act tier.","docs_url":"https://www.niceactimize.com/xceed","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"sapiens-insurance-platform","name":"Sapiens Insurance Platform","vendor":"Sapiens International","category":"Insurance Core System (P&C, Life & Annuities, Health)","walkthrough_category":"essential_services","prevalence":3,"aliases":["sapiens","sapiens underwritingpro","underwritingpro","sapiens idit","sapiens alis","tia","tia technology","sapiens decision"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Sapiens UnderwritingPro","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","document_analysis","summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Cloud-native automated underwriting and new-business case management for Life & Annuities carriers, at v14 as of the latest release. A configurable rules engine automates routine underwriting decisions without IT involvement, generative AI summarises medical and third-party evidence documents to support the risk assessment, and the platform integrates external evidence providers. This decides whether an individual is accepted for life cover, at what rating, and on what exclusions — the most consequential automated decision in the whole insurance stack. Sapiens presents the AI as core functionality rather than a premium module, but it is bought as part of an L&A implementation, so treat as separately contracted.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Machine learning models (claim risk, churn, cross-sell)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Sapiens ships ML models across the platform for claim risk assessment, churn prediction and cross-sell targeting. Claim risk models score an individual claimant; churn and cross-sell models profile policyholders for retention and marketing treatment. Whether these are in scope depends entirely on the line of business they run against. Not enabled by default; each model needs configuring against carrier data.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"AI Co-Persona","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","summarization","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Persona-based AI assistant configured for agent, customer, claim handler and employer roles. The customer persona is consumer-facing and therefore engages Article 50 disclosure. The claim-handler and underwriter personas surface AI-generated context into decisions about individuals, which is where the human-oversight evidence needs to be. Requires configuration per persona; ask which personas are actually live.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Smart Process Automation (decision automation and business rules)","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Rules-driven decision automation across the platform, including generative AI document summarisation, translation and conversational querying alongside deterministic rules. Pure rules are arguably outside the Article 3(1) definition of an AI system; the same engine executing an ML-derived model is not. As with every core insurance system, the compliance answer depends on what is loaded, not on what the module is called.","domains":["essential_services"]}],"typical_domains":["essential_services"],"escalation_note":"Unlike Guidewire, Sapiens is squarely in Annex III point 5(c) territory because it runs Life & Annuities and Health as well as P&C. UnderwritingPro is the cleanest point 5(c) example in this whole catalogue: AI used for risk assessment and pricing in relation to natural persons in life insurance, which is high-risk on the face of the Annex. Scope by line of business, not by system: the same Sapiens estate can contain an in-scope L&A book and an out-of-scope motor and property book, and the P&C side is not caught by 5(c) merely because it shares a platform. Note the Nordic angle — Tia Technology has been a Sapiens company since November 2020 and continues to serve Danish, Norwegian, Swedish, Finnish and Baltic carriers across P&C, Life & Pension and Health; I could not verify any distinct Tia-branded AI feature on Sapiens' own site, so treat Tia installations as needing a direct question to the account team rather than assuming AI is present or absent. Sapiens is the provider of packaged models; a carrier that configures its own underwriting rules and models becomes the provider of those.","docs_url":"https://sapiens.com/us/life-and-annuities/underwritingpro/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"sas-intelligent-decisioning","name":"SAS Intelligent Decisioning","vendor":"SAS Institute","category":"Decisioning & Risk Analytics Platform","walkthrough_category":"essential_services","prevalence":3,"aliases":["sas intelligent decisioning","intelligent decisioning","sas credit scoring","sas credit origination","sas model manager","sas decisioning"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"SAS Intelligent Decisioning","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Combines business rules with real-time analytical scoring to automate operational decisions in milliseconds — SAS positions it explicitly for credit originations, risk management, fraud detection and transaction approval. It executes SAS, Python and open-source models side by side, which in a bank means the decision path for one applicant can traverse several models of different provenance. Part of the SAS Viya platform and licensed as a distinct offering; it is not something that arrives switched on with a Viya subscription. Ask for the decision-flow diagram and the list of scoring nodes in it — that document is your AI inventory for this system.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Integrated machine learning and model management","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The bridge between model development and production: registration, versioning, champion/challenger and performance monitoring of the models that Intelligent Decisioning executes. This is unusually good raw material for Article 12 logging and Article 17 quality-management evidence, and it is often the only place in a Nordic bank where model lineage is genuinely captured. Licensed within Viya. If the client has this, use it — it will save them building an AI Act evidence pack from scratch.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"SAS credit origination and credit scoring solutions","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"SAS packages Intelligent Decisioning into an end-to-end credit lifecycle offering covering application scoring, behavioural scoring and origination workflow, with model governance built in. This is the version of the product that is unambiguously about deciding whether a named person gets a loan. Sold as a solution, typically with SAS or partner implementation services. SAS is strongly entrenched in Nordic banking, insurance and public-sector risk functions, so this turns up more often in Scandinavia than its global market share would suggest.","domains":["essential_services"]}],"typical_domains":["essential_services"],"escalation_note":"Where SAS Intelligent Decisioning drives credit origination or behavioural scoring, it is Annex III point 5(b) high-risk and the bank is the provider, because the models and decision flows are the bank's own work executed on SAS infrastructure — SAS supplies the engine, not the judgement. That provider allocation is the single most important thing to establish here, because it puts Articles 9-15, technical documentation and post-market monitoring entirely on the client. Two boundaries. Intelligent Decisioning is a horizontal engine: the same deployment can run telco churn, retail next-best-offer or public-sector case routing, none of which is Annex III point 5, so scope by decision flow rather than by system. And SAS's fraud and AML decision flows benefit from the point 5(b) exception for AI used to detect financial fraud — but only for that purpose, and not where the fraud score also informs a credit or account-access outcome. Public-sector SAS deployments deserve a separate look: an eligibility or benefits-triage flow is Annex III point 5(a), which is a different limb with the same tier.","docs_url":"https://www.sas.com/en_us/software/intelligent-decisioning.html","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"temenos-core-banking","name":"Temenos Core Banking and Financial Crime Mitigation","vendor":"Temenos","category":"Core Banking Platform","walkthrough_category":"essential_services","prevalence":3,"aliases":["temenos","t24","transact","temenos ai","temenos fcm","financial crime mitigation","temenos copilot","conversational studio"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Temenos FCM AI Agent (sanctions screening and instant payments)","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Agentic AI layered on Temenos Financial Crime Mitigation that evaluates transactions and customers against global and domestic watchlists in real time and triages the resulting alerts, cutting false positives from a typical 5-8% to under 2% in live deployments. GA since May 2025, with a 2026 extension specifically for instant-payment flows. Temenos is explicit that it augments rather than replaces the compliance analyst — verify that claim against the actual auto-close thresholds configured, because 'automating over 20% of alerts' means one in five alerts is disposed of without a human reading it. Requires the FCM module licence and deployment-specific enablement; FCM also includes KYC risk scoring and AML transaction monitoring.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Temenos Copilot for Core and for Workbench","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","summarization","content_generation","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Two distinct copilots announced at TCF 2026: Copilot for Workbench helps developers plan and build platform extensions, and Copilot for Core targets branch manager and officer personas inside the core banking product. The Core copilot is the one that matters for compliance — it puts generative AI in front of a member of staff who is about to make or influence a decision about a customer sitting opposite them. Temenos has not published whether these are bundled or separately priced; treat as a separate commercial line until the contract says otherwise. No credit-decisioning claims are made for either.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Conversational Studio for Digital","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Natural-language environment for building end-to-end digital banking journeys, including customer-facing conversational flows in Temenos Digital. Where a bank uses it to build a customer-facing assistant, Article 50 transparency applies — the person must know they are talking to a machine. It is a build tool, so the risk profile depends entirely on what the bank builds with it. Ask to see the inventory of journeys published from it.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":["essential_services"],"escalation_note":"Temenos is the system of record for retail banking, so the domain is inherently essential services — but that does not make every Temenos AI feature Annex III high-risk, and being precise here saves the client a lot of wasted work. Sanctions screening and AML monitoring are legal obligations under the AML framework rather than Annex III point 5 systems, and point 5(b) in any case excepts AI used to detect financial fraud; so the FCM AI Agent is normally out of Annex III, while remaining subject to GDPR Article 22 and to supervisory expectations on model risk. The real escalation trigger is different: if the FCM output drives account closure, payment refusal or exit of a retail customer, the bank is using AI to determine a natural person's access to an essential private service, and that use should be scoped even if the tool is branded compliance. Temenos does not currently market AI credit scoring in core, so point 5(b) is usually reached through a third-party engine integrated into Temenos rather than through Temenos itself — go looking for that integration. Any customer-facing conversational journey built in Conversational Studio triggers Article 50 disclosure regardless of risk tier.","docs_url":"https://www.temenos.com/press_release/temenos-launches-embedded-ai-capabilities-to-help-banks-move-faster/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"ncino-platform","name":"nCino Platform","vendor":"nCino","category":"Loan Origination / Bank Operating System","walkthrough_category":"essential_services","prevalence":2,"aliases":["ncino","banking advisor","ncino banking advisor","mortgage advisor","continuous credit monitoring","ccm","ncino los"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Banking Advisor","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","summarization","document_analysis"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Generative AI interface built around role-based agents (Executive, Analyst, Service, Processor, Client) that drafts credit memo narratives, application summaries and quotes inside the nCino workflow. It does not formally issue the credit decision, but it writes the document the credit committee reads, which is decision-shaping in substance. Also drives Quick Quote for consumer auto lending. Presented as a platform enhancement rather than a standalone SKU; nCino does not publish licensing, so confirm from the order form whether it is contracted.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"nCino Mortgage Advisor","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","recommendation_profiling","content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Customer-facing generative AI giving 24/7 multilingual, loan-specific guidance to a borrower during mortgage origination, informed by that individual's own application data and guiding them through automated approval. This is the clearest consumer-facing AI in the nCino estate: a natural person interacting directly with a model about their own mortgage. Article 50 disclosure obligations bite here immediately. Separate enablement and heavily US-weighted today.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Continuous Credit Monitoring (CCM)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Monitors booked loans against performance patterns and peer benchmarks, generates prioritised alerts with impact scoring and recommends corrective action on at-risk relationships. It re-assesses borrowers continuously with no application event, and a downgrade can trigger covenant action, repricing or withdrawal of facilities. Predominantly a commercial-lending capability, which matters for scoping. Add-on to the core platform.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Automated Spreading and Document Validation","capabilities":["document_analysis","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Intelligent document processing that extracts and categorises tax and financial statement data (nCino cites over 99% correct categorisation in the US) and validates mortgage documents at onboarding. Pure extraction rather than judgement, but an extraction error propagates silently into the credit assessment, so it is in the causal chain of a point 5(b) decision. Add-on module. Ask what the human review rate is on low-confidence extractions.","domains":["essential_services"]}],"typical_domains":["essential_services"],"escalation_note":"Scope this one line of business at a time — nCino spans commercial, small business, consumer and mortgage, and only some of it is Annex III. Commercial lending to incorporated borrowers is outside point 5 because it assesses companies; mortgage, consumer lending and consumer account opening are inside point 5(b). Small-business lending is the ambiguous middle: sole traders and partnerships are natural persons. Banking Advisor raises the 'is a drafting assistant a high-risk system' question — the AI Act attaches to systems intended to be used for creditworthiness evaluation, and a tool whose declared purpose is to produce the credit memo is arguably intended for exactly that, so do not accept 'it only writes text' as a scoping answer; look at whether the memo is materially edited before approval. Mortgage Advisor is customer-facing and triggers Article 50 disclosure independently of tier. nCino is the provider of these features; the bank is the deployer with Article 26 human-oversight duties.","docs_url":"https://www.ncino.com/solutions/data-ai-analytics","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"provenir","name":"Provenir Decision Intelligence Platform","vendor":"Provenir","category":"Credit Risk Decisioning Platform","walkthrough_category":"essential_services","prevalence":2,"aliases":["provenir","provenir ai","provenir decisioning","decision intelligence"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI-powered decisioning and auto-ML model building","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active","notes":"The core of the product: ingest applicant and bureau data, run ML models and rules, return a real-time credit, fraud, onboarding or collections decision on a named individual. Provenir's differentiator is that risk teams can generate, deploy and monitor ML models without data scientists, which in practice means models reach production with lighter governance than in a traditional bank model-risk process. Rated 'active' because automated decisioning is the entire product — there is no non-AI mode. Popular with fintechs, BNPL and consumer lenders rather than tier-1 banks, so the deployer often has thin model documentation.","domains":["essential_services"]},{"feature_name":"Agentic AI in Decision Intelligence","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","summarization","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Launched February 2026 as part of a repositioned 'Decision Intelligence' platform bringing data, signals, models, decisioning and optimisation into one continuous system with agentic capabilities and model-management tooling. Signals are derived behavioural and risk features computed from raw customer data — i.e. inferred attributes about a person that never appeared in any application form. Newly available and being switched on selectively. Notably, Provenir's public platform pages say nothing about explainability, bias testing or AI Act readiness; that absence is itself worth raising with the system owner.","domains":["essential_services"]}],"typical_domains":["essential_services"],"escalation_note":"Annex III point 5(b) applies directly: this platform's declared purpose is evaluating creditworthiness and issuing credit decisions on natural persons. Its application-fraud and identity modules benefit from the point 5(b) financial-fraud exception, but because Provenir deliberately unifies credit, fraud and collections into one decision flow, the exception is hard to rely on in practice — insist on a use-by-use scoping rather than accepting 'it's a fraud platform'. The deployer is almost always the provider here, because the models are built by the customer inside Provenir's no-code environment; that means Articles 9-15, technical documentation, logging and post-market monitoring all land on the lender, not on Provenir. Where the customer is a smaller fintech, expect the Article 11 technical documentation not to exist at all. A Provenir instance used only for B2B trade-credit limits on incorporated customers is out of point 5 scope.","docs_url":"https://www.provenir.com/platform/decision-intelligence/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"instructure-canvas","name":"Canvas LMS (IgniteAI)","vendor":"Instructure","category":"Higher Education / K-12 Learning Management System","walkthrough_category":"education","prevalence":4,"aliases":["canvas","canvas lms","instructure canvas","igniteai","ignite ai","speedgrader"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"IgniteAI Grading Assistance for SpeedGrader","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Auto-generates a suggested score and draft written feedback for written assignments against the assignment's rubric, surfaced inside SpeedGrader. Instructure's framing is that the instructor reviews, edits or rewrites every suggestion so the final grade reflects human judgement. It is a Feature Option that an institution enables at institutional, departmental or course level, and it sits in the Canvas Plus and Canvas Next tiers — but Instructure made the advanced AI features free through 30 June 2026 in the US and 30 September 2026 worldwide, which means a large number of institutions currently have a grading AI available without ever having procured one. Ask the Canvas admin what happens at the end of the free window and whether any faculty are bulk-accepting suggestions.","domains":["education"]},{"feature_name":"IgniteAI Agent","capabilities":["content_generation","basic_automation","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Agentic assistant announced for Canvas that executes multi-step course workflows from a single educator prompt — building modules, aligning content to standards, generating rubrics, reviewing discussions. Released as an administrator Feature Option in early 2026 with broader educator access following. Instructure documents an opt-in posture and publishes 'AI Nutrition Facts' per feature describing the model and data handling. It acts on course structure and content rather than on individual student records, which keeps it out of Annex III on its own.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"LLM-Enabled Assignment","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"An assignment type that embeds a structured conversational learning assistant into the assignment itself, so the student works through the task in dialogue with the model, and the educator gets visibility into the learner's reasoning as well as the finished answer. This is student-facing AI, not teacher-facing, which changes the transparency analysis: the pupil is interacting with an AI system and Article 50 disclosure applies. Confirm whether the conversation transcript is retained and whether it feeds the grade.","domains":["education"]}],"typical_domains":["education"],"escalation_note":"Grading Assistance is the clearest Annex III 3(b) case in this whole category — an AI system used to evaluate learning outcomes. Instructure's human-in-the-loop design does not remove the classification; Annex III applies to the intended use, and Article 14 human oversight is what the design is *satisfying*, not a reason to fall outside scope. The practical boundary: an instructor who reads submissions and adjusts AI drafts is exercising real oversight; a department that accepts suggestions in bulk to clear a marking backlog has converted it into automated evaluation and owes the student an Article 86 explanation of the decision. The free-through-2026 licensing is the governance risk to name out loud — AI grading arrived in these estates through a vendor promotion rather than a procurement decision, so there is often no DPIA and no owner.","docs_url":"https://www.instructure.com/igniteai","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"google-classroom","name":"Google Classroom (Gemini in Classroom)","vendor":"Google","category":"K-12 / Higher Education LMS with embedded generative AI","walkthrough_category":"education","prevalence":4,"aliases":["google classroom","classroom","gemini in classroom","gemini for education","google workspace for education","gwfe"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Gemini in Classroom","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Generative AI embedded directly in Classroom for both teachers (lesson material, differentiated texts, quiz and rubric drafting) and students (turning class materials into flashcards, practice quizzes and study guides). Google's own admin documentation states it is ON by default for teachers and students of all ages across Education Fundamentals, Standard and Plus, with the only exception being tenants that had previously switched it off for under-18s. An admin turns it off at Admin console > Generative AI > Gemini for Workspace > Feature access in Classroom, and it also depends on the underlying Gemini app or Gemini Notebook (NotebookLM) services being enabled. This is one of the very few genuinely default-on AI capabilities in the education stack, so a school that has made no AI decision at all still has generative AI in front of minors.","domains":["education"]},{"feature_name":"Gemini for Education (LearnLM)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"The standalone Gemini app tuned for learning (Google positions it on Gemini 2.5 Pro incorporating LearnLM), included at no extra charge in all Google Workspace for Education editions. Features include Gems (custom AI personas), Canvas for building quizzes and study guides, Deep Research and Audio Overviews. Under-18 accounts get a separate experience with tighter content filters, AI-literacy onboarding and automatic double-check on factual answers. Marked active_partial because inclusion is universal but the under-18 experience and per-OU scoping depend on the institution's age-based access settings, which many tenants have never reviewed.","domains":["education"]}],"typical_domains":["education"],"escalation_note":"Teacher-side drafting and student study aids are not by themselves Annex III point 3 high-risk: nothing is being admitted, graded or monitored. It crosses into Annex III 3(b) the moment a teacher lets Gemini score or grade student work of record, and edges toward 3(d) (steering the learning process) if adaptive practice-quiz output is used to place or stream a pupil. The live exposure for most schools today is not high-risk classification at all but Article 50 transparency toward pupils and parents, Article 4 AI-literacy duties for staff, and GDPR/child-data governance. Because it is default-on, the first consulting question is not 'do you use AI' but 'when did you last look at the Gemini toggle in your Admin console'.","docs_url":"https://edu.google.com/intl/ALL_us/ai/gemini-for-education/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"moodle","name":"Moodle LMS","vendor":"Moodle Pty Ltd","category":"Open-source Learning Management System","walkthrough_category":"education","prevalence":4,"aliases":["moodle","moodle lms","moodle 5","moodle 5.1","moodle workplace"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI subsystem (AI placements and AI providers)","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Core subsystem introduced in Moodle 4.5 and extended through 5.0/5.1. Two placements ship in core: the Text editor placement (generate text, generate image) and the Course assistance placement (summarise text, explain text). Moodle's own docs are unambiguous: 'By default, all AI placements are disabled' — a site admin must enable them at Site administration > General > AI placements, and users must accept an AI usage policy on first use. Nothing works until the admin also configures a provider (Azure AI, OpenAI API, Ollama, or DeepSeek new in 5.1) with credentials, which for the hosted providers means the institution signs its own paid LLM contract. Consultant question: who owns that provider contract and where does the prompt data land.","domains":["education"]},{"feature_name":"Analytics prediction models","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Moodle core ships machine-learning prediction models, notably 'Students at risk of dropping out', alongside static rule-based models such as 'Upcoming activities due' and 'No teaching'. The docs state most models are not enabled by default and should only be enabled after considering institutional goals. When enabled, the model produces per-student risk insights with suggested actions, including messaging the flagged student directly. This is genuine profiling of an identified learner and it long predates the vendor's generative-AI branding — it is easy to miss in an AI inventory because nobody calls it 'AI'.","domains":["education"]}],"typical_domains":["education"],"escalation_note":"Annex III point 3 throughout. The drop-out risk model is the sharp end: profiling an identified student and steering what support or intervention they receive is Annex III 3(d) (monitoring and steering the learning process), and if a risk flag influences continuation on a programme it touches 3(a) (admission/assignment). Generative placements are not high-risk when a teacher drafts material, but become 3(b) if an institution wires an AI action into automated marking of an assessment of record. One Moodle-specific trap: because Moodle is self-hosted and pluggable, an institution that writes its own placement, fine-tunes a model, or puts its own name on the output is likely a *provider* under Article 25, not merely a deployer — a much heavier obligation set than most universities assume they have taken on.","docs_url":"https://docs.moodle.org/501/en/AI_placements","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"turnitin","name":"Turnitin","vendor":"Turnitin, LLC","category":"Academic Integrity / Similarity and AI-writing detection","walkthrough_category":"education","prevalence":4,"aliases":["turnitin","turnitin feedback studio","similarity report","turnitin ai detection","ai writing detection","turnitin clarity","ouriginal"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI writing detection","capabilities":["document_analysis"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Classifies how much of a submitted document was likely machine-generated and reports it as a percentage alongside the Similarity Report. Turnitin consolidated its multi-model ensemble into a single model in July 2026 while holding a stated false-positive rate below 1%. Marked active_partial rather than active because the indicator is bundled into the Similarity Report for most institutional licences and is visible to instructors without any extra purchase, but administrators can switch it off tenant-wide and availability differs by product line and region — confirm the actual toggle state with your Turnitin administrator rather than assuming. Turnitin's own published data (roughly 15% of submissions above 80% AI writing since October 2025) tells you how often staff are now acting on this number.","domains":["education"]},{"feature_name":"Turnitin Clarity","capabilities":["document_analysis","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Separately licensed product launched July 2025 that records the student's writing process from first draft to submission, and optionally exposes an AI assistant inside the writing environment that gives feedback but refuses to produce prose for the student. The output an educator sees is a process record plus the student's prompt history. It is a different evidential posture from detection — it observes rather than infers — but it is continuous monitoring of a named student's work, and the prompt log is a rich behavioural record that needs a retention answer.","domains":["education"]}],"typical_domains":["education"],"escalation_note":"Annex III point 3(d) names this almost verbatim: AI systems intended for monitoring and detecting prohibited behaviour of students during tests. Turnitin's detection output used as evidence in an academic misconduct case is a high-risk use, and the deployer obligations fall on the institution — Article 26 (use per instructions, competent human oversight, logging) and Article 86 (the student's right to an explanation of a decision that adversely affects them). The sub-1% false positive figure is a population statistic, not a per-student guarantee; across a faculty's annual submission volume it still produces a predictable number of wrongly accused students, disproportionately non-native writers. The boundary that matters: using the percentage as a *trigger to look* at a paper is defensible; using it as the finding of fact is not, and Turnitin itself does not present the score as proof. Note Article 5 is NOT engaged here — this is text classification, no emotion inference and no biometrics.","docs_url":"https://guides.turnitin.com/hc/en-us/articles/28477544839821-Turnitin-s-AI-writing-detection-capabilities-FAQs","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"blackboard-learn","name":"Blackboard Learn (AI Design Assistant and AVA)","vendor":"Anthology","category":"Higher Education Learning Management System","walkthrough_category":"education","prevalence":3,"aliases":["blackboard","blackboard learn","bb learn","anthology","ai design assistant","ava","anthology virtual assistant"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI Design Assistant","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Instructor-facing course authoring AI running on Microsoft Azure OpenAI. Generates learning module structures and module images, test questions and question banks, assignments, discussion prompts, journals, rubrics, and Unsplash search keywords, with a context picker and language selector. Anthology's admin documentation is explicit that it is opt-in: an administrator enables it under Building Blocks ('AI Design Assistant and Unsplash'), each individual function can be switched on or off separately, and course-role privileges must then be granted. Primary hosting is in the United States with other Azure regions possible — a transfer question worth raising for EU institutions independently of the AI Act.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI Conversation","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Student-facing activity type where the learner converses with an AI persona configured by the instructor, in either a Socratic questioning or a role-play format, followed by a reflection component that can be graded. This is the Blackboard feature that most often surprises a compliance review, because it puts an AI system in direct dialogue with a student and then attaches a mark to the interaction. Confirm whether the grade is derived from the conversation itself or only from the human-marked reflection.","domains":["education"]},{"feature_name":"AVA (Blackboard AI Virtual Assistant)","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Suite layered on top of the Design Assistant: AVA Assisted Feedback drafts constructive feedback against a rubric, AVA Responses answers common student questions, and AVA Playground gives staff and students an institutionally managed sandbox with selected models inside the LMS. Anthology's EMEA marketing presents AVA as included as standard for Blackboard LMS customers, while release material has indicated an introductory inclusion period after which a separate AVA licence applies — the licensing has moved more than once, so read the current contract rather than the website. Left at available_not_enabled because, as with the Design Assistant, an administrator has to switch the functions on and grant role privileges.","domains":["education"]}],"typical_domains":["education"],"escalation_note":"AI Conversation with a graded reflection, and AVA Assisted Feedback where the drafted feedback drives the mark, are the two paths into Annex III 3(b) (evaluating learning outcomes). Generating test questions is not itself an evaluation of a person and stays out of scope. The realistic boundary: a rubric-based feedback draft that an academic reads, edits and owns is oversight; a marker who pastes AVA output unread has produced an automated evaluation and the institution carries Article 26 and Article 86 duties. Nothing in Blackboard's documented AI touches Article 5 — no emotion inference, no biometrics. The item to track is licensing drift: features that arrived bundled are moving behind an AVA licence, and institutions that stop paying may find a governance record for a system they no longer run and no record for the one they do.","docs_url":"https://help.anthology.com/blackboard/administrator/en/tools-management/blackboard-ai-design-assistant.html","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"cornerstone-galaxy","name":"Cornerstone Galaxy","vendor":"Cornerstone OnDemand","category":"Corporate LMS / Talent and Skills Development Platform","walkthrough_category":"education","prevalence":3,"aliases":["cornerstone","cornerstone ondemand","csod","cornerstone galaxy","galaxy ai","cornerstone workforce ai"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Adaptive Learning Agent","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","content_generation","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Launched 16 March 2026 in the Galaxy Spring release. Generates role- and skill-specific learning spaces for an individual employee, with AI-driven content recommendations, quizzes and role-play practice, and no curation work by an administrator. In other words it decides what an individual employee is offered, tests them on it, and tracks readiness. Cornerstone positions it as part of the Galaxy platform rather than a separate SKU, but new release capabilities sit behind administrator-controlled release settings — confirm the toggle state in your own release configuration rather than assuming the announcement means it is live.","domains":["education","employment"]},{"feature_name":"AI-Powered Course Assistant","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Also from the March 2026 release. An in-course assistant that lets a learner ask questions, clarify meaning and apply knowledge without leaving the learning experience. Conversational support against course content; it does not by itself score the learner. The question to ask the system owner is whether the assistant's interaction data feeds any completion, engagement or readiness signal that is visible to the employee's manager.","domains":["education"]},{"feature_name":"Personalised recommendations, skills inference and talent matching (Galaxy AI)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Cornerstone's platform-level AI: learning recommendations tailored to each person's role, goals and style, AI content curation, talent matching and workforce planning insights, described as a system of agents working together. Marked active_partial because personalised content recommendation on the learner home page is core platform behaviour that most tenants have running, while skills inference, talent matching and opportunity marketplace behaviour depend on which modules the customer has licensed. This is the layer that builds a durable machine-held profile of an employee's skills — ask to see the skills profile of a real employee and ask where each inferred skill came from.","domains":["education","employment"]}],"typical_domains":["education","employment"],"escalation_note":"This is the double-exposure case and it is worth getting right. Cornerstone is simultaneously an LMS (Annex III point 3: access to and assignment on training, evaluating learning outcomes, monitoring and steering the learning process) and a talent platform (Annex III point 4: recruitment and selection, and decisions on promotion, termination, task allocation and evaluation of performance). An Adaptive Learning Agent that determines which development an employee is offered is 3(d) on its own. The moment completion of that development is a condition of promotion, of internal mobility, or of an inferred readiness score read by a succession process, the same recommendation becomes an input to a 4(b) decision — and an employee who was never recommended the course was quietly filtered out of the promotion before any human looked. Concrete test for the system owner: does any Cornerstone-derived skill, completion or readiness value get read by the promotion, succession or internal-mobility process? If yes, both classifications apply and Article 26(7) requires informing the affected workers and their representatives before putting it into service. Separately, Article 5(1)(f) prohibits inferring emotions in the workplace — a role-play or immersive simulation that rates a learner's confidence, empathy or tone from voice or facial signals would be a prohibited practice, not a high-risk one. Get in writing what the role-play scoring actually measures.","docs_url":"https://www.cornerstoneondemand.com/platform/cornerstone-galaxy-ai/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"docebo","name":"Docebo","vendor":"Docebo Inc.","category":"Corporate LMS / Learning Experience Platform","walkthrough_category":"education","prevalence":3,"aliases":["docebo","docebo lms","docebo learn","harmony ai","docebo skills","ai virtual coaching","docebo roleplay"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Skills Intelligence","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Infers an individual's skills from their activity and content, identifies gaps against a role, and drives development recommendations. This is inference about a named employee rather than about content, and the inferred skill set is the thing that other systems then consume. Sold as part of the Docebo product set rather than being on out of the box; confirm which modules the tenant has licensed, because Docebo's marketing presents the whole suite as one platform and the contract is the only reliable source. Ask to see one employee's inferred skill profile and the evidence behind three of the inferred skills.","domains":["education","employment"]},{"feature_name":"AI Virtual Coaching / Roleplay","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Scenario-based simulation where an employee practises a real conversation against an AI-driven video avatar, in voice or chat mode, with real-time responses and a personalised feedback report at the end. An administrator turns a prompt into a full coaching scenario without setup expertise. Docebo has said it will connect to the Skills Engine to track progress and refine learning paths. The feedback report is the compliance object: it is a machine-generated evaluation of how a named employee performed in a simulated interaction, and it is the feature most likely to have been bought as an experiment by L&D without an assessment.","domains":["education","employment"]},{"feature_name":"Content Creator (AI authoring)","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"AI authoring that builds interactive courses and lessons from prompts and source material, launched from a single unified creation entry point in the January 2026 release. Author-facing content generation with no learner profiling, which keeps it out of Annex III on its own. The realistic exposure is copyright and accuracy of generated compliance training, not fundamental rights.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Harmony AI (Harmony Search, Harmony Copilot, AgentHub, Enterprise Knowledge)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Docebo's conversational and agentic layer: Harmony Search surfaces answers and learning paths from catalogues, Harmony Copilot gives contextual task guidance and routes to agents, AgentHub hosts agents that execute work, Enterprise Knowledge searches across connected platforms, and an MCP Server exposes Docebo to external AI tools. The MCP Server is the one to flag to security — it is a deliberate route for third-party AI to read learning and skills data about employees.","domains":["education"]}],"typical_domains":["education","employment"],"escalation_note":"Same dual exposure as Cornerstone, with a sharper Article 5 edge. On the education side, Annex III 3(b) and 3(d) apply where Docebo evaluates learning outcomes or steers what an employee is offered; on the employment side, Annex III 4(b) applies where a Skills Intelligence score, a certification, or a Roleplay result is read by promotion, task-allocation or performance processes. The boundary is stakes: a Roleplay used purely as private practice, with the feedback report visible only to the learner and never retained, is low risk; the same report stored on the employee record or gating a certification is an evaluation of a person and pulls both classifications. The Article 5(1)(f) question is unavoidable here — an AI avatar conversation that rates the employee's tone, confidence, empathy or emotional state from voice or video is emotion inference in a workplace context and is a **prohibited practice**, not a high-risk one. Docebo's public material describes scenario feedback without stating whether affect signals are used; do not assume either way, get the scoring dimensions in writing before the tool is deployed.","docs_url":"https://www.docebo.com/whatsnew/2026-january/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"inspera-assessment","name":"Inspera Assessment (with Inspera Proctoring)","vendor":"Inspera AS","category":"Digital Examination and Remote Proctoring","walkthrough_category":"education","prevalence":3,"aliases":["inspera","inspera assessment","inspera proctoring","inspera exam","digital eksamen"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Smart flagging (AI proctoring)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Monitors webcam, screen and microphone during an exam and flags behaviour in real time — the candidate leaving the frame, conversing with another person, or using a secondary device. Configurable per test: camera, microphone and screen recording can each be selected, plus room scan and second camera, and browser lockdown ranges from fully open to locked with permitted resources. Institutions choose fully automated flagging, live human proctoring, or a hybrid. Inspera Proctoring is a separately purchased module on top of Inspera Assessment, so a tenant running Inspera for on-campus digital exams may have no proctoring AI at all. Reviewers work a colour-coded severity timeline of flagged incidents; Inspera states plainly that 'the final decision about conduct is your institution's. AI is your assistant, not the expert.'","domains":["education"]},{"feature_name":"ID Scan (candidate check-in)","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Identity verification step at the start of a proctored session, listed across both recorded and live proctoring types. Inspera's public proctoring page documents ID Scan as part of check-in but does not describe automated face matching against the ID photo, so whether the comparison is done by an algorithm or by the human proctor is the open question — ask the vendor directly, because the answer changes the analysis. Tagged with biometric_categorisation on the assumption that a face-to-ID comparison is performed somewhere in the flow; if the institution's configuration has a human doing the comparison, drop the biometric tag for that deployment.","domains":["education","biometrics"]}],"typical_domains":["education","biometrics"],"escalation_note":"Annex III point 3(d) names this directly — AI intended for monitoring and detecting prohibited behaviour of students during tests. Proctoring is high-risk whenever it is used on an examination, and no amount of human review changes the classification; human review is how the deployer satisfies Article 14, not how it escapes Annex III. The Article 5 boundary is the important distinction and Inspera sits on the right side of it as documented: it flags *behaviour* — out of frame, second voice, second device — and its published material describes no inference of emotional state, stress or deception from face or voice. Behavioural and gaze anomaly detection is high-risk; inferring how a candidate feels would be a prohibited practice under Article 5(1)(f), which bans emotion inference in education. Do not let a vendor blur the two, and ask in writing whether any 'nervousness', 'stress' or 'suspicion' signal derived from affect exists in the product. On biometrics: a one-to-one face match against the candidate's own ID photo is biometric *verification*, which Annex III point 1(a) expressly carves out of remote biometric identification — so it is not high-risk on that ground alone, but it is still special-category biometric data under GDPR Article 9 and needs its own lawful basis and retention answer. Inspera being Norwegian and EU-hosted is a data-residency comfort, not a classification argument.","docs_url":"https://inspera.com/inspera-proctoring/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"itslearning","name":"itslearning","vendor":"itslearning AS","category":"K-12 Learning Management System (Nordic)","walkthrough_category":"education","prevalence":3,"aliases":["itslearning","its learning","itsl","ai toolkit"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI Toolkit","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Seven teacher-facing generation tools inside itslearning: Assignment (with basic/standard/advanced differentiation), Test, Worksheet, Article, Session Builder, Glossary and Inspire Me. Runs on Azure OpenAI hosted in Sweden; itslearning states generated content is not stored, not retained and not used to train models. It is explicitly not part of the standard licence — the vendor describes it as an optional add-on priced per licence plus usage, following a seven-country pilot in April 2026. A system administrator enables it under Administration > Edit Global Settings > AI Settings and decides which schools and which teachers get access; each organisation gets a monthly pool of AI requests (20,000 in the pilot configuration). Students cannot use it at all, which is the single most important fact for a risk assessment.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":["education"],"escalation_note":"As shipped this is a thin entry and that is the honest answer: the AI Toolkit drafts teaching material for teachers, no pupil interacts with it and no pupil is scored, profiled or monitored by it, so Annex III point 3 is not engaged. It escalates only if a school takes an AI-generated Test and wires it into automated scoring that becomes a mark of record (3(b)), or if a future release opens the tools to pupils, at which point Article 50 transparency toward minors applies. Do not let the Swedish Azure hosting be presented as AI Act compliance — EU data residency answers a GDPR transfer question and says nothing about risk classification. Because itslearning is a Norwegian vendor serving Nordic municipalities, the deploying school is frequently a public body, which brings the Article 27 fundamental rights impact assessment into play the moment any feature does become high-risk.","docs_url":"https://itslearning.com/itslearning-ai-toolkit","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"kahoot","name":"Kahoot!","vendor":"Kahoot! ASA","category":"Game-based learning and engagement platform","walkthrough_category":"education","prevalence":3,"aliases":["kahoot","kahoot!","kahoot 360","kahoot for schools"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI-assisted Creator","capabilities":["content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Generates question proposals, answer alternatives and illustrative images from a short prompt (max 300 characters), a URL, a Wikipedia article or synced slides. Kahoot's trust centre lists the models used by name: Azure OpenAI GPT 3.5x/4x/5x, Google Gemini 2.5 Pro, Apple Foundation Model and Perplexity.ai, with DALL-E 3 and Gemini behind image generation. Available only on a selection of product plans, so a free or basic school tenant does not have it. Kahoot states no platform data is used to train or refine any model, and warns users not to put personal or confidential data into prompts — which is exactly the warning teachers ignore when they paste a pupil's work in.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"PDF to Story / PDF to Kahoot","capabilities":["content_generation","document_analysis"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Uploads a PDF (roughly 150 pages / 300k characters) and generates a kahoot or a Story from it, using GPT-4o, GPT-4o mini and GPT-3.5-turbo-16k. Kahoot documents that user PDFs are rendered in the user's browser and never shared or stored. Available on a selection of Teacher, education-institution and Business plans. Same practical caution as the Creator: the content risk is a teacher uploading a document containing pupil data, not the feature itself.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Brainstorming clustering","capabilities":["summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Groups similar free-text participant responses in a brainstorm so the facilitator sees themes rather than a wall of answers. Runs on paraphrase-multilingual-MiniLM-L12-v2 hosted by Kahoot itself, with no external sharing of the responses. On a selection of paid plans for teachers, institutions and businesses. It processes what participants typed, which in a classroom means pupil-authored text, but it clusters content rather than profiling the person who wrote it.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Search relevance ranking","capabilities":["natural_language_query"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Embedding-based ranking (ada-Embedding-002) of public kahoots by relevance to a search. Available to all users on every plan, so this one genuinely is on by default — but it ranks published content, not people, and Kahoot states only kahoots the user designated public are processed. Included precisely because it is the low-risk counterexample: an always-on AI feature that carries no Annex III exposure at all.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":["education"],"escalation_note":"Kahoot's documented AI generates and organises content; it does not admit, grade, profile or monitor a learner, and the trust centre describes no emotion recognition and no biometric analysis. In normal formative classroom use, Annex III point 3 is simply not engaged, and saying so plainly is more useful to a client than manufacturing a concern. It would cross into 3(b) only if an institution used a kahoot as a summative assessment of record with the platform's scoring treated as the grade. Corporate Kahoot 360 used for mandatory compliance quizzes touches \`employment\` only where a pass/fail result gates access to a role or a certification — and if it does, the employment analysis, not the education one, is the live issue. The forward-looking watch item is that Kahoot routes prompts to four different third-party model providers, so the transparency and data-flow answer differs feature by feature.","docs_url":"https://trust.kahoot.com/ai-powered-features-in-kahoot/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"proctoru","name":"ProctorU Proctoring Platform","vendor":"Meazure Learning","category":"Remote Proctoring and Test Delivery (human-validated)","walkthrough_category":"education","prevalence":2,"aliases":["proctoru","meazure","meazure learning","proctoru live+","yardstick"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Technology-assisted incident detection (explicitly not AI-only)","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"This is a valuable negative finding and should be recorded as such. Meazure Learning states in its own FAQ: 'Our proctoring platform does not rely on AI or automation; it relies on human expertise', and that 'our technology simply reduces administrative workload and helps our human proctors identify activities that might violate the test provider's integrity requirements'. Every exam session is reviewed by at least two trained professionals before any suspected incident is reported, and Live+ sessions are watched in real time. This follows ProctorU's public 2021 decision to discontinue fully automated, record-and-review services entirely, on the reasoning that AI flagged eye-rubbing or a barking dog as misconduct. Sold as a per-exam proctoring service, not a tenant feature. Capabilities are tagged as basic_automation deliberately — on the vendor's own account there is no model making inferences about the candidate.","domains":["education"]},{"feature_name":"Identity verification (ID capture with facial comparison, keystroke biometrics, challenge questions)","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Meazure's FAQ lists three identity methods: public-records challenge questions, keystroke biometrics, and ID capture with facial comparison. Keystroke biometrics is the one most reviewers miss — it is behavioural biometric processing of the candidate, running throughout the session rather than only at check-in. Which methods are active depends on what the test provider configured for that exam, so the answer is per-exam, not per-tenant. Ask the exam owner which of the three are switched on and whether the comparison and keystroke matching are performed algorithmically or reviewed by the human proctor.","domains":["education","biometrics"]}],"typical_domains":["education","biometrics"],"escalation_note":"Two separate questions, and keeping them apart is the whole value of this entry. (1) Proctoring an examination falls under Annex III point 3(d) by intended purpose — but if the flagging really is non-inferential assistive tooling with two human reviewers, as Meazure states, the system may not meet the Article 3(1) definition of an AI system at all, in which case the AI Act does not bite and the analysis is purely GDPR. That is a materially different outcome from every other proctoring vendor in this catalogue, so get the statement confirmed contractually rather than relying on a marketing FAQ. (2) The identity layer is real biometric processing regardless of the AI question. Face comparison against the candidate's own ID is biometric *verification*, expressly carved out of Annex III point 1(a) remote biometric identification, so it is not high-risk on that ground — but keystroke dynamics is behavioural biometric data under GDPR Article 9 and needs an explicit lawful basis, a retention period and an accessible alternative for candidates who cannot be verified this way. On Article 5(1)(f): nothing in ProctorU's documentation claims to infer emotional state, and the company's stated position is that computers are bad at judging honesty, which is the opposite of an emotion-inference product. Still ask the question, because the underlying platform is periodically re-specified.","docs_url":"https://www.meazurelearning.com/about-us/faq","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"respondus-monitor","name":"Respondus Monitor (with LockDown Browser)","vendor":"Respondus, Inc.","category":"Automated Remote Proctoring / Exam Lockdown","walkthrough_category":"education","prevalence":2,"aliases":["respondus","respondus monitor","lockdown browser","ldb","respondus lockdown"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Automated proctoring with facial detection and Review Priority","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Fully automated webcam proctoring layered on LockDown Browser. Respondus runs a facial detection check before the exam starts — prompting the student to fix lighting or framing rather than flagging them — and continues facial detection during the session, which is what produces flagged events such as 'Missing from frame' and 'Different person in frame'. Those events plus other session data roll up into a Review Priority value that ranks sessions for instructor attention. Respondus is explicit that flagged events and Review Priority do not determine whether a student cheated; they prioritise which recordings a human should watch. Licensed as an institutional site licence (list price around USD 4,950 for year one regardless of institution size, then tiered, unlimited or student-purchase) with no per-exam fee. LockDown Browser itself is a hardened browser with no AI in it — do not put it in the AI inventory.","domains":["education"]}],"typical_domains":["education"],"escalation_note":"Annex III point 3(d), same as any exam proctoring. On Article 5: as documented, Respondus performs facial *detection* (is a face present, is it lit and framed) and behavioural flagging, not emotion inference — that keeps it high-risk rather than prohibited, and the distinction is worth stating explicitly to a client who has read that 'the proctoring tool watches their face'. Two things to probe. First, the 'Different person in frame' flag implies some frame-to-frame comparison of facial features; Respondus's public material does not say whether that constitutes biometric processing, and the answer matters for GDPR Article 9 even if it falls short of biometric identification under the AI Act — ask the vendor in writing rather than inferring. Second, institutional guidance widely documents that the facial detection is sensitive to lighting, movement and webcam quality and disproportionately misregisters students of colour, students who move to concentrate, and students on weaker equipment. Under Article 14 the deployer owns that: a human must review the recording before any misconduct finding, and Review Priority must never be treated as a score. European prevalence is low — this is a US-dominant product and Nordic institutions more often run Inspera or Safe Exam Browser — but it turns up via US partner programmes and online providers.","docs_url":"https://web.respondus.com/he/monitor/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"nordic-bankid-mitid","name":"BankID (Sweden), BankID (Norway) and MitID (Denmark)","vendor":"Finansiell ID-Teknik BID AB / BankID BankAxept AS / Digitaliseringsstyrelsen","category":"National eID and Strong Customer Authentication (not an AI system)","walkthrough_category":"biometrics","prevalence":5,"aliases":["bankid","mobilt bankid","bankid på mobil","mitid","nemid","svensk bankid","norsk bankid","sverige-id"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Biometric unlock of the BankID / MitID app (device Face ID or fingerprint)","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"This entry exists so it can be ruled out. When a Nordic client is asked 'do you use biometrics', BankID or MitID is the first thing they name, and in almost every case the answer for AI Act purposes is no. What actually happens is that the user optionally enables Face ID / Touch ID / Android fingerprint to unlock the eID app instead of typing a PIN; the comparison is performed by Apple's or Google's secure enclave on the handset, the biometric never leaves the device, and BankID and MitID never receive a face image or a template. Norwegian BankID markets this as 'BankID biometri' for faster login; Swedish BankID lets the user choose separately whether biometrics apply to login only or also to signing. There is no model trained by the eID provider and no inference about the person — it is a local unlock gate on a PKI credential. Two things do deserve a second look: Denmark's MitID app activation flow reads the passport chip and captures the user's face, which is a real one-to-one biometric comparison and not merely a device unlock; and Sweden's state eID, Sverige-ID, is due to launch 1 December 2026 alongside BankID, so the enrolment architecture in that market is about to change.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Deliberately tagged with no Annex III domain. Swedish and Norwegian BankID as used day to day are PKI-based electronic identification schemes with device-level biometric unlock — no AI system within Art. 3(1), no biometric data processed by the scheme operator or by you as relying party, and therefore nothing to place on an AI inventory. Say this early in an engagement, because otherwise a Nordic client's inventory fills up with eID entries and the real biometric AI (the KYC vendor sitting behind onboarding, the face reader on the office door) gets buried. Two genuine caveats. First, MitID's app activation using a passport chip plus a face capture is a one-to-one biometric verification performed by the scheme, not by the handset; it would still fall under the Art. 3(42) verification carve-out and outside Annex III point 1(a), but it is biometric processing and should be recorded as such — mitid.dk blocks automated retrieval, so confirm the current flow with the client rather than relying on this note. Second, the relying party's own onboarding around an eID — the document check and selfie done before a BankID is issued or when a customer without one is enrolled — is very often an Onfido, Veriff, IDnow or Signicat flow, and that is where the assessment actually belongs.","docs_url":"https://www.bankid.com/foretag/ansiktsigenkanning","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"axis-video-analytics","name":"AXIS Analytics (AXIS Object Analytics, AXIS Face Detector, ACAP)","vendor":"Axis Communications","category":"Video Surveillance / Edge Video Analytics","walkthrough_category":"biometrics","prevalence":4,"aliases":["axis","axis communications","axis object analytics","aoa","axis face detector","acap","artpec","axis camera application platform"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AXIS Object Analytics","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Axis's own suite of AI-based edge analytics, running on the camera's ARTPEC deep-learning processor: classifying detected objects as humans or vehicle types, line crossing, area intrusion, occupancy and time-in-area. It is included on most current Axis cameras rather than sold as an add-on, which is why it is marked active — a Swedish or Norwegian customer who has never bought an 'AI product' is almost certainly running deep-learning object classification on every camera in the building. It classifies object types, not identities: no face template, no person-level recognition.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AXIS Face Detector","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Detects the presence of a face in the scene — Axis positions it for loss prevention — and does not identify anyone. This distinction is the single most common misunderstanding in a physical-security assessment: face detection locates a face region, face recognition compares it against a stored template. Axis does not sell face recognition. Detection alone produces no biometric template and no identification, so it does not by itself constitute biometric processing, though it will usually be misdescribed as 'facial recognition' in the client's own asset register. Read the licence list, not the register.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Third-party face recognition via ACAP","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The AXIS Camera Application Platform is an open environment for partner applications to run on the camera itself. This is the route by which genuine face recognition reaches an Axis estate — a partner ACAP, licensed separately, doing on-camera matching against a watchlist. Nothing about the Axis hardware or Axis's own software puts it there; a person has to install and license it. Ask for the list of installed ACAPs per camera model, because it will not appear in any Axis licence Axis itself issued.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"AXIS Live Privacy Shield","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Dynamic privacy masking that obscures moving people or defined regions in the live and recorded stream in real time. Worth naming in a report as the available mitigation rather than only cataloguing the risks — where a camera overlooks a public pavement or a staff break area, this is the control that makes a deployment defensible, and it ships with the platform rather than being an extra purchase.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Deliberately left with no Annex III domain: Axis's own analytics classify objects and detect faces without identifying anyone, so out of the box an Axis estate is not an Annex III point 1 system at all. The domain depends entirely on what the customer installs on top. The escalation is precise and it is worth putting in a client's hands as a rule: the moment a partner ACAP performs face matching against a reference database of people, the deployment becomes a remote biometric identification system under Art. 3(41) — subjects are identified at a distance without their active involvement — and if that runs live in a shop entrance, a station, a stadium or any other publicly accessible space on behalf of law enforcement, it is prohibited by Art. 5(1)(h) except in the narrowly listed cases with prior judicial or administrative authorisation. A private retailer running the same watchlist for shoplifting is not caught by 5(1)(h), which is addressed to law enforcement, but is squarely in Annex III point 1(a) as a high-risk deployer with Art. 26 obligations and, in most member states, a national law problem on top. Two further boundaries: retaining faces captured from the camera feed to build or grow that watchlist is untargeted scraping from CCTV and is prohibited under Art. 5(1)(e); and any partner application claiming to infer ethnicity, religion, political affiliation, union membership or sexual orientation from video is prohibited under Art. 5(1)(g) and should never be installed. If cameras cover employee work areas and the analytics feed attendance or productivity data, Annex III point 4 employment attaches as well.","docs_url":"https://www.axis.com/products/analytics","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"milestone-xprotect","name":"Milestone XProtect","vendor":"Milestone Systems","category":"Video Management Software (VMS)","walkthrough_category":"biometrics","prevalence":4,"aliases":["milestone","xprotect","milestone xprotect","xprotect corporate","xprotect expert","milestone systems","briefcam"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Centralized Search","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"The native search in XProtect Smart Client: search across alarms, human motion, events, bookmarks and metadata within a chosen timeframe, aggregating whatever metadata the connected cameras and analytics produce. On its own this is search over camera-generated metadata rather than a biometric system — the intelligence usually lives in the camera or in a plugin, not in the VMS. It is included in the platform, which is why a client will say 'the AI is built in' when what is built in is the search interface over someone else's analytics.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Third-party video analytics and facial recognition plugins","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Milestone's own positioning is an open platform with more than a thousand third-party applications, and it lists BriefCam as the video analytics software alongside XProtect (both Milestone and BriefCam are Canon subsidiaries). Face recognition in an XProtect estate is delivered this way — a separately licensed partner application that pushes match alerts into XProtect's Alarm Manager — not by XProtect itself. That matters for the AI Act provider/deployer split: the customer is the deployer, the plugin vendor is the provider, and Milestone is neither for that function. Get the plugin inventory and each plugin's licence, because the VMS licence tells you nothing about whether faces are being matched. Confirm per-module capability with the analytics vendor rather than assuming from the platform name.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"License Plate Recognition","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"A separately licensed XProtect integration for reading and tracking vehicle plates. A registration plate is not biometric data, so this is not an Annex III point 1 system, but it produces a persistent, searchable record of an identifiable person's movements and is frequently the component that triggers the works-council or DPA conversation in a car-park or logistics deployment. Worth cataloguing next to the biometric components even though it is legally distinct.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Left with no Annex III domain on purpose: XProtect out of the box is a video management platform, and whether it becomes a biometric system depends wholly on which plugins the customer licensed. The escalation is the same boundary as for Axis but with a different commercial shape, since here the plugin is bought through a Milestone integrator and the client often believes it came with the VMS. Once a face recognition plugin matches people in the video against a reference database, the deployment is a remote biometric identification system under Art. 3(41) and the deployer sits in Annex III point 1(a) with the full Art. 26 obligation set. If it runs in real time in a publicly accessible space and the deployer is, or is acting for, a law enforcement authority, Art. 5(1)(h) prohibits it outside the narrow listed purposes with prior authorisation — and a municipal operator running city-centre cameras is exactly the deployment that walks into this without noticing. Retrospective identification against recorded footage is not caught by 5(1)(h) but is caught by Annex III 1(a) and by the Art. 26(10) authorisation requirement where law enforcement is the user. Building a watchlist by harvesting faces out of your own recorded footage is untargeted scraping from CCTV and is prohibited under Art. 5(1)(e), regardless of who the deployer is. Milestone itself makes clear that facial recognition is only to be used where local law and policy permit, and puts that verification on the administrator — meaning it lands on the client, and on you.","docs_url":"https://www.milestonesys.com/products/software/xprotect/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"signicat","name":"Signicat Digital Identity Platform","vendor":"Signicat","category":"Digital Identity Orchestration / eID Brokerage","walkthrough_category":"biometrics","prevalence":4,"aliases":["signicat","signicat eid hub","signicat identity proofing","signicat wallet hub","encap","connectid"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"ID Document and Biometric Verification","capabilities":["document_analysis","biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"A licensable module, separate from the eID Hub, that does optical or NFC-based document verification, a one-to-one face match between the user's selfie and the portrait on the document or passport chip, and a liveness check against spoofs and deepfakes — targeted at eIDAS Level of Assurance High. Signicat presents this alongside integrated solutions such as VideoID and ReadID, and its developer documentation carries provider-specific integration pages, so a Nordic customer frequently ends up with a third-party biometric engine running under a Signicat contract. The single most useful question here is: which sub-processor actually performs the face match, and is it named in the DPA? Consultants routinely assume Signicat builds all of this itself and it is not safe to assume.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"eID and Wallet Hub","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Listed here deliberately so it is ruled out rather than swept in. The eID Hub brokers roughly 35 European national eID schemes — Norwegian and Swedish BankID, MitID, FTN, iDIN, itsme and so on — through one integration, and Signicat has extended it to host EU Digital Identity Wallets ahead of the end-2026 eIDAS 2.0 deadline for member states to offer a wallet. This is protocol and credential brokerage, not machine learning: no model, no biometric template, no inference about a person. It is the largest part of most Nordic Signicat contracts and it is not an AI system.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":["biometrics"],"escalation_note":"Signicat is the Nordic default and the entry most often misclassified in both directions. The eID and Wallet Hub is not an AI system at all — brokering a BankID or MitID login involves no model and no biometric processing by Signicat — so do not let it inflate a client's AI inventory. The ID Document and Biometric Verification module is a genuine biometric AI system, but it performs one-to-one verification against the user's own document, which Art. 3(42) treats as verification and Annex III point 1(a) therefore excludes. The two structural risks are contractual rather than technical: first, where the biometric engine is a third-party sub-processor, the client may be the AI Act deployer while having no visibility into the provider's Art. 16 documentation, so insist the sub-processor be named; second, if a deployer asks Signicat to orchestrate a duplicate-applicant or repeat-face check across its user base, that is one-to-many identification and the carve-out no longer applies. Art. 5(1)(e), (g) and (h) are not reachable through the product as sold — there is no scraping capability, no inference of protected characteristics and no public-space surveillance component.","docs_url":"https://www.signicat.com/products/identity-proofing/id-document-and-biometric-verification","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"entrust-idv-onfido","name":"Entrust Identity Verification (formerly Onfido)","vendor":"Entrust","category":"Remote Identity Verification / KYC Onboarding","walkthrough_category":"biometrics","prevalence":3,"aliases":["onfido","entrust idv","onfido atlas","atlas ai","onfido studio","entrust onfido","known faces"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Document report (Atlas AI)","capabilities":["document_analysis","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Atlas AI is the in-house model stack that reads a photographed or NFC-read identity document, extracts the data fields, and classifies the document as genuine, suspected forgery or unreadable. There is no non-AI mode: if you have licensed the platform, this runs on every applicant, and in a Full Auto configuration it returns a clear/consider verdict with no human in the loop, which is what actually gates account opening. The 2024-era Onfido branding is being folded into Entrust IDV; the developer docs now live at documentation.identity.entrust.com and the legacy Onfido SDKs are on a deprecation path. Ask the system owner whether Full Auto or a hybrid review queue is configured, because that determines whether an applicant is refused by a model or by a person.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Facial Similarity report (photo, video, Motion)","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Compares the live selfie, video or Motion capture against the photo on the identity document — a strict one-to-one comparison, plus presentation-attack and injection-attack detection. This is biometric verification in the Art. 3(42) sense: the applicant is actively participating and the purpose is only to confirm the claimed identity. It is the check that makes the whole product a special-category (GDPR Art. 9) processing operation, so the DPIA and the retention period for the face media are the first two things to ask for.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Known Faces report","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"This is the one that changes the legal picture. Known Faces takes the applicant's most recent face capture and searches it one-to-many against every previous applicant in your own Entrust account database (roughly the last twelve months), returning matching applicant IDs so you can spot repeat fraud or duplicate accounts. Entrust's own documentation states that enabling it requires Entrust to retain facial biometric identifiers for future comparison and that the deployer must inform users and obtain permission. Fuzzy name matching, which distinguishes a genuine returning user from a fraudster reusing a face under a new name, is off by default and has to be switched on via Client Support. Only applicants who previously completed a Facial Similarity report are in the index.","domains":["biometrics"]}],"typical_domains":["biometrics"],"escalation_note":"Facial Similarity on its own is one-to-one biometric verification with the applicant actively involved, which Art. 3(42) and Recital 15 treat as verification rather than remote biometric identification — it is therefore outside Annex III point 1(a), though still Art. 9 GDPR special-category data and subject to Art. 50 transparency. Known Faces is the escalation: it is a one-to-many search against a face database you are asked to let Entrust build and keep. Whether that lands in Annex III 1(a) turns on the word 'remote' in Art. 3(41), which requires identification 'without their active involvement' — a consenting applicant taking a selfie arguably fails that test, so this is a genuinely contested boundary and should go to counsel rather than be waved through. The hard line is Art. 5(1)(e): populating or enriching that Known Faces index with facial images scraped from the internet or pulled from CCTV would be a prohibited practice. The product as sold does not do that — it indexes only your own consented applicants — but the configuration boundary is exactly there. Nothing in the product infers race, religion, political opinion, trade union membership or sexual orientation, so Art. 5(1)(g) is not engaged.","docs_url":"https://documentation.identity.entrust.com/guide/known-faces-report","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"idnow","name":"IDnow Identity Verification Platform (AutoIdent / VideoIdent)","vendor":"IDnow","category":"Remote Identity Verification / KYC Onboarding","walkthrough_category":"biometrics","prevalence":3,"aliases":["idnow","autoident","videoident","idnow esign","idnow online ident"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AutoIdent","capabilities":["document_analysis","biometric_categorisation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"active","notes":"IDnow's fully automated flow: AI document analysis with fraud checks across a large document library, optional NFC read of the chip, a biometric selfie matched one-to-one against the document or chip portrait, and passive liveness aimed at synthetic identities, bots, deepfakes and video injection attacks. IDnow is the dominant IDV vendor in German-speaking Europe and reports 115 million identities verified annually for 600+ clients across 215 issuing authorities. Because it is used for German GwG-regulated onboarding and for eIDAS qualified signature issuance, a failed check does not just block an account — it blocks a legally significant act.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"VideoIdent","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","document_analysis"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"The human-in-the-loop variant: a trained ident specialist runs a live video call while AI-based document and face checks support them, and the specialist makes the call. IDnow was moving more than a million video identifications a month across Europe as of 2026. This is materially different from AutoIdent for compliance purposes — the AI is decision support, not the decision — and it is often the fallback path when AutoIdent rejects someone, which is exactly the human review a client should be able to point to. Confirm the fallback actually exists in the customer's configuration rather than assuming it.","domains":["biometrics"]}],"typical_domains":["biometrics"],"escalation_note":"Both products are one-to-one biometric verification of a consenting applicant against their own identity document or passport chip — Art. 3(42) verification, outside Annex III point 1(a). The distinction that matters commercially is between AutoIdent and VideoIdent: AutoIdent in full-auto mode produces an automated decision about a natural person with legal effect (account refused, qualified signature not issued), which engages GDPR Art. 22 and makes the availability of a human review path a hard requirement, whereas VideoIdent keeps a person in the loop by design. IDnow does not sell one-to-many identification against a face database, so Annex III 1(a) is not reachable through normal use; Art. 5(1)(e) would only come into play if a deployer tried to retain rejected applicants' face images to build a searchable repeat-fraudster index, which would need its own lawful basis and, if fed from scraped or CCTV sources, would be prohibited. Nothing here infers protected characteristics, so Art. 5(1)(g) is not engaged. Watch the German market specifically: BaFin-supervised deployments have documentation duties that arrive before the AI Act ones.","docs_url":"https://www.idnow.io/products/idnow-autoident/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"jumio","name":"Jumio Platform","vendor":"Jumio","category":"Remote Identity Verification / KYC Onboarding","walkthrough_category":"biometrics","prevalence":3,"aliases":["jumio","jumio kyx","kyx platform","jumio liveness premium","selfie.done","jumio netverify"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"ID Verification (document + selfie face match)","capabilities":["document_analysis","biometric_categorisation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"active","notes":"The core onboarding flow: capture a government ID, extract and validate the data, capture a selfie, match the two. Jumio positions this at large banks, marketplaces and fintechs and reports over a billion verifications processed. The 'KYX platform' name from the 2023-2024 marketing has largely given way to 'the Jumio Platform' in current material, so expect both names in contracts. In fully automated configurations the model's verdict is the onboarding decision, which is the point at which a person is refused a service by an algorithm.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Jumio Liveness Premium","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"A separately-sold upgrade over standard liveness, generally available since June 2025, using a patented active-illumination technique — randomised colour sequences projected onto the face, with AI analysis of device positioning and lighting response — to catch deepfakes and camera-injection attacks. Jumio reports more than 100 million authentication transactions on it and it took Gold in the biometrics category of the 2026 Cybersecurity Excellence Awards. Standard-tier customers do not get it; check the SKU, because a client who believes they have deepfake protection and is on the base tier does not.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"selfie.DONE reusable identity / face reauthentication","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Turns the onboarding selfie into a persistent, reusable biometric credential so a returning user can be re-authenticated by face, or verified once and reused across services. This is the feature that quietly converts a one-off KYC event into indefinite retention of a face template, and in a reusable-identity model potentially into a template shared beyond the original controller. The retention period, the controller/processor split and whether the template ever leaves the original relying party are the three questions to put in writing.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"AML Screening and Jumio Watch","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Automated screening of the verified customer against sanctions, PEP and adverse-media lists, with Jumio Watch providing ongoing monitoring after onboarding rather than a single point-in-time check. This is name and attribute matching rather than biometric processing, but it produces a risk classification attached to a named individual that can freeze an account, so it belongs in the same assessment even though it is not an Annex III point 1 system.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":["biometrics"],"escalation_note":"The document-plus-selfie flow and Liveness Premium are one-to-one biometric verification with an actively participating subject: Art. 3(42) verification, outside Annex III point 1(a), still Art. 9 GDPR and still Art. 50 transparency. The escalation to watch is selfie.DONE: a reusable identity built on a stored face template is only verification for as long as each use is a one-to-one check against the template belonging to the person making the claim. If the platform is ever configured to search a new selfie across a population of stored templates — to find duplicate accounts, or to answer 'who is this' — it becomes one-to-many identification and the carve-out is gone. Building or enriching that template store with facial images obtained by untargeted scraping from the internet or CCTV would cross Art. 5(1)(e), which is prohibited outright; Jumio does not sell that and there is no indication it does it, but that is where the configuration boundary lies. AML screening is not a biometrics question at all — assess it separately, and note it is not Annex III point 5 creditworthiness either.","docs_url":"https://www.jumio.com/products/liveness-premium/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"veriff","name":"Veriff","vendor":"Veriff","category":"Remote Identity Verification / KYC Onboarding","walkthrough_category":"biometrics","prevalence":3,"aliases":["veriff","veriff idv","veriff liveness","veriff age estimation","veriff biometric authentication"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Identity & Document Verification (Full Auto / Hybrid)","capabilities":["document_analysis","biometric_categorisation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Veriff's core flow reads the identity document (12,500+ document types across 230+ countries per the vendor), extracts the data, runs a face match against the document portrait and combines it with 1,000+ device and behavioural signals into an approve/decline decision. Veriff sells this in a 'Full Auto' mode where the model decides alone and a 'Hybrid' mode where borderline sessions go to a human reviewer. Full Auto is the mode that turns a failed session into an automated refusal of service to a natural person, so establish which one the customer bought before assessing anything else.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Liveness","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Passive liveness — the user takes a selfie without being asked to blink, smile or nod — analysed for presentation attacks (printed photos, masks, screen replays) and AI-generated or injected selfies. Veriff states conformance to ISO/IEC 30107-3 Level 1 and 2 presentation attack detection, independently tested by the NIST/NVLAP-accredited iBeta lab, which is the certification a procurement questionnaire should actually ask for. Passive liveness is a genuine transparency problem: the user is not told a biometric anti-spoofing model is running because there is nothing for them to do.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Biometric Authentication","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Re-authentication of a returning user by face against the template captured at onboarding, sold as a separate product from the onboarding verification. It requires the customer to have agreed to persistent storage of a face template rather than deleting the face media after the KYC event — a materially different retention posture from one-off onboarding, and the thing most likely to be missing from the original DPIA.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Age Estimation","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Estimates a user's age band from a selfie, without a document, for age-gating. Veriff lists it alongside a separate document-based 'Age Validation' product; Age Estimation is the biometric one. It infers an attribute about a person from their face rather than confirming who they are, which is biometric categorisation in the Art. 3(40) sense and not covered by the verification carve-out. Typically bought by gambling, alcohol, tobacco and adult-content platforms and by retailers facing national age-verification duties.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Fraud Protect / Fraud Intelligence","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Add-on risk scoring layered over the verification session using device analytics, network signals and cross-customer fraud patterns. It profiles the applicant rather than identifying them, and it is what produces a 'decline' on a session where the document and face both passed. Ask what the decline reasons are and whether the applicant is ever told, because an unexplained refusal driven by a fraud score is the part that generates complaints.","domains":["biometrics"]}],"typical_domains":["biometrics"],"escalation_note":"Document verification plus one-to-one face match plus liveness is biometric verification under Art. 3(42): the applicant actively participates and the sole purpose is confirming a claimed identity, so it sits outside Annex III point 1(a). Age Estimation is the entry that escalates — inferring an attribute from a face is biometric categorisation, and Annex III 1(b) captures categorisation according to sensitive or protected attributes. Age is not one of the GDPR Art. 9 categories, so a defensible reading keeps pure age-banding out of Annex III 1(b), but it is close enough to the line that it needs a documented position rather than an assumption, and Art. 50(3) transparency toward the person almost certainly applies. Veriff does not offer an untargeted face-scraping capability, so Art. 5(1)(e) is not in play, and nothing in the product deduces race, political opinion, trade union membership, religion or sexual orientation, so Art. 5(1)(g) is not engaged. If a customer ever asks to point the face-matching engine at a database of previously-seen faces to catch repeat applicants, that is the moment the one-to-one carve-out stops applying.","docs_url":"https://www.veriff.com/product/identity-verification","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"idemia-visionpass","name":"IDEMIA VisionPass","vendor":"IDEMIA","category":"Biometric Physical Access Control / Time and Attendance","walkthrough_category":"biometrics","prevalence":2,"aliases":["idemia","visionpass","visionpass sp","morphowave","morphomanager","morpho","idemia access control","sigma"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"VisionPass face recognition terminal","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"active","notes":"A wall-mounted face recognition reader that opens a door. It combines 3D, visible-light and infrared cameras with IDEMIA's face recognition algorithms, tolerates changes in hairstyle, glasses and helmets, and works across lighting conditions; IDEMIA cites results from iBeta anti-spoofing evaluation for its presentation-attack defences, and the hardware is rated IP65/IK07 for outdoor and industrial doors. Three variants exist — VisionPass, the compact VisionPass SP, and VisionPass SP with Fingerscan. There is no non-AI mode: the terminal's only function is to recognise a face and grant or deny passage, which is an automated decision about a person taken in under a second with no human in the loop. Whether a given door is configured as identification (walk up, terminal works out who you are from the enrolled population) or verification (badge or PIN first, then the face confirms the claim) is a per-door setting and it is the setting that decides the legal classification.","domains":["biometrics","employment"]},{"feature_name":"Time and attendance capture","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"IDEMIA lists staff time-and-attendance tracking as a core use case for its access control range, alongside site access and access to enterprise systems. The terminals feed timestamped biometric events into MorphoManager or into a third-party physical access control system, and from there frequently into payroll or workforce management. That data flow is the part clients forget to declare: the same reader that opens a door is also producing a continuous, biometrically-authenticated record of when each named employee arrived, left and moved between zones.","domains":["biometrics","employment"]}],"typical_domains":["biometrics","employment"],"escalation_note":"This is the entry where the verification/identification distinction has the most practical bite, and it is set per door. Configured as verification — the employee presents a badge or PIN and the face confirms the claim one-to-one — it is Art. 3(42) biometric verification and outside Annex III point 1(a). Configured as identification, where a person simply walks up and the terminal searches their face against every template enrolled on the reader to work out who they are, it is one-to-many biometric identification; whether it is also 'remote' under Art. 3(41) is arguable, since the subject deliberately presents themselves to a reader, but a controlled entrance is a much weaker case for active involvement than a KYC selfie and should be treated as an Annex III point 1(a) candidate. Independently of that, biometric access control in a workplace engages Annex III point 4(b) once the event data is used to monitor or evaluate workers — and time-and-attendance feeding payroll is exactly that use. In Nordic and German deployments the works council or union consultation and the national employment-law position on mandatory biometrics usually bite harder and earlier than the AI Act; a Swedish or Norwegian employer generally needs a genuine, non-biometric alternative for employees who refuse. Two prohibitions to name explicitly: enrolling faces captured from site CCTV rather than from a consented enrolment session would be untargeted scraping under Art. 5(1)(e); and any use of the same terminals or IDEMIA's wider portfolio to sort people by ethnicity, religion, political affiliation, union membership or sexual orientation is prohibited under Art. 5(1)(g). The product as sold does none of this — it matches a face to an enrolled employee record — but IDEMIA's public-security business does sell one-to-many identification to state customers, so be clear which IDEMIA contract you are actually looking at.","docs_url":"https://www.idemia.com/visionpass","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"iproov","name":"iProov Biometric Solution Suite","vendor":"iProov","category":"Face Biometric Liveness / Genuine Presence Assurance","walkthrough_category":"biometrics","prevalence":2,"aliases":["iproov","dynamic liveness","genuine presence assurance","gpa","flashmark","express liveness"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Dynamic Liveness (Genuine Presence Assurance)","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"active","notes":"iProov's high-assurance face check. The device screen fires a unique sequence of colours (patented Flashmark) at the user's face and the reflected illumination is analysed server-side to prove the person is physically present at that instant, defeating replayed video, deepfakes and injected streams. It is passive — no blinking or head-turning — and it answers three questions: right person, real person, right now. iProov states it is the first vendor to meet the biometric verification requirements of NIST SP 800-63-4 and to reach CEN/TS 18099 High for injection attack detection, which is the evidence to ask for rather than accuracy percentages.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Express Liveness","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"The low-friction tier: a near-instant selfie analysed for presentation attacks (masks, printed photos, screen replays) and device-level spoofing, without the Flashmark illumination sequence. Intended for repeat authentication and lower-risk journeys where conversion matters more than assurance. Deployments usually run both tiers and route by risk, so establish which journeys fall to which tier — that routing rule is the real control.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"iProov Workforce Solution Suite","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Announced March 2026, this applies the same face biometric to employees rather than customers — verified onboarding of new starters, help-desk password reset, step-up for privileged actions and verified participants in meetings. It is a separate suite from the consumer-facing IDV products. The moment it lands on staff rather than customers, the Annex III point 4 employment questions attach alongside the biometrics ones, and works-council or union consultation becomes a live issue in Nordic and German deployments.","domains":["biometrics","employment"]}],"typical_domains":["biometrics"],"escalation_note":"iProov is the clearest case in this category of a product that stays on the safe side of the line by design: everything it sells is one-to-one verification of a consenting, actively-participating user against a template or document photo they supplied. That is biometric verification under Art. 3(42), explicitly carved out of Annex III point 1(a), and it is worth stating plainly to a client who assumes 'facial recognition' automatically means high risk. iProov does not operate or sell a one-to-many identification database, so Art. 5(1)(e) untargeted scraping and Art. 5(1)(h) real-time remote identification in public spaces are not reachable through the product as sold. The realistic escalation is the Workforce suite: face-based authentication for staff is still verification, but if it is wired into attendance, productivity monitoring or access logs used in performance or disciplinary decisions, Annex III 4(b) (monitoring and evaluating behaviour of workers) is engaged and the deployer obligations in Art. 26 follow. Also note there is a physical access variant — the same one-to-one logic holds only while a credential or claim of identity accompanies the face; a walk-up terminal that identifies whoever appears is a different system.","docs_url":"https://www.iproov.com/liveness-detection","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"nuance-gatekeeper","name":"Nuance Gatekeeper","vendor":"Microsoft (Nuance Communications)","category":"Voice Biometrics / Contact Centre Authentication (legacy, off sale)","walkthrough_category":"biometrics","prevalence":2,"aliases":["nuance gatekeeper","gatekeeper","nuance security suite","nuance voice biometrics","nss"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Voice biometric authentication (opt-in enrolment)","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Enrols a caller's voiceprint on an opt-in basis and then verifies them on every subsequent call from natural conversation, with no security questions or shared secrets. Where it is integrated with Dynamics 365 Customer Service, administrators enable Gatekeeper per channel in the omnichannel configuration and agents can see the caller's consent history in the agent desktop — that consent-history surface is a genuinely useful artefact for an audit. Opt-in enrolment is a meaningfully better posture than the passive enrolment some competitors default to.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Suspected fraudster watchlist","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Compares inbound callers against a watchlist of suspected fraudster voiceprints and alerts the agent to act under the organisation's fraud policy. Agents can also manually add a caller they consider suspicious to the list. This is the feature to interrogate: it is a one-to-many voice search, and a human agent's suspicion is enough to place a named person's voiceprint on a list that then affects how they are treated on every future call, usually with no notification and no route to contest it.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Synthetic speech and playback detection","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Layered detectors for recorded-audio playback and synthetic (text-to-speech or cloned) voices, combined with device, network and location signals to score a call before it reaches the agent. Functionally comparable to Pindrop Pulse, but note the product status below — a client relying on this for deepfake defence in 2026 is relying on a de-emphasised product.","domains":["biometrics"]}],"typical_domains":["biometrics"],"escalation_note":"First, the commercial reality, because it changes the advice: this product is being wound down. The nuance.com Gatekeeper product page now redirects to Microsoft's health-solutions site, Microsoft retired the related Azure AI Speaker Recognition service on 30 September 2025, and the on-premises Nuance Security Suite is on an end-of-life path. If you find Gatekeeper in an estate, the compliance question and the exit-planning question arrive together. On the substance: opt-in voice authentication is one-to-one verification under Art. 3(42) and sits outside Annex III point 1(a). The fraudster watchlist does not — searching every inbound voice against a database of enrolled fraudster voiceprints to establish who the caller is is one-to-many biometric identification, and it is aggravated by agents being able to add people to that list on suspicion alone, which raises Art. 22 GDPR and fairness questions independently of the AI Act. Art. 5(1)(g) is not engaged: nothing here deduces race, political opinion, trade union membership, religion or sexual orientation from voice, and any vendor claiming to do so from voice should be refused. If the deployer is a law enforcement or public authority rather than a bank, re-run the analysis under Annex III point 6 and Art. 26(10).","docs_url":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/dynamics-365/blog/it-professional/2023/03/21/improve-customer-loyalty-and-reduce-fraud-with-nuance-gatekeeper/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"pindrop","name":"Pindrop (Passport, Protect, Pulse)","vendor":"Pindrop","category":"Voice Biometrics / Contact Centre Fraud Detection","walkthrough_category":"biometrics","prevalence":2,"aliases":["pindrop","pindrop passport","pindrop protect","pindrop pulse","phoneprinting","toneprinting"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Pindrop Passport","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Passive caller authentication that runs while the caller talks to an agent or IVR. It layers voice analysis with Phoneprinting (Pindrop states over 1,300 audio features characterising the call's acoustic path and device) and Toneprinting (keypress and device signals), plus metadata and behaviour, into a real-time authentication decision. The detail that matters legally: Pindrop markets enrolment as frictionless and conversational — voice profiles created during normal conversation, without a PIN or a spoken pass-phrase. That means a voiceprint, Art. 9 special-category data, can be created from a call the customer thought was an ordinary service call. Establish exactly where consent is captured and whether it is opt-in or notice-and-continue, because notice-and-continue is very hard to defend for biometric enrolment in the Nordics.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Pindrop Protect","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Runs alongside Passport and returns a single fraud risk score per call, flagging suspicious activity before or during agent handling. In practice deployments also match incoming callers against a repository of known fraudster voices, which is a one-to-many search rather than a one-to-one check — confirm whether the customer's configuration includes a fraudster voice list and whether it is local to their tenant or shared across Pindrop's customer base, because a cross-customer consortium list changes the controller analysis entirely.","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Pindrop Pulse","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Deepfake and synthetic-speech detection for calls and, since 2026, for virtual meetings — Pindrop states it can flag a synthetic participant within roughly two seconds of speech, and Zoom extended the meetings integration into customer service in 2026. Pulse classifies the audio as human or machine-generated rather than identifying who is speaking, so it is a narrower processing operation than Passport, but it still analyses voice, which is biometric data. Useful to separate in the inventory: a client may want Pulse without ever enrolling a voiceprint.","domains":["biometrics"]}],"typical_domains":["biometrics"],"escalation_note":"Passport used purely as one-to-one verification — this caller claims to be account holder X, does the voice match X's enrolled voiceprint — is Art. 3(42) verification and falls outside Annex III point 1(a), the same as face-based KYC. Two configurations break that. First, a known-fraudster voice list: comparing every inbound caller against a database of voiceprints to work out who they are is one-to-many biometric identification, and while a phone caller is not obviously being identified 'at a distance without their active involvement' under Art. 3(41), a passively-enrolled caller who was never told is much closer to that description than a KYC applicant taking a selfie — treat it as an Annex III point 1(a) candidate and take advice. Second, passive enrolment during ordinary conversation is the real exposure and it is a GDPR Art. 9 and Art. 13/14 problem before it is an AI Act one. Nothing in Pindrop infers race, religion, political opinion, trade union membership or sexual orientation, so Art. 5(1)(g) is not engaged, and a contact centre is not a publicly accessible space for Art. 5(1)(h) purposes. Pindrop does sell into public-sector and law-enforcement-adjacent call handling; if the deployer is a law enforcement authority, the Annex III point 6 questions and the Art. 26(10) authorisation regime for post-remote biometric identification come into play and the analysis restarts.","docs_url":"https://www.pindrop.com/product/pindrop-passport/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"aveva-pi-system","name":"AVEVA PI System (with AVEVA Predictive Analytics and the Industrial AI Assistant on CONNECT)","vendor":"AVEVA (Schneider Electric group)","category":"Operational Data Infrastructure / Industrial Analytics","walkthrough_category":"critical_infrastructure","prevalence":3,"aliases":["pi system","osisoft pi","aveva pi","pi historian","aveva predictive analytics","aveva connect"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AVEVA Predictive Analytics","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"notes":"Anomaly detection and fault diagnostics over PI historian data, giving time-to-failure forecasting and prescribed remedial actions weeks or months ahead of a failure. It is a no-code product with model templates, and it also supports 'bring your own algorithm' in Python — which is important, because the customer's own data-science team may have inserted models nobody has documented. Sold as a distinct product, not part of a PI Server licence; AVEVA's own reference customers are power generation, oil and gas and chemicals. Ask whether any BYO models are in the loop and who validated them.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Industrial AI Assistant (CONNECT)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"notes":"A generative assistant on AVEVA's CONNECT industrial intelligence platform that answers questions over operational data and cites the underlying data sources, which AVEVA markets specifically as a data-integrity/traceability feature. It is only reachable if the customer has moved PI data onto CONNECT — a large number of European PI estates are still entirely on-premises and therefore have no exposure at all. AVEVA has not published clear entitlement detail for the assistant, so treat the licensing answer as something to get from the account team rather than assume. AVEVA also references a 'Vision AI Assistant'; scope was not verifiable at time of research.","default_state":"available_not_enabled","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Deliberately empty. The PI System is a historian — an operational data infrastructure layer sitting beside the control system, deployed as readily in a brewery as in a transmission operator. Reading and analysing process data is not the management or operation of supply, and an AI that answers questions about that data is not a safety component. There is no Annex III point 2 exposure from PI System itself, and consultants should resist tagging it just because a DSO owns it. Two real boundaries: first, an AVEVA Predictive Analytics model that writes back into a closed loop controlling water, gas, heating or electricity supply is a safety component and is in scope for that model only; second, AVEVA System Platform / SCADA deployments in a utility are the operational layer where the Annex III point 2 question genuinely lives — assess those separately from the historian. Cybersecurity-only AI functions are expressly outside high-risk classification per Recital 55.","docs_url":"https://www.aveva.com/en/solutions/digital-transformation/artificial-intelligence/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"rockwell-factorytalk","name":"FactoryTalk Design Studio with Copilot","vendor":"Rockwell Automation","category":"Industrial Automation / Generative AI Engineering Assistant","walkthrough_category":"critical_infrastructure","prevalence":3,"aliases":["factorytalk","factorytalk design studio","rockwell copilot","factorytalk optix","logix designer copilot"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"FactoryTalk Design Studio Copilot","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","natural_language_query"],"notes":"Generative AI inside Rockwell's cloud-based design environment: it writes ladder and structured-text PLC code from natural language, explains a selected block of existing code, and — newer and more consequential — adds, removes, configures and assigns devices on the engineer's behalf across large device estates. It targets ControlLogix 5590, ControlLogix/CompactLogix L8 and GuardLogix safety controllers. Note the GuardLogix support: Copilot can operate in a project that also contains safety logic, so ask explicitly whether the customer permits Copilot in safety-instrumented projects and who signs off the safety routine. Most Rockwell shops in Europe still engineer in Studio 5000 Logix Designer and have no Copilot exposure at all; Design Studio is a separate SaaS subscription.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"FactoryTalk Optix AI-guided operator instructions","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","basic_automation"],"notes":"Rockwell, with Microsoft, has adapted small language models (Phi-3 family) to deliver AI-guided step-by-step instructions to machine operators through the FactoryTalk Optix HMI, assisting with process and device operations at the edge. It advises the operator at the panel rather than commanding the machine. Optix is itself a separately licensed HMI/IIoT platform, so this only exists where the customer has chosen Optix over classic FactoryTalk View. Ask whether the guidance is ever presented as mandatory procedure, because that changes the human-oversight picture.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Deliberately empty. Rockwell's installed base is overwhelmingly discrete and process manufacturing — automotive, food and beverage, packaging, life sciences — not utility supply, and code generation happens in an engineering tool before commissioning, so it is not a safety component under Annex III point 2. The sharp question with Rockwell is not Annex III at all: because Copilot can generate logic for GuardLogix safety controllers, any AI-assisted content that ends up in a safety function is caught by the Art. 6(1) product-safety route through the Machinery Regulation and the site's functional-safety regime (IEC 61508/62061), which demand traceable authorship and validation of safety logic. Annex III point 2 only engages if the machine being programmed is itself a safety component in water, gas, heating, electricity supply or road traffic, and the AI output reaches it without engineering sign-off. Art. 50 transparency applies to the operator-facing Optix guidance.","docs_url":"https://www.rockwellautomation.com/en-us/products/software/factorytalk/design-studio.html","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"siemens-industrial-copilot","name":"Siemens Industrial Copilot","vendor":"Siemens","category":"Industrial Automation / Generative AI Engineering Assistant","walkthrough_category":"critical_infrastructure","prevalence":3,"aliases":["industrial copilot","siemens copilot","tia portal copilot","simatic copilot","engineering copilot"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Industrial Copilot for Engineering (TIA Portal)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","natural_language_query","summarization"],"notes":"Generates and explains SIMATIC S7 PLC code (SCL/LAD) and WinCC Unified HMI screens from natural-language prompts inside TIA Portal, and answers questions against Siemens manuals. It is a separate subscription bought per engineering seat and offered through Siemens Xcelerator — a plant running TIA Portal V19/V20 does not have it unless someone bought it. It acts on engineering artefacts, not on the running process: the generated code still goes through the customer's normal review, simulation (PLCSIM/Logix-equivalent) and commissioning gates. Ask the automation lead whether generated code is reviewed by a named engineer before download, and whether prompts and project context leave the plant to Azure OpenAI or run on the on-premises SIMATIC IPC bundle.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Industrial Copilot for Operations / Maintenance","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","summarization","natural_language_query"],"notes":"Conversational troubleshooting for machine operators and maintenance staff — describe a fault, get probable causes, manual extracts and suggested corrective steps. Siemens offers it as an on-premises hardware/software bundle on the SIMATIC IPC 1047E with NVIDIA NIM, which is a strong signal it is a deliberate, separately procured deployment rather than something that appears in an existing estate. It advises a human; it does not write to the controller. The main compliance question is transparency to workers under Art. 50 and whether operator interactions are logged and used for performance monitoring, which would pull it into the employment domain.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Deliberately empty. Annex III point 2 covers AI used as a *safety component* in the management and operation of road traffic or the supply of water, gas, heating and electricity — a component whose failure endangers life or health at scale. An engineering copilot that drafts PLC code offline, and an operator chatbot that suggests fixes, are neither: a human engineer commissions the code and a human operator acts on the advice. A plant that runs Siemens is not automatically high risk. It crosses into Annex III point 2 only if Siemens or an integrator wires Copilot output into a closed loop that writes setpoints or safety logic into a controller governing an electricity, gas, heating or water supply asset without human commissioning — at which point the same code is also caught by the Art. 6(1) product-safety route via the Machinery Regulation. Separately, Art. 50 transparency applies because a natural person is interacting with an AI system, and if maintenance-copilot transcripts are used to assess individual worker performance the deployment becomes Annex III point 4 (employment).","docs_url":"https://www.siemens.com/en-us/company/insights/generative-ai-industrial-copilot/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"abb-genix","name":"ABB Genix Industrial IoT and AI Suite","vendor":"ABB","category":"Industrial Analytics / Asset Performance Management","walkthrough_category":"critical_infrastructure","prevalence":2,"aliases":["genix","abb genix","abb ability genix","genix apm","abb ability"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Genix APM (Asset Performance Management)","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"notes":"Monitors turbines, compressors, breakers and instrumentation, estimates remaining useful life and prescribes maintenance actions, feeding lifecycle and capital-planning decisions. It converges OT, engineering and IT data into a single asset model — which is the part that matters for a consultant, because it means Genix often becomes the place where plant data from many vendors' systems lands. Licensed as an application on the Genix suite, priced per asset or per site; it is not part of an 800xA or ABB drives purchase. Ask which decisions are taken on the strength of an RUL number without an engineer's countersignature.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Genix APM Copilot","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"notes":"A generative-AI layer over Genix APM built on Azure OpenAI that lets reliability engineers ask about asset condition in natural language and get contextualised summaries rather than navigating dashboards. ABB describes it as a user-experience layer on top of the existing analytics, not a new decision engine. Because it runs on Azure OpenAI, confirm the data-residency terms with ABB — for a European utility this is usually the first question procurement asks. Note ABB has folded the older 'ABB Ability' branding into 'Genix'; a customer inventory that still says 'ABB Ability Genix' is describing the same product.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Deliberately empty — Genix is deployed across mining, pulp and paper, marine, oil and gas and general manufacturing as often as in utilities, so the domain follows the deployment, not the product. Genix APM's outputs are maintenance and capital-planning recommendations consumed by humans; that is not a safety component under Annex III point 2 and does not become one because the asset happens to be a generator. The boundary is control authority: if a Genix analytic is allowed to derate, isolate or trip supply-critical plant automatically, that specific analytic is a safety component and Annex III point 2 applies to it — not to the whole suite. A separate and more likely trap: ABB also sells emissions-monitoring analytics on Genix, which are a regulatory-reporting use, not an Annex III use, and should not be escalated.","docs_url":"https://global.abb/group/en/technology/did-you-know/abb-ability-genix-industrial-analytics-and-ai-suite","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"yokogawa-centum-vp","name":"CENTUM VP (OpreX Control and Safety System)","vendor":"Yokogawa Electric","category":"Distributed Control System / Autonomous Process Control","walkthrough_category":"critical_infrastructure","prevalence":2,"aliases":["centum","centum vp","yokogawa dcs","oprex","fkdpp","prosafe-rs"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Autonomous control AI (FKDPP reinforcement learning agents)","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"notes":"This is the rare case of AI genuinely inside the control loop rather than beside it. Yokogawa's Factorial Kernel Dynamic Policy Programming is a reinforcement-learning algorithm that derives control policies and, per Yokogawa, autonomously responds to situations it has not previously experienced. Multiple coordinated FKDPP agents have been commissioned in production at Aramco's Fadhili gas plant, integrated with CENTUM VP, directly controlling acid-gas removal. It is engineered per plant under a project contract, not switched on from a menu — but a customer who has it has an AI writing setpoints to a live process. Ask for the agent's operating envelope, the fallback to conventional PID or operator control, and how the guard bands were validated.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"CENTUM VP R7 scenario-based operational decision support","capabilities":["basic_automation","summarization"],"notes":"The R7 generation presents operators with predicted future plant scenarios drawn from captured operational expertise and can perform operations in the operator's place — Yokogawa's own framing is a plant-operation style that merges experiential knowledge with AI. Predictive event detection flags developing abnormal process conditions before an alarm. This ships as part of the R7 platform capability set rather than as a bolt-on, but has to be configured and licensed per project; an installed R6 estate does not have it. Confirm which specific loops, if any, are permitted to execute autonomously versus advise.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Left empty on purpose, but this is the entry most likely to escalate. CENTUM VP is a general process DCS installed across refining, chemicals, pharmaceuticals, pulp, LNG, power and water treatment, so the Annex III domain depends entirely on what plant it runs. Where FKDPP or R7 autonomous operation controls a plant that supplies gas, heating, electricity or water to the public, that AI function is a safety component in the management and operation of that supply and is squarely Annex III point 2 high-risk — provider obligations for Yokogawa, Art. 26 deployer obligations plus Art. 14 human oversight for the operator. Where the same technology optimises a chemical reactor or a refinery unit that feeds an industrial customer rather than a public supply network, Annex III point 2 does not apply, though the Art. 6(1) product-safety route may via the Machinery Regulation and the site's IEC 61511 functional-safety case. Do not let the presence of ProSafe-RS confuse the assessment: the safety instrumented system itself is deterministic logic, not AI, and is regulated under IEC 61511 rather than the AI Act.","docs_url":"https://www.yokogawa.com/solutions/products-and-services/control/control-and-safety-system/distributed-control-systems-dcs/centum-vp/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"schneider-ecostruxure-adms","name":"EcoStruxure ADMS / One Digital Grid Platform","vendor":"Schneider Electric","category":"Electric Utility / Advanced Distribution Management System","walkthrough_category":"critical_infrastructure","prevalence":2,"aliases":["ecostruxure adms","schneider adms","one digital grid","ecostruxure derms","arcfm","grid ai assistant"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Grid AI Assistant","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","document_analysis"],"notes":"An assistant embedded in EcoStruxure ADMS that lets a control-room operator ask in natural language what a function does, pull the relevant documentation and jump to the right screen, report or pre-configured view. Schneider positions it as productivity and adoption support, and on the published description it navigates and explains rather than recommends switching actions. That distinction is the whole compliance question and should be verified against the customer's actual release, not the brochure. Announced with the One Digital Grid Platform (late 2025 into 2026), so an existing ADMS installation will not have it without an upgrade and a commercial conversation.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":["critical_infrastructure"]},{"feature_name":"Estimated Time of Restoration (ETR)","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"notes":"Generates and continuously updates outage restoration estimates from live grid telemetry, weather forecasts, crew availability and historical outage patterns, for use during storms, wildfires and public safety power shutoffs. The output drives customer communications and crew dispatch, not switching. It matters for vulnerable-customer duties — a bad ETR during a winter outage in the Nordics has real safety consequences even though the model touches no breaker. Part of the One Digital Grid application set, licensed with it.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":["critical_infrastructure"]},{"feature_name":"AI-based network model tuning","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"notes":"Reconciles the GIS/ArcFM network model against operational and customer data to detect and correct mismatches between the digital model of the grid and its real topology. This is quietly the highest-consequence AI in the platform: every ADMS switching recommendation, fault location and load-flow calculation is computed against that model, so a wrong correction propagates into operational decisions. Ask whether corrections are auto-applied or queued for a network-records engineer to approve.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":["critical_infrastructure"]}],"typical_domains":["critical_infrastructure"],"escalation_note":"Tagged because an ADMS is by definition the system through which a distribution system operator manages and operates electricity supply — outage management, switching, network monitoring and SCADA — so the deployment context is never in doubt the way it is for a factory platform. That does not make every AI feature here high risk. Annex III point 2 catches the functions that are safety components of that supply: anything that determines, recommends for one-click execution, or automatically performs switching, isolation, load shedding or protection changes, and — on a proper reading — the network model tuning that those calculations depend on. It does not catch the Grid AI Assistant while it remains a documentation-and-navigation copilot, nor ETR as a customer-communication forecast. Get the customer to enumerate which AI outputs can reach a switching order. If the DSO is a public undertaking or performs a public task, Art. 27 fundamental rights impact assessment may also apply to any high-risk function, and disconnection or load-shedding logic that selects between individual customers should additionally be checked against Annex III point 5(a) (essential services). Note Recital 55: AI used solely for cybersecurity of the grid is not high risk on that basis.","docs_url":"https://www.se.com/ww/en/about-us/newsroom/news/press-releases/schneider-electric-debuts-one-digital-grid-platform-to-help-utilities-modernize-and-address-energy-costs-691af6851937b58c890951a3/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"kapsch-ecotrafix","name":"EcoTrafiX Platform","vendor":"Kapsch TrafficCom","category":"Intelligent Transport Systems / Urban Traffic Management","walkthrough_category":"critical_infrastructure","prevalence":2,"aliases":["ecotrafix","kapsch","kapsch trafficcom","urban traffic management kapsch"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI-driven predictive analytics and traffic strategy","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"notes":"Predicts developing congestion and incidents from historical patterns plus real-time feeds, and proposes or applies routing and network strategies across a city. The next-generation platform launched January 2026 consolidates Kapsch's previously separate urban traffic management, connected-vehicle and mobility-data products into one console, and now ingests TomTom floating car data for flow, incident and travel-time inputs. Delivered as an engineered municipal project, not a self-service subscription — the city buys modules and Kapsch configures them.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":["critical_infrastructure"]},{"feature_name":"Adaptive traffic signal control","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"notes":"Optimises signal timings at junctions in real time in response to measured conditions, including transit and emergency-vehicle priority. This is the function that actually commands road infrastructure: green times, phase order and pedestrian phases are being set by the system. Whether the AI layer or a conventional deterministic adaptive algorithm (SCOOT-lineage or equivalent) computes the plan varies by deployment and must be established site by site — Kapsch markets both under one platform name. The intergreen and conflict-monitoring safety functions remain in the certified signal controller hardware, outside the AI.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":["critical_infrastructure"]}],"typical_domains":["critical_infrastructure"],"escalation_note":"Tagged because road traffic is named in Annex III point 2 and EcoTrafiX has no non-road use. Apply the safety-component test carefully: strategic analytics, congestion dashboards, travel-time information, low-emission-zone monitoring and modal-shift reporting are traffic management information, not safety components, and should not be escalated. Adaptive signal control that sets green times and pedestrian phases in a live network is the safety-component case — a bad phase decision endangers road users at scale — and where an AI model rather than a deterministic algorithm produces those timings, Annex III point 2 applies, with provider obligations on Kapsch and Art. 26 deployer obligations on the city. The certified conflict-monitoring layer in the signal controller does not exempt the AI above it; it limits the failure mode, which belongs in the risk file, not in the classification. City and road authorities are public bodies, so Art. 27 FRIA duties attach to any high-risk function. Watch for ANPR/enforcement modules bolted onto the same platform: those are a different question entirely and can reach Annex III point 6 (law enforcement) or Art. 5(1)(h) if used for real-time remote biometric identification.","docs_url":"https://www.kapsch.net/en/traffic/urban","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"ge-vernova-gridos","name":"GridOS","vendor":"GE Vernova","category":"Electric Utility / Grid Orchestration (ADMS, DERMS, EMS)","walkthrough_category":"critical_infrastructure","prevalence":2,"aliases":["gridos","ge gridos","ge vernova adms","gridos derms","grid orchestration"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Intelligent grid applications (AI/ML in the control room)","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"notes":"GE Vernova's stated design intent for GridOS is to bring AI and machine learning into the control room for proactive and automated grid management, running over a federated grid data fabric with a unified transmission-and-distribution network model. In practice this is a family of analytics across the ADMS, DERMS and planning applications rather than a single named feature, and GE Vernova has not published a feature-level breakdown — so establish with the utility exactly which analytics are live and which are advisory versus executing. GridOS for Transmission arrived in 2026 alongside whitepapers on autonomous grid-edge operations, which signals direction of travel toward less human mediation.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":["critical_infrastructure"]},{"feature_name":"GridOS Visual Intelligence","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"notes":"An AI-enabled asset intelligence application that analyses imagery of grid assets — the typical use is inspection of lines, poles and substation equipment from drone, vehicle or satellite capture — to identify defects and vegetation encroachment. Output feeds inspection and vegetation-management programmes. This is asset condition work, not network operation, and should be assessed separately from the control-room analytics. Licensed as a distinct GridOS application.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":["critical_infrastructure"],"escalation_note":"Tagged because GridOS exists only to run transmission and distribution networks — there is no non-utility deployment of it, so unlike an industrial platform it is inherently in the supply of electricity. The Annex III point 2 line runs through control authority, not through the platform boundary: ADMS and DERMS analytics that perform or determine switching, DER curtailment, load shedding, islanding or restoration sequencing are safety components and are high risk; planning analytics, asset-condition scoring and Visual Intelligence inspection are not, even though they sit in the same portfolio. Push hard on the 'autonomous grid-edge operations' direction GE Vernova published in 2026 — automated DER dispatch at the grid edge without an operator in the loop is the clearest Annex III point 2 case in the whole category. Where the utility is state-owned or discharges a public task, Art. 27 FRIA obligations attach to the high-risk functions, and any logic that curtails or disconnects identifiable customers rather than feeders should also be tested against Annex III point 5(a).","docs_url":"https://www.gevernova.com/software/products/gridos","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"siemens-senseye-predictive-maintenance","name":"Senseye Predictive Maintenance (Senseye Cloud Application)","vendor":"Siemens","category":"Industrial Asset Performance / Predictive Maintenance","walkthrough_category":"critical_infrastructure","prevalence":2,"aliases":["senseye","siemens senseye","senseye cloud","predictive maintenance siemens"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI-driven asset intelligence and failure forecasting","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"notes":"Ingests vibration, current, temperature and historian data from existing machines and automatically builds per-asset behaviour models to forecast time-to-failure and rank which assets need attention first. Siemens explicitly positions it as requiring no data-science team, which means nobody in the plant can usually explain the model. It profiles machines, not people. Sold as a separate cloud subscription priced per asset — it is never bundled with a SIMATIC or TIA licence, so if the customer has it, someone signed for it. Ask who owns model retraining and what happens to a prediction that contradicts the OEM maintenance interval.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Generative AI conversational interface","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","summarization"],"notes":"Added on top of the core Senseye engine so maintenance planners can ask about asset condition in natural language and receive summarised case narratives instead of reading charts. Siemens announced this as an enhancement to the existing product rather than a new SKU. It affects how a human plans work; it issues no instruction to the plant. Note that Senseye also models 'maintainer behaviour' — confirm with the customer whether that data is ever surfaced at individual-technician level.","default_state":"available_not_enabled","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Deliberately empty. Predictive maintenance is the single most common false positive in Annex III point 2 assessments. Forecasting that a pump bearing will fail in six weeks is asset optimisation, not a safety component: the output goes to a maintenance planner who schedules work, and the plant's protection and shutdown systems are untouched. It stays out of Annex III point 2 even when the asset sits in a water or power utility. It would only cross over if the prediction were wired to automatically trip, derate or reconfigure supply-critical plant without a human decision. The realistic escalation here is not Annex III point 2 but Annex III point 4: Senseye models maintainer behaviour as well as machine behaviour, and if those metrics are used to evaluate, allocate work to, or make decisions about individual technicians, the deployment becomes an employment high-risk use and triggers works-council and Art. 26(7) worker-notification duties in most Nordic and German sites.","docs_url":"https://www.siemens.com/en-us/products/industrial-digitalization-services/senseye-cloud-application/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"xylem-vue","name":"Xylem Vue powered by GoAigua","vendor":"Xylem (with Idrica)","category":"Water Utility / Digital Water Operations Platform","walkthrough_category":"critical_infrastructure","prevalence":2,"aliases":["xylem vue","goaigua","idrica","xylem vue powered by goaigua","digital water platform"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Drinking water network and treatment analytics","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"notes":"Analytics over SCADA, telemetry, AMI and hydraulic-model data for a potable-water utility: leak and burst detection, non-revenue-water localisation, demand forecasting, pressure management and treatment-plant optimisation, presented as real-time decision support for network operators. The platform is deliberately vendor-agnostic and ingests data from non-Xylem equipment, so it commonly becomes the utility's single operational view. Delivered as a subscription platform with modules selected per utility; it is not bundled with Xylem pumps or instruments.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":["critical_infrastructure"]},{"feature_name":"Wastewater and sewer network optimisation","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"notes":"Treatment-plant process optimisation and sewer-network management, including storm and overflow handling. Real-time control of storage and pumping during a rain event has direct public-health and flooding consequences, which is what separates this module from ordinary process optimisation. Ask whether the platform issues advisory recommendations to a control room or writes setpoints to pumps, valves and dosing systems — Xylem's marketing does not settle this, and the answer differs by installation.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":["critical_infrastructure"]},{"feature_name":"Water resources early warning","capabilities":["basic_automation"],"notes":"River-basin monitoring and early-warning analytics for water resource management, aimed at authorities and basin operators rather than distribution utilities. Flood and drought early warning informs public protective action, so treat this as a public-safety function and confirm what human authority sits between a model alert and a public warning.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":["critical_infrastructure"]}],"typical_domains":["critical_infrastructure"],"escalation_note":"Tagged because Xylem Vue is sold to and only used by water and wastewater utilities — the supply of water is named explicitly in Annex III point 2, and unlike a generic IIoT platform there is no ordinary-factory deployment to disambiguate. The safety-component test still has to be applied per module. Analytics that detect leaks, forecast demand or rank capital projects are optimisation: the utility decides what to do and Annex III point 2 does not bite. Analytics that automatically dose chemicals, control chlorine residual, adjust network pressure, or operate storm-water storage and overflow are safety components in the supply of water — a wrong output is a public-health or flooding event — and are high risk, with Art. 14 human oversight and Art. 26 deployer duties on the utility and provider duties on Xylem/Idrica. Water-quality monitoring that triggers a boil-water notice sits on the same side of the line. Most European water utilities are public bodies or bodies governed by public law, so Art. 27 fundamental rights impact assessment is likely to attach to any high-risk module, and customer-level analytics used to restrict or disconnect supply should be checked against Annex III point 5(a) instead.","docs_url":"https://www.xylem.com/en-us/brand/xylem-vue/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"axon-ai-era-plan","name":"Axon AI Era Plan","vendor":"Axon","category":"Body-Worn Camera & Digital Evidence / Police AI Suite","walkthrough_category":"law_enforcement","prevalence":1,"aliases":["axon","draft one","axon draft one","ai era plan","axon evidence","evidence.com","axon body","taser"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Draft One","capabilities":["content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Draft One generates a police report narrative from the auto-transcribed audio of an Axon Body 3/4 recording, and the officer then edits, completes the placeholders and signs it. Axon states the model is configured not to speculate or embellish beyond the transcript, that human review and sign-off is mandatory, and that agencies can restrict use by incident type, charge level or arrest status. It is part of the paid AI Era Plan, so a force with Axon cameras but no AI plan does not have it. The two questions worth asking a system owner: is there an audit record showing which submitted reports were AI-drafted, and are officers permitted to use it for incidents involving use of force or arrest.","domains":["law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"Auto-Transcribe","capabilities":["document_analysis"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Automatic speech-to-text over body-worn and interview audio in English, Spanish, French, Italian, Dutch, German and Brazilian Portuguese, which then feeds evidence search, audio redaction and BWC audit workflows. It is the substrate the rest of the plan sits on — Draft One and Brief One both consume its output. Included in the AI Era Plan rather than in base Axon Evidence. Transcription error in a second language is the practical risk to raise, because downstream generated text inherits it.","domains":["law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"Smart Detection","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"Smart Detection finds and follows human forms through uploaded video so a reviewer can jump to the moments where a person is present; Axon documents it as running automatically on Axon Body 3 and Body 4 video as it uploads, with the unlimited version included in the AI Era Plan. Axon describes it as human-form detection, not face recognition, and it does not by itself identify anyone. It is tagged as biometric categorisation here because automated detection and tracking of persons in public-space video is close enough to the boundary that a consultant should not wave it through — confirm in writing with the agency that no face matching or person re-identification is layered on top.","domains":["law_enforcement","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Axon Assistant","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"A voice assistant running on the Axon Body 4 camera that performs live two-way translation during an encounter when no human interpreter is available, and answers general and policy questions for the officer in the field. Part of the AI Era Plan and dependent on Body 4 hardware. The exposure is that machine translation errors happen inside a real police interaction — cautions, consent to search, and rights notifications are exactly the utterances where a mistranslation has legal consequence. Ask whether the agency permits it for rights-bearing communications or only for general dialogue.","domains":["law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"Policy Chat","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"A retrieval assistant over the agency's own uploaded policy documents, returning answers with links to the source policy. It affects officers rather than members of the public and is the lowest-risk item in the plan — closer to an internal knowledge assistant than to an Annex III use. Included in the AI Era Plan. Worth documenting as low-risk explicitly so it does not get swept into the high-risk register alongside Draft One.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Brief One","capabilities":["summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Generates transcript summaries and flags key moments across evidence items so an officer or supervisor can triage a case file without watching everything. Included in the AI Era Plan; distinct from Draft One in that it summarises rather than authoring the report of record. Axon also lists AI Auto-Tagging (evidence metadata enrichment) as early access, which means it can appear in an estate without a purchasing decision — check the early-access opt-ins.","domains":["law_enforcement"]}],"typical_domains":["law_enforcement"],"escalation_note":"Annex III point 6 covers AI used by law enforcement to evaluate the reliability of evidence in the course of investigation or prosecution, and Draft One sits close to that line: it does not evaluate reliability, but it authors the narrative that becomes evidence, from audio that a machine transcribed. Treat Draft One as high-risk unless the agency can show the officer materially reviews and owns the text, and note that Art. 50 transparency and the agency's own disclosure duty to defence and prosecutors are separate obligations from the AI Act tiering. None of the features documented by Axon perform individual predictive policing, so Art. 5(1)(d) is not engaged as shipped — it would be engaged if an agency wired Draft One or Brief One output into a scoring model that ranks named people by likelihood of future offending. Art. 5(1)(h) is not engaged by Smart Detection as documented, because human-form tracking in retrospectively uploaded video is neither real-time nor remote biometric identification; it would be engaged if the agency added face matching against a watchlist to live camera feeds in publicly accessible space. Where Axon is deployed by a private security operator rather than a police force, the Art. 5(1)(h) law-enforcement carve-outs do not apply at all and the analysis is harsher, not softer.","docs_url":"https://www.axon.com/help/axon-ai/axon-ai-era-plan.htm","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"cellebrite-pathfinder","name":"Cellebrite Pathfinder","vendor":"Cellebrite","category":"Digital Forensics / Investigative Analytics","walkthrough_category":"law_enforcement","prevalence":1,"aliases":["cellebrite","pathfinder","cellebrite pathfinder","ufed","inseyets","physical analyzer","cellebrite guardian","cellebrite genesis"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Link Analysis","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","document_analysis"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Maps and visualises relationships between entities — people, locations, events — extracted from seized devices and other case data. The output is a network graph in which non-suspects appear as nodes purely because they appear in a suspect's device. Pathfinder is a separately licensed analytics product on top of the extraction tooling, so a lab that only runs Cellebrite extraction does not have it. Ask what the retention rule is for the analytic graph once the case closes, because that is usually looser than the rule for the underlying extraction.","domains":["law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"Person Resolution","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Correlates phone numbers, user IDs and usernames across platforms to conclude that separate identifiers belong to the same individual. This is identification of a natural person by inference, not by biometrics, and it is the feature most likely to be wrong in a way that matters — a shared handset or a recycled username produces a false merge that then propagates into the link graph. Licensed with Pathfinder. Ask whether the investigator sees a confidence indicator and whether merges can be manually broken.","domains":["law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"Media Categorization and Search","capabilities":["document_analysis","biometric_categorisation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"AI classifies images and video from an extraction into categories (weapons, documents, nudity, faces and similar) so a reviewer can triage tens of thousands of media items. Cellebrite states the categorisation set can be restricted, for example to keep review inside the scope of a search warrant, which is the control a consultant should insist on being configured. Where categorisation groups images by depicted person it edges into biometric categorisation, which is why the tag is applied here. Licensed with Pathfinder; a lighter AI image-analysis capability also exists inside Physical Analyzer / Inseyets.","domains":["law_enforcement","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Topic Detection","capabilities":["document_analysis","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Identifies patterns and recurring subjects across communications including coded terminology and multiple languages, so investigators can find drug, weapon or grooming vocabulary without knowing the slang in advance. False positives here directly generate investigative attention on individuals, so the human-review step is not cosmetic. Licensed with Pathfinder.","domains":["law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"Speech-to-Text and Translations at Scale","capabilities":["document_analysis","content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Transcribes audio and video evidence into searchable text and machine-translates multilingual material at case volume. In practice this is what makes a foreign-language device reviewable at all, and it is also where evidential quality is most contested — a machine translation of an intercepted message can end up quoted in a case file. Licensed with Pathfinder. Cellebrite states publicly that it does not train models on customer data and that it maps its AI governance to the EU AI Act and the NIST AI RMF; get that in the DPA rather than relying on the marketing page.","domains":["law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"AI image analysis in Physical Analyzer / Inseyets","capabilities":["document_analysis"],"default_state":"active_partial","notes":"The core extraction and analysis tooling ships with AI image analysis and customisable categorisation, so a forensics lab that never bought Pathfinder still has some AI running over seized media as part of normal case processing. This is the entry's most useful finding: the AI conversation with a digital forensics unit cannot start at 'do you license Pathfinder'. Confirm which categorisation classes are enabled and whether they are narrowed per warrant.","domains":["law_enforcement"]}],"typical_domains":["law_enforcement"],"escalation_note":"Annex III point 6 applies squarely — this is AI used by law enforcement to evaluate the reliability of evidence and to analyse material in the course of detecting, investigating and prosecuting offences. Pathfinder as documented is retrospective analysis of already-seized material, which is high-risk but not prohibited. Art. 5(1)(d) would be engaged only if an agency used the link graph and topic detection to build a score predicting that a named individual will commit a future offence on the basis of their profile or associations — the platform can be pointed that way, and the fact that the underlying data is lawfully seized does not cure it. Art. 5(1)(h) is not engaged: nothing here performs real-time remote biometric identification in public space. Two adjacent Cellebrite SKUs should be assessed separately rather than assumed in scope: Guardian Investigate (agentic AI workflows, natural-language questioning of case data, CDR normalisation and chat-relationship classification) and Cellebrite Genesis (credit-priced SaaS AI that surfaces traceable investigative leads across 35+ formats and 100+ languages, sold only to law enforcement and government).","docs_url":"https://cellebrite.com/en/products/pathfinder/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"magnet-one","name":"Magnet One","vendor":"Magnet Forensics","category":"Digital Forensics / Investigation Platform","walkthrough_category":"law_enforcement","prevalence":1,"aliases":["magnet","magnet forensics","magnet one","magnet axiom","axiom","magnet copilot","magnet ai","graykey"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Magnet Copilot","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","document_analysis"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"A chat interface inside Magnet Axiom that lets an examiner ask questions of an extraction and surface relevant conversation threads or web history without hand-searching. It launched as a cloud integration and Magnet has since released an offline variant, which matters for European forces that will not send evidence to a vendor cloud. It has to be turned on per deployment and the offline option is a deliberate procurement choice, so treat cloud-versus-offline as the first question, not the second. Ask which model backend the offline build uses and where inference runs.","domains":["law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"Intelligent Search","capabilities":["natural_language_query","document_analysis"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Launched in Magnet Review, this uses natural language to identify leads across thousands of media files, messages, audio and video in seconds. It is a retrieval and ranking function over case material rather than a decision about a person, but what it ranks up is what investigators look at first, which is a real influence on the direction of an investigation. Part of the Magnet One / Magnet Review platform rather than base Axiom.","domains":["law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"Intelligent Insights","capabilities":["summarization","recommendation_profiling","document_analysis"],"default_state":"in_exploration","notes":"Announced April 2026 and available through early access in Magnet Review, this uses LLMs to produce structured investigative summaries, identify relationships, timelines and patterns, and — the part that matters — suggest lines of analysis, with citations back to source evidence for court defensibility. Suggesting lines of analysis about named individuals is machine-generated investigative direction, not just retrieval, so it should be logged in the AI register even while it is in early access. Early-access status is exactly how AI enters an estate without a procurement decision, so ask who accepted the early-access terms.","domains":["law_enforcement"]}],"typical_domains":["law_enforcement"],"escalation_note":"Annex III point 6 applies: Magnet One is used by police forensics units and prosecutors to analyse and evaluate digital evidence in criminal investigations. Everything documented is retrospective analysis of seized material, so the Art. 5(1)(d) individual predictive policing prohibition is not engaged by the product as shipped; it would be engaged if an agency took Intelligent Insights' 'suggested analysis' output and used it to build a standing risk score for named individuals rather than to progress a specific case. Art. 5(1)(h) is not engaged — there is no real-time biometric identification component here. The genuine near-term compliance issue is more mundane: Intelligent Insights is in early access, which means the version an agency is running may change between an assessment and an audit, and citation-verified LLM summaries still need a documented human check before anything reaches a case file or a court bundle.","docs_url":"https://www.magnetforensics.com/products/magnet-one/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"motorola-solutions-assist","name":"Motorola Solutions Assist","vendor":"Motorola Solutions","category":"Public Safety Command Centre / Dispatch & Records AI","walkthrough_category":"law_enforcement","prevalence":1,"aliases":["motorola","motorola solutions","assist","assist chat","commandcentral","commandcentral aware","vigilant","viqi","vesta"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Assist Chat","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"A browser-based CJIS-compliant chat assistant that lets any role in an agency — dispatch, investigations, front line, back office — upload content for summarisation, query permitted incident data and ask questions of agency procedure and case history. Motorola announced a complimentary starter edition for all US public safety agencies, which is the important detail: in that market it can be present without a purchase order, and the same commercial move may follow elsewhere. In a European estate treat it as available-but-not-enabled and confirm whether an agency admin has activated it. Ask what 'permitted incident data' resolves to for a given user role, because that is the whole access-control story.","domains":["law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"Assist in CommandCentral (call handling, CAD and records)","capabilities":["document_analysis","content_generation","summarization","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Assist is being pushed across the command-centre portfolio: transcription and translation of emergency calls, resolving non-emergency calls, contextual incident identification and pre-population in computer-aided dispatch, and in records management polishing an officer's narrative and proofreading it against the video, audio and CAD record. The records feature is the one to slow down on — a machine cross-checking an officer's written account against recorded evidence is a form of evidence evaluation, and it also functions as oversight of the officer who wrote it. Licensed as part of the CommandCentral software suite.","domains":["law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"Assist Responder Suite / ViQi voice queries","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Voice-activated in-vehicle and on-body queries that let an officer run database checks, pull location information, adjust radio settings, tag video and get on-scene translation without typing. The compliance-relevant part is the database check: a spoken query against a person or plate returns a record that shapes what happens to someone in the next thirty seconds. Licensed with the responder portfolio and dependent on Motorola radio/vehicle hardware.","domains":["law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"Vigilant VehicleManager AI vehicle search","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","document_analysis"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"ALPR analytics over fixed and mobile plate-reader detections, with AI search that narrows results by make, model, colour, accessories and distinguishing features such as damage or bumper stickers, plus hotlist management and integration into CommandCentral Records. Plates are not biometric data, but a searchable history of where a given vehicle has been is location profiling of the person who drives it, and hotlist hits trigger stops. Separately licensed and hardware-dependent. Ask for the detection retention period and the hotlist governance — those two settings, not the AI, determine whether this is proportionate.","domains":["law_enforcement"]}],"typical_domains":["law_enforcement"],"escalation_note":"Annex III point 6 applies to the investigative and records uses; Annex III point 5(d) (emergency call triage and dispatch prioritisation) is a separate high-risk heading that catches the 911/112 call-handling side, so a Motorola estate can land in two Annex III points at once and consultants routinely miss the second. Art. 5(1)(d) is not engaged by anything documented — none of these features scores a named individual's likelihood of future offending. Art. 5(1)(h) is not engaged by ALPR, because a licence plate is not biometric data; it would be engaged if an agency added live face matching to the camera estate feeding CommandCentral Aware, and Motorola's adjacent video-security portfolio is out of scope of this entry and must be assessed on its own. One further boundary worth stating plainly: the records feature that proofreads an officer's narrative against evidence is simultaneously an Annex III point 4 employment question if its output is used to evaluate the officer rather than the report.","docs_url":"https://www.motorolasolutions.com/en_us/ai/assist.html","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"nice-evidencentral","name":"NiCE Evidencentral","vendor":"NiCE","category":"Digital Evidence Management / Public Safety & Justice","walkthrough_category":"law_enforcement","prevalence":1,"aliases":["nice public safety","evidencentral","nice investigate","nice inform","nice justice"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"AI Assistant (Evidencentral)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","summarization","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Summarises reports, builds case timelines and answers evidence-grounded questions across the material gathered into a case in NiCE Investigate. NiCE describes it as grounded in the agency's own case data rather than open-ended generation. Because Evidencentral is also used to assemble disclosure packages for prosecutors, an AI-generated timeline can end up shaping what a prosecutor sees first. Enabled per deployment; confirm whether summaries are stored as case artefacts or regenerated on demand, because the former becomes disclosable material.","domains":["law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"Face Detection and Redaction","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Automatically detects faces and other sensitive visuals in body-worn video and CCTV and redacts them before disclosure or public release. This is face detection for privacy protection, not face recognition for identification — nobody is matched to an identity — and it is the clearest example in this catalogue of biometric-adjacent processing that reduces rather than increases risk to individuals. It still belongs in the AI register, because a missed face is a data breach and an over-redaction is a disclosure failure. Ask what the human QA step is before release.","domains":["law_enforcement","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Video and audio transcription with universal text search (OCR)","capabilities":["document_analysis","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Transcribes body-worn video, CCTV and interviews and applies OCR to documents and handwritten notes so the whole case file becomes text-searchable. Straightforward in itself, but it is what makes the AI Assistant and the redaction workflow work, so it is usually the first thing switched on. Transcription of a recorded police interview is evidentially sensitive — confirm whether the transcript or the audio is the record of truth.","domains":["law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"Automated call QA scoring and coaching","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","summarization"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"On the emergency-communications side, NiCE transcribes 999/911/112 calls, generates AI summaries and automatically scores calls against APCO/NENA standards, feeding coaching for call handlers. This is the feature that quietly changes the domain: it evaluates the performance of named employees. In a European deployment that engages works-council and worker-consultation obligations before it engages the AI Act, and Norwegian and Swedish public-sector employers in particular will need the union conversation first. Ask whether scores feed appraisal or only coaching, and get that answer in writing.","domains":["employment"]}],"typical_domains":["law_enforcement"],"escalation_note":"Annex III point 6 applies to the investigative side — Evidencentral is used by police to assemble and evaluate evidence for investigation and prosecution. Annex III point 5(d) applies to the emergency call-handling side, and Annex III point 4 applies to the automated call QA scoring, because that evaluates workers' performance; a single NiCE estate can therefore sit in three Annex III headings, which is the reason this vendor is worth a slow assessment rather than a checkbox. Art. 5(1)(d) is not engaged: nothing documented predicts individual offending. Art. 5(1)(h) is not engaged either — face detection for redaction identifies nobody, and treating it as prohibited real-time biometric identification is a common and expensive misreading. The real escalation trigger is if an agency swaps the detection model for a matching model, or if it pipes redacted-but-retained face crops into any identification workflow.","docs_url":"https://www.nicepublicsafety.com/nice-ai","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"palantir-gotham","name":"Palantir Gotham","vendor":"Palantir Technologies","category":"Intelligence & Investigative Analytics Platform","walkthrough_category":"law_enforcement","prevalence":1,"aliases":["palantir","gotham","palantir gotham","gotham europa","hessendata","vera","palantir aip","dar"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Data integration, entity resolution and link analysis (Gotham ontology)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","document_analysis","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"active","notes":"Gotham's core function is to ingest police records, case files, custody and traffic data, telecoms interception product and other sources into a shared object model, resolve them into single 'person', 'vehicle' and 'event' entities, and surface connections between them. This is not an optional module — a force that has bought Gotham has bought this, so it is one of the rare genuine 'active' cases. Its practical effect is that people with no crime attributed to them (witnesses, contacts, relatives) become resolved entities in a police graph. The consultant question for the system owner is not 'is AI on' but 'which source systems are ingested, on what legal basis, and who can see a person's dossier'.","domains":["law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"Palantir AIP (LLM agents over the ontology)","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","summarization","natural_language_query","document_analysis"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"AIP is Palantir's LLM layer that lets an analyst ask questions of the integrated data in natural language and have agents draft summaries, briefings and next-step suggestions grounded in the ontology. It is a distinct platform capability that has to be licensed and configured against a specific deployment — an existing Gotham installation from 2019 will not have it unless the agency added it. Because AIP output is written into intelligence products that officers act on, it is the layer where a human-oversight and logging regime has to be proven. Ask whether AIP is enabled, which model backend is used, whether it runs in the agency's own environment, and whether prompts and outputs are retained for audit.","domains":["law_enforcement"]}],"typical_domains":["law_enforcement"],"escalation_note":"Annex III point 6 is engaged almost by definition: Gotham is deployed by law enforcement to evaluate evidence reliability, support investigations and profile persons. The Article 5 line matters more than the Annex III line here. If a force configures Gotham to score or rank named individuals by their likelihood of committing a future offence on the basis of profiling, personality traits or characteristics, that is prohibited under Art. 5(1)(d) — no risk-management process makes it lawful. Place-based or hotspot forecasting (which areas or times to patrol) is outside that prohibition and remains high-risk, not prohibited; so does supporting analysis of an offence that has already occurred. Nothing here asserts that Palantir sells or that any force operates a prohibited configuration — the point is that the platform is generic enough to be configured into one, so the configuration, not the product, is what must be assessed. Separately, if Gotham is fed live face-matching output for real-time identification in public space, Art. 5(1)(h) applies to that identification component. European context to raise with the client: German state deployments (Hesse HessenDATA, NRW, Bavaria, Baden-Württemberg) have been the subject of a February 2023 Federal Constitutional Court ruling on the legal basis for automated police data analysis, so the national-law question sits alongside the AI Act one.","docs_url":"https://www.palantir.com/platforms/gotham/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"veritone-idems","name":"Veritone iDEMS","vendor":"Veritone","category":"Digital Evidence Management / Public Safety AI Applications","walkthrough_category":"law_enforcement","prevalence":1,"aliases":["veritone","idems","veritone redact","veritone track","veritone identify","aiware","veritone illuminate"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Veritone Redact","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","document_analysis","basic_automation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Automated redaction of video, still images and audio using head detection plus time-correlated transcription to locate PII, extended in 2026 with document redaction, AI voice masking, inverse blur and transcription in 64 languages. Head detection is detection, not identification, and the purpose is privacy protection ahead of disclosure or FOI release. Sold as an application in its own right and used by 300+ government agencies. The consultant question is throughput governance: agencies buy this to clear backlogs, and the temptation is to release without the human QA pass.","domains":["law_enforcement","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Veritone Track","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Uses similarity detection to follow the same person or vehicle across multiple video sources so investigators can reconstruct a movement timeline. Veritone describes it as non-facial similarity matching on appearance attributes, but following a specific individual across a camera estate is person re-identification in substance, whatever the modality. Separately licensed. This is the feature most likely to be described to a client as 'just video search' and it is not.","domains":["law_enforcement","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Veritone Identify","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Compares video and photographic evidence against known-offender and person-of-interest databases to generate suspect leads — this is facial identification against a watchlist, stated as such on Veritone's own public-safety page. It is a separately licensed application and is the single highest-risk item in this catalogue's law-enforcement half. If a European agency is even scoping it, the assessment stops being an Annex III exercise and becomes an Article 5 and national-law exercise first.","domains":["law_enforcement","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"iDEMS evidence transcription, search and translation (aiWARE)","capabilities":["document_analysis","natural_language_query","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The evidence-management layer itself consolidates body-worn, CCTV, still image, audio and PDF material into one CJIS-compliant cloud location and runs Veritone's aiWARE engines over it for transcription, search and review; the Illuminate variant exposes the same capability inside eDiscovery platforms such as Relativity and OpenText. Cloud-only delivery is the first blocker for most European police clients. Ask where the tenancy sits and whether an EU-region deployment is actually offered, not just promised.","domains":["law_enforcement"]}],"typical_domains":["law_enforcement","biometrics"],"escalation_note":"Annex III point 6 applies to the evidence-analysis features and Annex III point 1 (biometrics) applies independently to Track and Identify. Article 5 must be handled directly here. Veritone Identify performs facial identification against offender and person-of-interest databases: used retrospectively on a seized image it is post-remote biometric identification, which is high-risk and, for law enforcement, subject to judicial or independent authorisation under Art. 26(10); pointed at live public-space camera feeds it becomes real-time remote biometric identification for law enforcement and is prohibited under Art. 5(1)(h) except in the narrow enumerated cases (targeted search for specific victims, prevention of a specific and imminent terrorist threat, or localisation of a suspect for listed serious offences), each requiring prior authorisation and a national implementing law. Veritone Track sits in the same conversation despite not being sold as face recognition, because re-identifying an individual across cameras is what the prohibition is about. Nothing here asserts that Veritone or any customer operates a prohibited configuration — the product is capable of both a lawful and an unlawful deployment and only the deployment can be assessed. Art. 5(1)(d) is not engaged: no feature scores individuals for future offending. Note also the Contact application (California AB 953 stop-data collection), which is compliance tooling rather than an AI risk, and should not be swept into the register.","docs_url":"https://www.veritone.com/industries/public-safety/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"idemia-borderguard","name":"BORDERGUARD","vendor":"IDEMIA Public Security","category":"Border Control / Automated Border Control and Traveller Risk Assessment","walkthrough_category":"migration","prevalence":1,"aliases":["borderguard","idemia public security","abc gate idemia","mface","onelook"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"BORDERGUARD Lane — automated border control gate","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","automated_decision_making"],"notes":"An ABC eGate that authenticates the travel document optically and electronically, captures the traveller's face (MFACE), iris (OneLook Gen2) or fingerprints (MorphoWave), matches live biometrics against the chip and against central border databases, and opens or refuses the gate. IDEMIA reports 1,400+ lanes and over 250 million crossings. There is no non-AI mode: the biometric matcher and the presentation-attack and morphing-attack detection are the product, so any authority that has bought the gate is running AI on every traveller by default. Comes in Essential, Premium and Advanced variants and connects to the national border system for EES entry/exit registration and eligibility checks.","default_state":"active","domains":["migration","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"BORDERGUARD Targeting","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","automated_decision_making"],"notes":"Pre-arrival risk assessment: analytics that cross-reference API/PNR and other data sources to score and flag travellers for secondary inspection before they reach the border. This is the module that profiles people rather than verifying them, and it is the one to ask about first — it produces a risk judgement about a named individual that changes how a border officer treats them. Sold as a separate module of the BORDERGUARD suite and procured by national authorities; a Lane deployment does not imply Targeting is present.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":["migration","biometrics","law_enforcement"]},{"feature_name":"BORDERGUARD Bio Matcher and Intelligence","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"notes":"The national-scale biometric matching engine and central management layer behind the gates and kiosks — 1:N identification of travellers against enrolled and watchlist galleries, plus centralised border operations management. 1:N identification against a gallery is a materially different legal object from 1:1 verification at a gate and must be assessed separately. Procured as national infrastructure.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":["migration","biometrics","law_enforcement"]}],"typical_domains":["migration","biometrics"],"escalation_note":"High risk on its face. BORDERGUARD Lane is Annex III point 7(c)/(d) — an AI system used by or on behalf of a competent public authority to examine applications and verify authenticity of travel documents, and to assist in examining entry conditions — and simultaneously Annex III point 1(a) as a biometric identification system, so it fails the Art. 6(3) derogation on both counts. BORDERGUARD Targeting is Annex III point 7(b), risk assessment of persons intending to enter the EU, which is the most consequential item here because the output is a profile of a named individual. Because the deployer is a public authority, Art. 27 requires a fundamental rights impact assessment before first use, in addition to Art. 26 deployer duties, Art. 14 human oversight and Art. 13 instructions-for-use compliance; Art. 49(3) requires registration in the EU database. Two boundaries worth stating plainly: 1:1 verification of a face against the document the traveller has just presented is not 'real-time remote biometric identification' under Art. 5(1)(h), because the person is not being identified remotely from a distance without their engagement — the Bio Matcher's 1:N gallery search is a different matter and needs its own analysis. And if any variant infers stress, deception or emotional state from a traveller, that is a hard stop: emotion inference at a border falls outside the workplace/education prohibition in Art. 5(1)(f) but sits in Annex III point 7(a)'s 'polygraphs and similar tools' as high risk, and would need explicit scrutiny. Not a product any ordinary organisation runs — a national border authority procures it, which is why prevalence is 1.","docs_url":"https://www.idemia.com/border-control-solutions-borderguard","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"vision-box-seamless-journey","name":"Seamless Gate / Seamless Journey Platform (formerly Vision-Box vb orchestra)","vendor":"Amadeus (Vision-Box, an Amadeus company)","category":"Border Control / Biometric Traveller Identity Management","walkthrough_category":"migration","prevalence":1,"aliases":["vision-box","visionbox","vb orchestra","orchestra","seamless gate","seamless journey platform","amadeus border control"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Seamless Gate — contactless biometric gate","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","automated_decision_making"],"notes":"A biometric eGate used at border control, security, boarding and lounge access: the traveller walks through and is identified by face capture, with fingerprint and a document reader as fallback for travellers the face matcher cannot process. At a border deployment it authenticates the travel document, matches the live face and gates entry or exit. Naming has moved twice — Vision-Box branded it Seamless Gateway under the vb orchestra platform; after the Amadeus acquisition the product pages are Amadeus Seamless Gate and Seamless Journey Platform. Expect customer inventories to still say 'Vision-Box' or 'Orchestra'.","default_state":"active","domains":["migration","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Seamless Kiosk pre-registration with watchlist checks","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","automated_decision_making"],"notes":"Self-service enrolment that scans the passport, captures face and fingerprints and performs watchlist checks before the traveller reaches a gate or an officer desk, routing them to Seamless Gate for automated clearance or Seamless Desk for manual immigration evaluation. The routing decision is the substantive one: an algorithmic output decides whether a person meets an officer. Deployed in national border programmes (Sint Maarten, Finnish Border Guard EES work, multiple airports).","default_state":"active","domains":["migration","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Passenger Data Gateway (API / iAPI / PNR)","capabilities":["automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling"],"notes":"Collects Advance Passenger Information, interactive API and Passenger Name Record data directly from carrier reservation and departure control systems across air, sea, rail and road, normalises it into a unified traveller data model and returns real-time board/no-board decisions to the airline, feeding a Passenger Screening application. Amadeus's published material stops at data collection and screening integration and does not describe the scoring methodology, so establish with the authority whether the screening layer applies rules, watchlist matching, or a learned risk model — the three have very different classifications. Procured by governments, not airlines.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":["migration","law_enforcement"]}],"typical_domains":["migration","biometrics"],"escalation_note":"High risk where deployed at a border. Seamless Gate and Seamless Kiosk are Annex III point 7(c) (examining entry conditions and verifying travel documents) plus Annex III point 1(a) (biometric identification); the kiosk's route-to-officer decision and the Passenger Data Gateway's board/no-board response also engage Annex III point 7(b), risk assessment of persons intending to enter. Art. 27 FRIA applies wherever a public authority is the deployer, with Art. 26 duties and Art. 49(3) registration. The important boundary for this vendor specifically: the same Seamless Gate hardware and platform is also sold for purely commercial airport use — boarding, bag drop, lounge access, security queue — with an airline or airport operator as deployer and no border function. That deployment is not Annex III point 7 at all; it is a biometric system under Annex III point 1(a) if it performs identification rather than 1:1 verification, and otherwise falls to Art. 50 transparency plus GDPR Art. 9 special-category processing and an explicit-consent basis. Ask which touchpoints are connected to the national border system before classifying. Renaming risk is high here: Vision-Box product names have been migrated to Amadeus branding and vision-box.com was not resolving at time of research.","docs_url":"https://amadeus.com/en/border-control/products/seamless-gate","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"secunet-easygate","name":"secunet easygate","vendor":"secunet Security Networks AG","category":"Border Control / Automated Border Control eGates and EES Infrastructure","walkthrough_category":"migration","prevalence":1,"aliases":["easygate","secunet","secunet easygate","easykiosk","easytower","bocoa","biomiddle"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"easygate biometric verification with PAD and MAD","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","automated_decision_making"],"notes":"The eGate reads the eMRTD chip, checks document authenticity optically and electronically, captures a live facial image and matches it against the chip image, then opens or holds the gate. Two AI functions matter beyond the matcher: presentation attack detection (spoof/mask/photo detection) and integrated morphing attack detection, which looks for a passport photo that has been digitally blended from two faces — a fraud vector that Frontex has run dedicated research programmes on. None of this is optional; the gate cannot operate without it. secunet easygates are in service in Bulgaria, Germany, Estonia, Iceland, Latvia, Lithuania, Austria, Poland, Switzerland and Hungary, which makes this the closest thing to a default Schengen border stack.","default_state":"active","domains":["migration","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"easykiosk / easytower EES enrolment","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"notes":"Self-service kiosks and stationary facial-capture towers that enrol third-country nationals into the EU Entry/Exit System — four fingerprints plus a facial image, plus document checks — before or alongside the officer or gate step. Zurich Airport's November 2025 rollout used the full stack: easygate, easykiosk, easytower, the bocoa border-control application and the easyserver backend linking to the central EES. Capture quality assessment is itself an algorithmic judgement that determines whether a traveller is re-enrolled repeatedly.","default_state":"active","domains":["migration","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"bocoa border control application and biomiddle","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","document_analysis"],"notes":"bocoa is the EES-compliant application the border officer works in; biomiddle is standards-oriented middleware brokering biometric and eID operations between capture devices, matchers and national/central systems. For a compliance assessment these are where the audit trail and the human-oversight design actually live — the gate is just the front end. Ask to see what bocoa shows the officer when a match is inconclusive, because that screen is the Art. 14 human oversight control.","default_state":"active","domains":["migration","biometrics"]}],"typical_domains":["migration","biometrics"],"escalation_note":"High risk. Annex III point 7(c) covers AI used by competent public authorities to examine applications for entry and to verify the authenticity of travel documents and supporting documentation, and Annex III point 1(a) covers the biometric identification component; the Art. 6(3) derogation is unavailable because these systems perform profiling of natural persons and make findings that condition a person's entry. Note that the EU's own EES and ETIAS central systems are not licensable products — they are Union information systems operated by eu-LISA and listed in Annex X, with their own conformity path under Art. 111(2). What a national authority procures and must assess is exactly this: the vendor stack that captures, matches and decides at the border, plus the middleware and the officer application. Art. 27 FRIA applies because the deployer is a public authority, alongside Art. 26 deployer duties and Art. 49(3) registration. Boundary worth stating: 1:1 verification against the presented document is not Art. 5(1)(h) real-time remote biometric identification. Morphing and presentation attack detection are anti-fraud classifiers, not emotion inference, and are legitimate high-risk functions — but if a supplier ever adds behavioural or deception analytics to the same lane, that lands in Annex III point 7(a) 'polygraphs and similar tools' and needs separate, sceptical treatment. Prevalence 1: this is procured by border police forces, not by ordinary organisations, and secunet's commercial relevance to a Nordic consultancy is limited to public-sector border and interior-ministry engagements.","docs_url":"https://www.secunet.com/en/products-consulting/easygate","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"sita-smart-path","name":"SITA Smart Path","vendor":"SITA","category":"Airport Passenger Processing / Biometric Identity Management","walkthrough_category":"migration","prevalence":1,"aliases":["smart path","sita smart path","sita biometrics","one id sita","sita digital travel"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"End-to-end biometric passenger identity (face as boarding pass)","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","automated_decision_making"],"notes":"Enrols a traveller's face once — at a kiosk, bag drop or via mobile — then uses face matching to authorise them at bag drop, security, boarding and, where the airport has integrated it, border control. SITA uses NEC's matching engine and cites sub-three-second processing across roughly 45 airports and 4,600 touchpoints. The touchpoint decides whether the traveller proceeds; there is no non-biometric path once a passenger has opted in. Whether any given touchpoint is a border touchpoint depends entirely on the airport's contract with the national authority, and that is the fact that determines classification.","default_state":"active","domains":["biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Digital Travel Credential and government border integration","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","document_analysis"],"notes":"SITA participates in IATA One ID and ICAO's Digital Travel Credential work and states it provides digital border solutions to over 70 governments, including support for the US CBP Biometric Exit mandate. A DTC is a chip-derived digital identity verified against a live face capture, which moves document examination off the officer desk and into the platform. Where Smart Path is bound to a government border system, the airport is no longer just running a convenience product. Procured as part of a government or airport authority programme.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":["migration","biometrics"]}],"typical_domains":["biometrics"],"escalation_note":"Deliberately tagged biometrics only, not migration, which is the opposite call from the other four entries in this category and is the point. Smart Path's core market is airport and airline passenger flow — check-in, bag drop, security queue, boarding — where the deployer is an airport operator or carrier, there is no examination of entry conditions, and the classification is Annex III point 1(a) if the system identifies rather than verifies, plus Art. 50 transparency and GDPR Art. 9 explicit consent with a genuine non-biometric alternative offered. It becomes Annex III point 7 only when a specific touchpoint is integrated with a national border system and is used by or on behalf of a competent public authority to verify travel documents or check entry conditions — at which point Art. 27 FRIA, Art. 26 deployer duties and Art. 49(3) registration attach to that touchpoint and its authority deployer, not to the whole airport installation. The practical consultant question is therefore narrow: get the list of touchpoints and, for each, name the deployer and say whether a border authority consumes the match result. Also check the enrolment gallery — a persistent multi-airport gallery matched 1:N is a materially different system from a per-flight gallery discarded after departure, and vendors describe both as 'Smart Path'.","docs_url":"https://www.sita.aero/solutions/sita-at-airports/sita-passenger-processing/sita-smart-path/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"thales-gemalto-border","name":"Thales Gemalto Border and Visa Management (EES Border Management System)","vendor":"Thales","category":"Border Control / Border and Visa Management Systems","walkthrough_category":"migration","prevalence":1,"aliases":["thales border","gemalto border","thales abc gate","thales border kiosk","gemalto visa management","thales ees"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Gemalto ABC eGates","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","automated_decision_making"],"notes":"Automated border control gates combining electronic travel-document verification with biometric authentication of the traveller, modular so the hardware can be laid out to suit airport passenger flow. As with every ABC gate, the biometric matcher is the product and cannot be turned off. Thales acquired this line with Gemalto in 2019 and the estate is documented across European and Canadian deployments.","default_state":"active","domains":["migration","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Thales Border Kiosk (EES self-service enrolment)","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","document_analysis"],"notes":"EES-compliant self-service kiosk for third-country-national registration: document verification plus facial and fingerprint capture with enrolment-quality assessment, designed to move the enrolment burden off the officer desk. France selected Thales biometric kiosks for EU border-check compliance. Quality-gating of a capture is itself an algorithmic decision that determines whether a traveller is sent back through the process.","default_state":"active","domains":["migration","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Multimodal biometric pod (face and iris)","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation"],"notes":"A capture-and-authentication unit combining iris and facial recognition for both enrolment and verification at borders, introduced to raise accuracy and speed over face-only capture. Multimodal fusion is a scoring decision — how the two modalities are weighted when they disagree — and the vendor does not publish that logic, so it belongs on the list of questions for a conformity file. Procured as part of a border programme, not standalone.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":["migration","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Gemalto visa and border management applications","capabilities":["document_analysis","automated_decision_making","recommendation_profiling"],"notes":"Back-office applications for immigration processes at border management headquarters and border crossing points, covering visa issuance workflows and border operations. This is the part of the Thales portfolio that examines applications rather than gating travellers, and it is the least publicly documented — Thales's own pages are behind bot protection and describe the offering at solution level rather than feature level. Treat the AI content of the visa-examination workflow as unverified and get it in writing from Thales during a procurement or assessment.","default_state":"not_licensed","domains":["migration"]}],"typical_domains":["migration","biometrics"],"escalation_note":"High risk. The gates and kiosks are Annex III point 7(c) and Annex III point 1(a); the visa management applications are Annex III point 7(c) in its purest form — AI used by a competent public authority to examine applications for visa and residence permits, including related assessment of the reliability of evidence. Art. 6(3) derogation is unavailable. Art. 27 FRIA is required of the public-authority deployer, plus Art. 26 duties, Art. 14 human oversight and Art. 49(3) registration; where the system contributes to a visa refusal, national administrative-law reason-giving duties and Art. 86 the right to explanation of individual decision-making both bite, and that combination is what will actually be litigated. Two boundaries: an eGate performing 1:1 verification against the presented document is not Art. 5(1)(h) real-time remote biometric identification; and any component that infers deception, stress or emotional state from a visa applicant or traveller is Annex III point 7(a) 'polygraphs and similar tools', which after the iBorderCtrl controversy is the single most reputationally dangerous thing in this category — ask the question explicitly rather than assuming a European vendor would not ship it. Prevalence 1: national border and interior authorities only.","docs_url":"https://www.thalesgroup.com/en/markets/digital-identity-and-security/government/eborder","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"innovatrics-border-control","name":"Innovatrics Border Control Platform","vendor":"Innovatrics","category":"Border Control Biometrics / ABIS","walkthrough_category":"migration","prevalence":1,"aliases":["innovatrics","innovatrics abis","innovatrics border control","border control core abis"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Border Control Core ABIS","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Innovatrics' automated biometric identification system for border authorities, performing both 1:1 verification and 1:N identification across face, fingerprint, contactless fingerprint, iris and palm, for deduplication and watchlist matching. The 1:N watchlist path is the one that matters: it searches a traveller's biometrics against a database of persons of interest and returns a match that a border officer acts on. Sold to system integrators building national border systems, never bought off the shelf by an ordinary organisation. Ask which modalities are actually deployed and whether a match blocks entry automatically or is referred to an officer.","domains":["migration","biometrics"]},{"feature_name":"Enrollment toolkits (Mobile Pre-Enrollment, Kiosk/PC, Mobile Field & Land Border)","capabilities":["biometric_categorisation","document_analysis"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Capture-side components: pre-arrival identity verification before the traveller reaches the border, biometric capture at airport checkpoints, officer-facing verification at the border post, and a mobile toolkit extending the same capture to remote land crossings. They also perform identity document verification. These feed the ABIS rather than deciding anything themselves, but they are where the biometric template is created, so the retention and lawful-basis questions land here.","domains":["migration","biometrics"]}],"typical_domains":["migration","biometrics"],"escalation_note":"Annex III point 7 (examination of applications and risk assessment of persons entering the EU) and Annex III point 1 (biometrics) apply together. Keep the two biometric modes apart, because they classify differently: a 1:1 check of a traveller's face against the chip on their own passport is biometric verification, which Annex III point 1(a) expressly carves out of remote biometric identification; a 1:N search against a watchlist or a deduplication database is identification and is squarely high risk. Article 5(1)(h) bans real-time remote biometric identification in publicly accessible spaces for law enforcement purposes — border control is a distinct purpose governed by Annex III point 7 rather than caught by that prohibition, but a deployment repurposed to general surveillance of a public concourse would cross into it, so pin down the stated purpose in the contract. Two structural points a consultant should raise: Innovatrics sells to system integrators, so the integrator that assembles and puts its name on the national system is likely the provider under Article 25 while Innovatrics is an upstream component supplier; and the deploying border authority is a public body, which triggers the Article 27 fundamental rights impact assessment before the system is put into service.","docs_url":"https://www.innovatrics.com/border-control/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"thomson-reuters-cocounsel-legal","name":"CoCounsel Legal","vendor":"Thomson Reuters","category":"Legal Research & Drafting AI","walkthrough_category":"justice_democracy","prevalence":3,"aliases":["thomson reuters","cocounsel","co-counsel","westlaw","westlaw advantage","practical law","casetext"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Deep Research","capabilities":["natural_language_query","document_analysis","summarization","content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"An agentic research capability that formulates and executes a multi-step research plan and returns a report grounded in Westlaw proprietary content, rather than answering a single query. Thomson Reuters launched it in the US and extended it to the UK in January 2026 alongside Westlaw Advantage and a Practical Law version. It is sold within CoCounsel Legal subscriptions, so it is never present by accident. The realistic failure mode is not hallucinated citations — it is a research plan that silently omits a line of authority, which no citation check catches.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"CoCounsel drafting and document review","capabilities":["content_generation","document_analysis","summarization","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Plain-language instruction to draft, summarise depositions, review and compare documents, with Westlaw authority, Practical Law guidance and the user's own uploaded documents pulled in automatically. Subscription-based and tiered by segment. Thomson Reuters lists government users including prosecutors, public defenders, courts and city attorneys as a customer segment, which is precisely why this product needs a deployment question rather than a product answer.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"This is the trap in the justice_democracy walkthrough and it must be stated explicitly: Annex III point 8(a) covers AI intended to be used by a judicial authority, or on its behalf, to research and interpret facts and the law and to apply the law to a concrete set of facts. A law firm or an in-house legal team using CoCounsel Legal is NOT in Annex III point 8, no matter how legally consequential the work is — the point attaches to the judicial authority, not to the legal profession. The same product licensed by a court, a tribunal, or a body acting on a court's behalf to help prepare or reason toward a decision IS Annex III point 8 and is high-risk. Thomson Reuters names courts among its government customers and Westlaw is used across the US federal and state judiciary, so the possibility is concrete rather than theoretical, and Nordic courts procuring legal-research AI would fall the same way. typical_domains is deliberately empty because the overwhelmingly normal deployment is a law firm or corporate legal function. Two boundaries not to overstate: purely ancillary court administrative use (scheduling, document management, anonymisation for publication) is expressly outside point 8, and alternative dispute resolution bodies whose decisions produce legal effects are inside it. Art. 50 transparency obligations for generated content apply regardless of tier.","docs_url":"https://legal.thomsonreuters.com/en/products/cocounsel-legal","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"karnov-kaila","name":"KAILA","vendor":"Karnov Group","category":"Nordic Legal Publishing & Research AI","walkthrough_category":"justice_democracy","prevalence":3,"aliases":["karnov","karnov group","kaila","kaila flow","juno","juno flow","norstedts juridik","allegra flow","lamylia connect"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"KAILA legal research assistant","capabilities":["natural_language_query","document_analysis","summarization","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Generative legal research grounded in Karnov's own jurisdiction-specific proprietary content and expert commentary, launched September 2024 and used daily in Denmark and Sweden. Karnov states its users include law firms, public authorities and corporate legal departments across a base of more than 400,000 users. Because Karnov (with JUNO in Sweden and Karnov in Denmark) is the incumbent legal-content platform rather than a challenger, this is the Nordic legal AI most likely to be already present in a client estate — and most likely to have been switched on as part of a content renewal rather than as an AI purchase. Ask when the subscription last renewed and what AI terms came with it.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Document analysis and advisory (next-generation KAILA)","capabilities":["document_analysis","summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The next generation extends KAILA from research into analysing the customer's own documents — reviews, risk identification and suggested improvements — with recommendations anchored in Karnov's expert-authored content. This is where it stops being a database with a chat box and starts producing advice on the client's own material. Confirm whether uploaded customer documents are processed in an EU region and whether they are excluded from any model improvement.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"AI-powered legal workflow solutions (KAILA Flow, JUNO Flow, Allegra Flow, LamyLia Connect)","capabilities":["content_generation","document_analysis","summarization","basic_automation"],"default_state":"available_not_enabled","notes":"Karnov's 2026 workflow layer covering assignment analysis, planning, legal analysis and document production in a single workspace, shipped under local brands: KAILA Flow (Denmark), JUNO Flow (Sweden), Allegra Flow (Spain) and LamyLia Connect (France). The per-country branding is a practical trap for a catalogue — a Swedish client will say 'JUNO Flow' and a Danish one 'KAILA Flow' for what is the same platform. Karnov has also announced an AI-enabled vertical solution for municipalities, which puts the same engine inside public authorities.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"This is the sharpest Nordic instance of the Annex III point 8 trap. Karnov explicitly names public authorities among its user base, and Karnov content is the standard legal source in Danish and Swedish public administration — so the question 'is this Annex III point 8' has a real chance of being answered yes in a Nordic engagement, unlike with Harvey or Legora. The distinction still holds strictly: a municipality, a ministry or a regulator using KAILA to research law is a public authority, not a judicial authority, and Annex III point 8(a) does not reach it; a court or tribunal using KAILA to research and interpret law and apply it to a concrete set of facts is inside point 8 and is high-risk. Note that an administrative body exercising adjudicative functions may be captured too, so 'is it a court' is the wrong question — 'does this body decide disputes with legal effects, and is the AI helping it reason' is the right one. typical_domains is left empty because the ordinary KAILA customer is a law firm, a corporate legal team or a non-judicial public authority. Nothing in Karnov's portfolio touches point 8(b) on elections.","docs_url":"https://www.karnovgroup.com/en/karnov-group-launches-next-generation-of-ai-based-services-and-expands-into-legal-workflow-solutions/","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"lexisnexis-lexis-plus-protege","name":"Lexis+ with Protégé","vendor":"LexisNexis","category":"Legal Research & Drafting AI","walkthrough_category":"justice_democracy","prevalence":3,"aliases":["lexisnexis","lexis","lexis+","lexis plus","lexis+ ai","protege","protégé","lexis protege","shepard's"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Protégé legal AI assistant","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","document_analysis","summarization","content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Conversational legal research, drafting, document upload, summarisation and analysis grounded in LexisNexis content and Shepard's citation signals, with an option to route reasoning tasks to general models from OpenAI, Google and Anthropic. The product reached general availability on 24 February 2026 and replaced the Lexis+ AI brand — if a client's AI register still says 'Lexis+ AI', the register is stale, not wrong. Licensed and priced per organisation. The dual legal-model / general-model design is worth asking about specifically, because the two paths have different grounding guarantees.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Agentic workflows and skills","capabilities":["content_generation","document_analysis","automated_decision_making","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Pre-built and custom multi-step workflows for transactional documents, litigation preparation and risk review, where the user approves a plan and agents then execute it with visible progress; May 2026 added agents that automate creation of contracts, briefs and motions. This is autonomous multi-step work product rather than assisted drafting, which is why automated_decision_making is tagged — not because it decides about a person, but because the plan-then-execute pattern is where human oversight thins out in practice. Ask what the approval gate actually gates.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Protégé Vault and Workrooms","capabilities":["document_analysis","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"A secure repository (up to 50 Vaults, 1-500 documents each) supporting drafting, timeline generation and citation analysis over the customer's own material, extended in 2026 to audio, video and spreadsheets, with permission-secured Workrooms for collaboration, role-based permissions, audit trails and customer-held encryption keys. Conversation history persists for 90 days, which is a retention answer a DPO will want before a lawyer uploads a live case file. Customer-held keys are the control to insist on for public-sector deployments.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Same distinction as CoCounsel and it is the one consultants get wrong: Annex III point 8(a) attaches to AI intended to assist a judicial authority in researching and interpreting facts and the law or in applying the law to facts. Lexis+ with Protégé in a law firm, an in-house team, or a government legal department acting as a party is not point 8 — a litigant's tool is not a judicial tool, however sophisticated. Deployed inside a court, tribunal or judicial-support body to help reason toward a decision, the identical product is high-risk under point 8 and triggers Art. 26 deployer duties including human oversight by someone competent to override it. typical_domains is empty because judicial deployment is not the normal case for this product. Watch the branding drift: this was Lexis+ AI until February 2026, so an assessment written in 2025 will not match what the client is running. Nothing in this product touches the point 8(b) limb on influencing elections or referendum outcomes.","docs_url":"https://www.lexisnexis.com/en-us/products/lexis-plus-protege.page","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"harvey-ai","name":"Harvey","vendor":"Harvey","category":"Legal AI Platform (Agents, Vault, Knowledge)","walkthrough_category":"justice_democracy","prevalence":2,"aliases":["harvey","harvey ai","harvey assistant","harvey vault","harvey agents"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Harvey Agents / Assistant","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","content_generation","document_analysis","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"The core interface: plain-English instruction to draft, analyse documents and answer complex legal questions, with agents that execute legal work end-to-end, run in parallel and can be scheduled. Firms can build custom agents that embed their own precedent and house style. Sold as an enterprise subscription — Harvey is never in an estate without a deliberate, and expensive, decision. Named users are law firms and in-house teams (Willkie, Gowling WLG, Hengeler Mueller, Clayton Utz, Mallesons); no court or government customers are listed.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Vault","capabilities":["document_analysis","summarization","natural_language_query"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Bulk cross-document analysis: store a large document set and run the same query across all of it, returning consolidated or tabular answers. Built for M&A diligence at volume and for regulatory document sets. The data-governance question is the obvious one — Vault is where a firm's client-confidential corpus accumulates, so residency, retention and privilege protection are the controls to check, not the model.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Knowledge","capabilities":["natural_language_query","document_analysis","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Research across legal, regulatory and tax sources with citations. Relevant to Nordic buyers specifically: Harvey's release notes document Lovdata as an available knowledge source, which brings Norwegian primary law into the platform and makes Harvey a realistic option for a Norwegian firm rather than a US-only one. Verify with the client which jurisdictional sources are actually licensed on their tenant, because source availability is contracted, not universal.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Contract Intelligence and Command Center","capabilities":["document_analysis","summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Contract review and insight surfacing, plus a Command Center giving firm-level analytics, benchmarking and agentic insights over platform usage. The Command Center is the item to look at twice: usage analytics across a professional workforce can shade into evaluating individual lawyers' productivity, which is an employment-law and works-council conversation in the Nordics before it is an AI Act one.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Harvey is a law-firm and in-house-counsel product; Harvey lists no judicial or court customers. Annex III point 8(a) is therefore not engaged in the normal deployment, and calling a Harvey rollout 'high-risk because it is legal AI' is the exact error this walkthrough exists to prevent — the trigger is the judicial authority, not the subject matter. If a court, tribunal or judicial-support body procured Harvey to help research and interpret law and apply it to facts, it would be high-risk under point 8 for that deployer. typical_domains is empty accordingly. The live obligations for a law-firm deployment are elsewhere: Art. 4 AI literacy for staff, Art. 50 transparency where generated content reaches a client or a court, professional-conduct duties on verifying citations, and privilege/confidentiality in Vault. Nothing in Harvey touches point 8(b) on election or referendum outcomes.","docs_url":"https://www.harvey.ai/products/assistant","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"legora","name":"Legora","vendor":"Legora","category":"Collaborative Legal AI Platform","walkthrough_category":"justice_democracy","prevalence":2,"aliases":["legora","leya","tabular review","legora assistant","legora agent"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Tabular Review","capabilities":["document_analysis","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"An AI grid over a document set — one row per document, one column per question, each cell linked back to its source, with collaboration controls such as 'Mark as Reviewed' and locked cells. It is the product Legora is known for and it is genuinely a review-workflow tool rather than a chatbot, which is why the citation-per-cell design matters more than model choice. Enterprise subscription; Legora is Stockholm-headquartered (rebranded from Leya in February 2025) and its Nordic law-firm penetration is the reason it appears in this catalogue ahead of larger US names.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Assistant","capabilities":["chatbot_assistant","natural_language_query","summarization","content_generation"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Conversational analysis that works over the same document set as Tabular Review, so questions can be asked against material already under review rather than against a general corpus. Delivered with Word and Outlook add-ins, an editor, mobile and a client-facing Portal — the add-ins matter for assessment scope because they put the AI inside the tools everyone already uses, which is where shadow adoption starts.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Legal Research","capabilities":["natural_language_query","document_analysis","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Parallel research across sources and jurisdictions, verified against primary law with results ranked by authority and links kept current. Legora has been building out European content partnerships — including a partnership with Djøf Forlag to bring Danish legal content onto the platform — rather than relying only on US coverage. Confirm on a per-tenant basis which Nordic content is actually licensed; content coverage is the differentiator here and it changes quarterly.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Workflows and Agents","capabilities":["content_generation","document_analysis","automated_decision_making"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"An agentic execution layer that runs multi-step tasks across connected sources and returns a single consolidated output, plus reusable Workflows for recurring matter types. Tagged automated_decision_making for the same reason as Lexis Protégé: the plan-and-execute pattern is where documented human oversight has to be designed in, not because it decides anything about a natural person. Ask to see one real workflow end to end rather than accepting the category description.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Legora's customers are law firms and in-house legal teams (including Nordic firms such as Mannheimer Swartling), so Annex III point 8(a) is not engaged in the normal deployment — a Swedish firm running Legora is not a judicial authority and its use is not high-risk on that ground. If a Nordic court, tribunal or a body acting on its behalf licensed Legora to research and interpret law and apply it to facts in pending matters, that deployment would be Annex III point 8 high-risk, and the deployer duties in Art. 26 would fall on the court, not on Legora as provider-of-a-general-tool — though Legora would likely become a provider of a high-risk system for that intended purpose. typical_domains is empty because judicial deployment is not the normal case. The most likely real finding in a Nordic engagement is not tiering at all but Art. 4 AI literacy plus the professional-secrecy question of what client material sits in the platform and in which region.","docs_url":"https://legora.com/product/tabular-review","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"vlex-vincent-ai","name":"Vincent AI","vendor":"vLex (Clio)","category":"Legal Research & Litigation Analytics AI","walkthrough_category":"justice_democracy","prevalence":2,"aliases":["vlex","vincent","vincent ai","clio","vincent studio","vlex justis"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"Research workflows (Ask a Research Question, 50-State Survey, Compare Jurisdictions)","capabilities":["natural_language_query","document_analysis","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Natural-language research over vLex's corpus with cited answers linked to primary sources, plus a cross-jurisdiction comparison workflow spanning 100+ countries. European coverage is documented for the EU, France, Ireland, Italy, Portugal, Spain and the UK — the Nordics are not listed, which is the single most important fact for a Nordic buyer and is usually discovered after the pilot rather than before. vLex is now part of Clio following the 2025-26 acquisition, so it may arrive bundled with a practice-management purchase.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Litigation workflows (Analyze a Complaint, Analyze Pleadings, Analyze Judicial Proceedings)","capabilities":["document_analysis","summarization","recommendation_profiling"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Structured analysis of filed documents and proceedings to extract issues, arguments and posture. This is analysis of case documents rather than of people, and it is the part of Vincent most directly analogous to what a judicial clerk does — which is exactly why deployment context decides the tier. Licensed within Vincent; enterprise customers can build custom no-code workflows in Vincent Studio.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Litigation Intelligence (Profile a Judge, Profile a Lawyer, Profile a Law Firm, Profile a Party)","capabilities":["recommendation_profiling","document_analysis","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Builds analytical profiles of named individuals — including sitting judges — from their decision and litigation history, to inform strategy. This is profiling of identified natural persons in their professional capacity, which is a GDPR question in every European jurisdiction and, in France, a national-law question with teeth: French law restricts the re-use of magistrates' identity data for the purpose of evaluating or predicting their professional practices, with criminal penalties attached. A Nordic firm running a cross-border matter can hit that without ever intending to. Confirm with the client whether judge analytics is enabled at all for European jurisdictions, and treat 'it's public data' as a non-answer.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Vincent is marketed to law firms, in-house teams, bar associations, law schools and law librarians — vLex does not list the judiciary as a user segment — so Annex III point 8(a) is not engaged in the normal deployment. It becomes engaged if a court or tribunal licenses Vincent to help research and interpret law and apply it to facts in a live matter, at which point the identical software is high-risk and the court is a deployer with Art. 26 duties. typical_domains stays empty for that reason. The distinctive escalation here is not the AI Act at all: Profile a Judge processes personal data about identified judicial officeholders for evaluative purposes, which is separately restricted under French law and is at minimum a GDPR legitimate-interest and Art. 22 analysis everywhere else in the EU/EEA. Flag it as a jurisdiction-scoped configuration item, not as a prohibited AI practice — it is not one of the Art. 5 prohibitions.","docs_url":"https://vlex.com/vincent-ai","researched":"2026-08-09"},{"id":"wolters-kluwer-expert-ai","name":"Wolters Kluwer Expert AI","vendor":"Wolters Kluwer","category":"Legal Research, Arbitration & Contract AI","walkthrough_category":"justice_democracy","prevalence":2,"aliases":["wolters kluwer","expert ai","vitallaw","vitallaw ai","kluwer arbitration","legisway","legisway advisor","elm solutions"],"ai_features":[{"feature_name":"VitalLaw Expert AI","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization","content_generation","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"AI-assisted search, summarisation, content creation and chat over Wolters Kluwer's editorially curated legal content, marketed as combining generative capability with editorial authority and governance. VitalLaw is primarily a US-content platform, so for a European client this is usually the least relevant of the three Expert AI surfaces — check which Wolters Kluwer content the client actually subscribes to before assessing it. Licensed as part of the VitalLaw subscription.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Kluwer Arbitration Expert AI","capabilities":["natural_language_query","summarization","document_analysis","chatbot_assistant"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"A conversational research assistant embedded in Kluwer Arbitration, operating as a closed-loop system over Wolters Kluwer's arbitration corpus. This is the one to look at hardest in a justice context: international arbitration tribunals decide disputes with legal effects, and an arbitrator or tribunal secretary using Expert AI to research and interpret the law is much closer to Annex III point 8 than a law firm associate doing the same thing. Licensed with the Kluwer Arbitration subscription.","domains":[]},{"feature_name":"Legisway Advisor","capabilities":["document_analysis","content_generation","summarization"],"default_state":"not_licensed","notes":"Contract review and redrafting powered by Expert AI inside Legisway, the corporate legal-department platform, alongside AI-enabled querying for tracking contractual obligations. Purely commercial contract work with no judicial dimension — include it in the AI inventory, keep it out of the Annex III point 8 discussion, and treat any dispute over an AI-redrafted clause as a professional-liability matter rather than an AI Act one.","domains":[]}],"typical_domains":[],"escalation_note":"Same rule, applied to a European publisher: Annex III point 8(a) attaches to AI assisting a judicial authority in researching and interpreting facts and the law or applying the law to facts. In-house legal teams and law firms using VitalLaw Expert AI or Legisway Advisor are not judicial authorities and are not in point 8. The genuinely arguable case in this portfolio is Kluwer Arbitration Expert AI: alternative dispute resolution bodies whose decisions produce legal effects for the parties are treated alongside courts for point 8 purposes, so an arbitral tribunal using it to reason toward an award should be assessed as a point 8 deployer, while a party's counsel using the identical database is not. typical_domains is empty because the ordinary customer is a firm or a corporate legal department. Nothing here touches point 8(b) on elections. Note for the catalogue owner: Wolters Kluwer consolidated its various legal AI brands under the 'Expert AI' umbrella, so entries referring to standalone product-level AI names from 2024-25 will drift.","docs_url":"https://www.wolterskluwer.com/en/solutions/vitallaw-law-firms/ai","researched":"2026-08-09"}]`),a={meta:e,products:t};export{a as c};
